{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_34","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c 'git check-ignore cores/bench/implementation_notes.md && cat cores/bench/implementation_notes.md'","aggregated_output":"cores/bench/implementation_notes.md\n# Six-clock radix-4 divider\n\nThe setup clock consumes the top two dividend bits and registers the full 34-bit value of `3*D` from the captured divisor magnitude. Each of the following five clocks computes three radix-4 digits. Each digit subtracts `D`, `2*D`, and `3*D` in parallel, then uses the three borrow bits to select the quotient digit and its 32-bit residual. A zero divisor selects digit 3 throughout and passes the dividend prefix through as the residual.\n\nThe digit schedule follows the hypothesis. Processing exactly 32 bits removes the four padding bits from the old quotient register. The divider uses the existing six-clock valid/consume protocol, signed-result correction, and ALTOPS control timing.\n\n## Verification\n\n- Local formal passed on the first run. All 53 single-channel ALTOPS checks passed with zero failures.\n- Verilator lint passed with `-Wall`. All 81 cocotb tests passed. The integer-oracle test now covers representable dividends one below, equal to, and one above each of the three divisor multiples, including divisors with multiples wider than 32 bits. It also covers all four leading bit pairs for divisors 0 through 3.\n- Cosimulation passed for selftest and three random programs, each with and without memory stalls. Stalled CoreMark passed its UART and CRC checks and completed ten iterations in **4,491,485 bracketed cycles**, matching the champion.\n\n## Gowin timing\n\nPlacement options 0, 1, and 2 reached 45.275, 46.194, and 46.194 MHz. The **46.194 MHz median** exceeds the 43.56 MHz threshold and corresponds to **102.85 CoreMark iterations/s** at the measured cycle count.\n\nThe parallel trials raised the Gowin logic count from 2,786 to 3,021 and the register count from 566 to 596. All three placements now report a worst setup path from the register file to an ID/EX operand register, through EX, fetch, and hazard logic. That path limits further clock gains from divider changes.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
