shell runs in its own PID namespace where $$ is 2 or 3\n# for every agent, so siblings wrote into one dir and wiped each other's\n# checks mid-SBY (V2 incident 03). mktemp gives a unique name instead.\n# FORMAL_WORK_ID pins the suffix so tools/eval/formal.py can remove exactly\n# its own dir afterwards. genchecks.py derives @core\n…[5748 of 8820 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:53f7dfc60d7c93d2]…\n, but each miss still cost a fetch opportunity and throughput trailed same-cycle EX target fetch (147.00 vs 153.52 CoreMark iterations/s).\n- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-001-r5s0 (regression, +9.73%): Registering effective addresses in ID/EX let loads issue in EX and forward from EX/MEM to the next dependent branch without a bubble, raising CoreMark throughput by 9.73% versus 15.0% for instruction replay on denied fetches.\n- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-002-r5s1 (regression, +8.45%): The 128-word replay window saved 444,281 cycles over ten CoreMark iterations by supplying fall-through instructions on denied fetches, but its `!redirect` check left taken loop backedges dependent on `io_imemReady`.\n- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-003-r5s2 (improvement, +15.00%): Replaying the successor instruction from a table indexed by predecessor PC kept CoreMark issuing across taken loop backedges with `io_imemReady` low, while parallel checks of the saved successor PC against `fallthrough_pc` and `redirect_target` let EX annul mismatches.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
