{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_99","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/implementation_notes.md'","aggregated_output":"# M-extension forwarding and divider retiming\n\nThe local Gowin placements measured 71.314, 73.409 and 73.409 MHz. At the 73.409 MHz median, ten CoreMark iterations in 3,234,996 bracketed cycles give **226.92 iterations/s**, up 12.13% from 202.37. The median exceeds the 68.478 MHz acceptance threshold.\n\nThe median placement's longest path still crosses the divider, from quotient bit 31 through the two trial stages to remainder bit 1, with an 18.917 ns reported arrival. Narrowing the intermediate remainder reduced the median to 72.779 MHz, so the implementation retains the full-width trials.\n\n## Implementation\n\n- All eight M operations use the existing MEM/WB dependency interlock. MEM selects the signed divider sideband, the product, or the integer and load result using the resident instruction. A held MEM slot retains its divide result, and the next independent divide can issue as the previous one enters WB.\n- The first wait clock consumes two dividend bits and registers 3D, 5D and 7D. Each of the next five wait clocks and the EX transfer edge consumes five bits using three 34-bit radix-4 trials followed by seven 35-bit radix-8 trials. Explicit borrows select the quotient digits and remainder. The transfer edge leaves the signed result available for MEM.\n- The forwarding unit captures four source-tag matches on ID advance and shares its rs1 comparisons with address prediction. The younger match wins when EX/MEM holds a trap-checked early writer. The older match stays usable while a denied data request holds EX, including after WB retirement.\n- Two storage choices improved the initial 67.730 MHz median. EX/MEM registers the combined write-enable and early-result qualification on the producer's transfer edge. Its existing operand registers capture divide operands on issue and hold them for RVFI. These changes brought the local Gowin register count from 851 to 788.\n\n## Verification\n\n- Verilator lint passed with the requested warning settings.\n- All 96 cocotb cases passed. The divider tests retain the arithmetic vectors, delayed consume, reset and consecutive operations. They check the signed remainder before and after the transfer and hold the consume input high to detect an extra iteration. Pipeline tests check exact stalls, eight-cycle independent divides, both forwarded source values after a trapping JAL, and store data across four denials and acceptance.\n- Selftest and the three random ELF retirement traces matched the reference with both buses ready and with both stall generators active. Stalled CoreMark produced the five canonical CRCs and printed `Correct operation validated.`\n- Local formal passed all 53 checks with zero failures or retries. The run used the single-channel wrapper and ALTOPS configuration selected by `core.yaml`. The arithmetic vectors and co-simulation exercise the real divider.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
