{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_104","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"git status --short --ignored -- cores/bench | head -n 20; cat cores/bench/implementation_notes.md; rg 'DONE \\\\((FAIL|ERROR)' \\\"\"'$TMPDIR/div-final-formal.log\"'","aggregated_output":" M cores/bench/rtl/alu.sv\n M cores/bench/test/test_alu.py\n!! cores/bench/generated/\n!! cores/bench/implementation_notes.md\n!! cores/bench/obj_dir/\n!! cores/bench/sim_build/\n!! cores/bench/test/test_alu_runner.result.xml\n!! cores/bench/test/test_decoder_runner.result.xml\n!! cores/bench/test/test_imm_gen_runner.result.xml\n!! cores/bench/test/test_mul_product_runner.result.xml\n!! cores/bench/test/test_pipeline_runner.result.xml\n!! cores/bench/test/test_reg_file_runner.result.xml\n# Shared radix-32 and radix-8 divider\n\nThe divider uses one 32-bit remainder register and one 32-bit dividend/quotient register. For divisor magnitudes from 1 through 0x3fff, setup resolves two dividend bits and six radix-32 steps resolve the remaining 30. For magnitudes of 0x4000 or greater, setup seeds the remainder with the high 14 dividend bits and six radix-8 steps consume the low 18.\n\nSeven 35-bit trials supply the radix-8 fit flags and every fourth radix-32 fit flag. For those shared candidates, appending the two dividend suffix bits to the low 12 difference bits yields the 14-bit residual. The other 24 candidates use 19-bit trials. Both banks read the same registers, and the mode selects the next quotient and remainder on the write side. The first wait clock performs setup, the next five clocks advance the recurrence, and consume takes the sixth digit. MEM applies the saved sign.\n\n**Zero-divisor handling** uses the radix-8 path. Setup seeds the quotient with 14 ones and the remainder with the high 14 dividend bits. All seven trials fit on each step, so the quotient becomes all ones and the remainder recovers the entire dividend magnitude. This eliminates a separate saved-dividend register.\n\n## Local checks\n\n- Verilator passed the requested full-core lint with zero warnings.\n- All 97 cocotb cases passed across six pytest runners, including the divider integer-reference and stalled pipeline tests. Added vectors cover every narrow digit threshold, the four low-bit suffixes at shared thresholds, the mode boundary, the largest wide quotient, signed operands and zero divisors. The tests also exercise alternating modes, reset during either mode, delayed consume and held MEM results.\n- Selftest and all three random ELF traces matched the ISS with and without bus stalls. Stalled CoreMark passed all five CRC checks and printed the validation message. The ten iterations ran from marker cycle 11,828 to 3,246,824, or 3,234,996 bracketed cycles.\n\n## FPGA result\n\nGowin placement options 0, 1 and 2 reached 77.777, 81.075 and 81.075 MHz. The **81.075 MHz median yields 250.62 CoreMark iterations/s**, a 10.44% gain over 226.92. The netlist uses 4,443 logic cells, 939 registers, six BSRAMs and five DSPs. The worst setup path in each placement starts at the instruction successor RAM and ends on the data-memory address path.\n","exit_code":1,"status":"failed"}}
