{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_90","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c 'git diff --numstat; cat cores/bench/implementation_notes.md'","aggregated_output":"22\t19\tcores/bench/rtl/core.sv\n11\t22\tcores/bench/rtl/ex_stage.sv\n14\t26\tcores/bench/rtl/forward_unit.sv\n6\t18\tcores/bench/rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n5\t2\tcores/bench/rtl/id_stage.sv\n0\t4\tcores/bench/rtl/mem_stage.sv\n2\t2\tcores/bench/rtl/wb_stage.sv\n107\t15\tcores/bench/test/test_pipeline.py\n# Split operand forwarding by producer age\n\nID captures each raw source from a matching forwardable EX/MEM result or the write-first register file. It saves that value even when the instruction in EX claims the same destination. The two younger tag matches advance with ID/EX. In EX, each select combines its saved match with the producer's registered, trap-checked forwarding eligibility. A suppressed write leaves the saved operand available to the ALU, branch comparator, store, address repair and RVFI.\n\nBoth source fields participate in the EX and MEM M-result interlocks. A destination of x0 creates no match. An immediate M consumer takes two bubbles on a ready bus. A consumer with one intervening instruction takes one. Repair and data backpressure take priority over a younger dependency.\n\n## Retirement during a memory hold\n\nThe first formal run found a liveness counterexample at step 20. Two M-dependency bubbles preceded an eight-clock divide. A younger load then needed address repair, which held the completed divide in MEM past the ten-clock retirement bound.\n\nThe fix lets MEM transfer to WB on the first held edge and clears EX/MEM valid. Its tag, forwardable bit and result remain stable for the resident EX instruction. Subsequent hold cycles produce bubbles in WB. Once an M result drains, a dependent fetch can capture it from the register file. A directed regression checks the divide's retirement cycle and the younger load's single successful transfer after repair and two denials.\n\n## Marker-window measurements\n\nThe ten-iteration CoreMark run with both stall generators active started at cycle 11,828 and stopped at 3,358,011. The window took 3,346,183 cycles, up 111,187 cycles (3.44%) from 3,234,996 in the starting RTL.\n\n| M-dependency bubbles between accepted MMIO markers | Starting RTL | Split forwarding |\n|---|---:|---:|\n| EX match | 35,599 | 35,593 |\n| MEM match alone | 0 | 85,999 |\n| Total | 35,599 | 121,592 |\n\nA simulation-only monitor counted valid fetches that actually inserted a dependency bubble. It excluded held EX slots and unavailable fetches. The net increase was 85,993 M-dependency bubbles. The remaining 25,194 cycles break down into 24,529 more unavailable fetches, 266 more annulled replay candidates, 414 more denied data requests and 15 fewer address repairs.\n\n## FPGA timing and fitness\n\nGowin placement options 0, 1 and 2 reached 82.934, 85.507 and 85.507 MHz. Median Fmax rose 0.21% over the champion's 85.328 MHz. CoreMark throughput fell from 263.77 to 255.54 iterations/s, a 3.12% regression. At the measured cycle count the acceptance threshold requires approximately 92.32 MHz.\n\nIn the two median placements the limiting path starts at EX/MEM's registered forwarding eligibility, crosses the EX operand selection and ALU, and ends at the EX/MEM integer result register. Placement option 0 is limited by a path from the instruction replay RAM through ID to data memory.\n\n## Verification\n\nVerilator passed the requested strict lint command. All six cocotb runners passed, including 31 pipeline cases. Co-simulation passed selftest and three random programs against the ISS with and without stalls. CoreMark produced the five canonical CRCs and the validation message.\n\nThe liveness check passed after one RTL fix. The remaining checks from the full formal rerun are in progress.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
