{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_35", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'tail -20 cores/bench/LESSONS.md && cat fpga/constraints/clock.sdc'", "aggregated_output": "- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-002-r6s1 (regression, +8.96%): Registering the load and ALU results separately in EX/MEM moved their selection mux off the ALU register input, raising median prototype Fmax from 60.666 to 64.712 MHz at the same 3,235,202 cycles for ten CoreMark iterations.\n- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-003-r6s2 (improvement, +14.62%): Issuing loads in EX let dependent branches use EX/MEM forwarding with either address generator, and the full-width adder took only 22 more cycles over ten CoreMark iterations while reaching 64.020 MHz versus 61.400 MHz for same-page prediction in matched prototypes.\n- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-001-r7s0 (improvement, +12.13%): With DIV/REM forwarded from MEM/WB, registering both producer tag matches in decode took the comparisons off the EX operand path and raised prototype median Fmax from 68.274 to 76.825 MHz, while `ex_mem_w.forwardable` kept a trapping younger writer from masking the older value.\n- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-002-r7s1 (regression, +10.13%): With forwarding matches registered in ID, splitting at divisor magnitude 2^17 limited divider feedback to two 20-bit radix-8 stages for small divisors or one full-width stage for large divisors, yielding 222.86 CoreMark iterations/s versus 226.92 for the design that forwarded DIV/REM from MEM/WB.\n- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-003-r7s2 (regression, +6.03%): Reading a 512-row table in ID let EX replay denied RAM loads when the tag matched and all requested byte lanes were valid, saving 132,870 cycles over ten CoreMark iterations, too few to offset the higher Fmax of the retimed divider and forwarding design (214.58 vs 226.92 iterations/s).\n- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-001-r8s0 (improvement, +10.44%): Splitting at divisor magnitude 2^14 raised Fmax by reducing divider feedback to one digit selection per clock, with the seven radix-8 trial subtractors supplying fit bits and residual prefixes for every fourth nonzero radix-32 digit.\n- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-002-r8s1 (regression, +5.53%): With reciprocal interpolation and one exact correct\n…[3690 of 6762 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:161c75f854259830]…\n Fmax to 80.378 MHz, with the MEM multiply bypass into `ID/EX.alu_b_default` only 6 ps shorter.\n- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-002-r12s1 (regression, -7.93%): With the synchronous RAM lookup in EX, 59,268 load-to-address interlocks and 52,157 refill-wait cycles limited CoreMark cycle savings to 1.92%, while the path from `fwd_rs1` through the EX ALU to EX/MEM limited median Fmax to 77.051 MHz versus 85.328 MHz for the champion.\n- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-003-r12s2 (regression, -11.15%): Independent replay reads for register-file and forwarded bases matched the single-port table's 3,089,744 CoreMark cycles, but a 16-level path from `MEM/WB.alu_result` through EX and ID to `EX/MEM.alu_result` limited median Fmax to 72.409 MHz versus 80.119 MHz with one port.\n// Gowin timing constraint for the V2 FPGA fitness (tools/eval/gowin.py).\n// The target only steers timing-driven optimization; the score is the\n// Actual Fmax from the timing report. Fixed for every design.\ncreate_clock -name clock -period 5 [get_ports {clock}]\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
