{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_128","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/implementation_notes.md'","aggregated_output":"# Carry-select forwarding\n\nEX computes the 16-bit low sum and carry alongside two 16-bit upper sums for ADD, ADDI, AUIPC and SUB. SUB complements the second operand and seeds the low adder with one. EX/MEM holds the carry and both upper candidates. MEM selects the upper half once for forwarding, address prediction and writeback. Loads, jump links and other integer results use the primary half with a clear carry select.\n\nID reuses the EX sum for the ADD base page and computes the low 13 address bits independently from the two operands and the displacement. ID/EX holds both candidate upper halves until EX drives the request. An address repair overwrites the primary address and clears its carry select. A denied request holds the entire representation.\n\nThe first implementation took 3,234,996 bracketed CoreMark cycles for ten iterations. Gowin reported 81.296, 78.937 and 78.937 MHz across placements. The registered carry fed a critical path through operand forwarding and the shifter. The register-file address calculation also appeared among the worst paths.\n\nThe timing refinement gives the shift and compare operations direct access to rs1, prepares the older forwarding operand while MEM selects its upper half, and computes the low register-file and writeback address candidates in parallel.\n\nLocal verification is in progress.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
