{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_46","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c 'git diff --check; git status --short --untracked-files=all; cat cores/bench/implementation_notes.md'","aggregated_output":" M cores/bench/rtl/alu.sv\n M cores/bench/test/test_alu.py\n# Two-stage radix-8 divider\n\nThe first iteration divides the top two dividend bits by the registered divisor magnitude and registers 3D, 5D and 7D. Each of the five remaining iterations computes two radix-8 digits. Each stage subtracts all seven multiples from a 35-bit trial in parallel. Adjacent borrow decisions select the largest fitting multiple and its precomputed difference. A zero divisor selects the maximum digit at every stage and preserves the dividend prefix as the remainder.\n\nThe implementation follows the proposed schedule. The existing counter controls issue, six iteration clocks and the held result in both hardware and ALTOPS mode. The existing signed-magnitude correction handles signed division by zero and signed overflow.\n\n## Measurements\n\n| Metric | Prediction | Measured |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| Bracketed cycles for 10 CoreMark iterations | 4,491,485 | 4,491,485 |\n| Median Gowin Fmax | ~50 MHz | 46.838 MHz |\n| CoreMark iterations/s | ~111 | 104.28 |\n\nGowin placement options 0, 1 and 2 reached 45.032, 46.838 and 46.838 MHz, respectively. The median improved 12.46% over 41.649 MHz and cleared the stated 43.56 MHz threshold by 3.278 MHz. On the two median-frequency placements, the worst setup path runs from the register file through the single-cycle multiplier DSP and result mux to the data-memory address. The parallel divider also costs area. Gowin reports 3,429 logic resources and 664 registers, up from 2,786 and 566.\n\n## Verification\n\n- Verilator passed the requested full-core lint with zero warnings.\n- Local riscv-formal passed all 53 checks on the first run with the single-retirement wrapper and ALTOPS enabled.\n- The ALU cocotb suite passed, including directed DIVU and REMU checks at each representable digit boundary and its neighbors for the selected divisors. The vectors exercise both digit stages, all four initial prefixes, zero divisors and large multiples. Each operation uses the six-clock handshake and an integer reference.\n- All 12 pipeline cocotb tests passed, including divider dependencies under memory stalls.\n- Cosimulation passed for the selftest and three random programs in both memory-stall modes. CoreMark passed the canonical CRC and UART checks under instruction and data stalls.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
