{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_34", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"date -u; rg -n -m5 'reg_q.rs|ssram|Register' cores/bench/generated/timing/gowin_p1/impl/gwsynthesis/*.rpt* | cut -c1-300; cat ../../LESSONS.md\"", "aggregated_output": "Fri Nov 20 04:57:46 UTC 2026\n144:<td class=\"label\"><b>Register </b></td>\n249:<td class=\"label\">Register</td>\n254:<td class=\"label\">&nbsp&nbsp--Register as Latch</td>\n259:<td class=\"label\">&nbsp&nbsp--Register as FF</td>\n- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-001-r1s0 (regression, +471.20%): Eight serial trial subtractions on the `partial_q` feedback path limited median Fmax to 31.094 MHz versus 41.649 MHz for six steps per clock, while CoreMark executed no DIV/REM between markers to benefit from the shorter divide latency.\n- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-002-r1s1 (improvement, +665.10%): Moving combinational `/` and `%` off the shared EX result path into a six-cycle restoring divider raised median Fmax from 5.442 to 41.649 MHz without adding divider stalls because CoreMark issued no DIV/REM between its markers.\n- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-003-r1s2 (regression, +6.19%): Predicting branches by displacement bias and taking JAL in fetch saved 302,818 CoreMark cycles by avoiding EX redirects, while the combinational DIV/REM path still limited median Fmax to 5.392 MHz.\n- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-001-r2s0 (regression, +10.91%): Replacing six serial trials with three radix-4 stages that test divisor multiples in parallel raised median Fmax from 41.649 to 46.194 MHz, exposing the path from the register file through hazard logic to ID/EX as the new timing limit.\n- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-002-r2s1 (improvement, +12.46%): Registering 3D, 5D and 7D let two radix-8 stages replace six serial trial subtractions, raising median Fmax from 41.649 to 46.838 MHz at the same 4,491,485 CoreMark cycles, with the single-cycle multiplier limiting both median-frequency placements.\n- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-003-r2s2 (regression, +10.77%): Predicting branches and JAL in fetch avoided EX redirects and saved 302,818 CoreMark cycles, but the six-step divider’s `remainder_q` feedback path limited median Fmax to 43.026 MHz, leaving throughput below the radix-8 core (102.72 vs 104.28 iterations/s).\n- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-001-r3s0 (regression, +18.45%): Registering four multiply partial products at EX/MEM raised median Fmax from 46.838 to 55.927 MHz, while registering the forwarded operands and doing the full multiply in MEM reached 62.078 MHz with the same 0.81% CoreMark cycle penalty from multiply-use stalls.\n- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-002-r3s1 (improvement, +31.48%): Bubbling invalid fetches at ID/EX let the load/multiply interlock compare raw `rs1` and `rs2` with the producer’s `rd` in parallel with EX redirect resolution, raising prototype median Fmax from 46.286 to 60.355 MHz with the same MEM multiplier.\n- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-003-r3s2 (regression, +9.70%): On the radix-8 core, predicting branches and JAL in fetch with ID/EX recovery saved 302,818 CoreMark cycles, but moving the timing-critical multiply from EX to MEM delivered higher throughput (137.11 vs 114.40 iterations/s).\n- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-001-r4s0 (improvement, +11.97%): Driving combinational imem with the resolved EX target let ID/EX capture a ready target instruction as the branch or jump left EX, avoiding the redirect bubble even for JALR with a forwarded base and cutting CoreMark cycles by 7.88%.\n- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-002-r4s1 (regression, +11.61%): A one-entry IF buffer let decode issue a word saved during an interlock even with `io_imemReady` low, saving 119,419 cycles over unbuffered EX target fetch across ten CoreMark iterations, but lower Fmax reduced throughput from 153.52 to 153.03 iterations/s.\n- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-003-r4s2 (regression, +7.21%): Carrying `alternate_pc` and registering `miss` kept the forwarded EX comparison off the same-cycle ID/EX flush path, but each miss still cost a fetch opportunity and throughput trailed same-cycle EX target fetch (147.00 vs 153.52 CoreMark iterations/s).\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
