he ID/EX address register. A crossing or unavailable base suppresses the request for one cycle while forwarded rs1 plus the displacement replaces the candidate. A denied request freezes the slot, successor replay metadata, and forwarding sources until transfer. Alignment faults suppress both strobes and the destination write. An accepted load puts its extended value on the EX/MEM bypass. Products still complete in MEM and retain the multiply-use interlock.\n\nAll writeback values share one MEM/WB result field. EX/MEM separately captures the serviced byte address and raw read word for RVFI. To shorten the register-file paths seen in placement, the 32×32 array uses distributed RAM. Reset clears a per-register written bit, and the read ports select zero for unwritten registers. The write-first bypass and x0 behavior are preserved.\n\n## Local verification\n\n- Verilator lint passed without warnings. All six cocotb runners passed, including 25 pipeline tests. Directed checks cover a load and dependent taken branch retiring on consecutive cycles, both page-crossing directions, 32-bit wrap, unsupported producers, and a corrected misaligned access. A repaired store held its address, mask, and data through three denials and transferred once.\n- The selftest and three random ELF traces matched the reference ISS with zero waits and with both stall generators. CoreMark completed ten iterations in 3,234,996 cycles between markers 11,828 and 3,246,824, with all five canonical CRCs and the validation message.\n- Gowin placement options 0, 1, and 2 reached 62.112, 65.467, and 65.467 MHz. The median gives 202.37 iterations/s, 14.63% above the supplied 176.55 iterations/s reference. The divider recurrence is the longest path in the median placement.\n- The original register file gave the same cycle count and a 58.342 MHz median. An experiment that also moved forwarding-tag comparisons into ID reached 64.738 MHz, so the selected design retains the existing EX comparisons. The through