{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_109","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/implementation_notes.md; git status --short'","aggregated_output":"# Decode-indexed load replay\n\nID reads a 512-row synchronous block RAM using bits `[10:2]` of the same effective-address candidate it sends to EX. The ADD candidate uses carry-save addition of the two forwarded operands and the displacement. EX retains the base-availability and page-cross checks, holds the row during a stall, and reads the corrected index on a repair edge.\n\nEach row contains a 9-bit address tag, a 4-bit byte mask, and a 32-bit word. An accepted RAM read fills all four lanes. An accepted store replaces the word and mask with the replicated write data and bus strobes. A denied load can advance only after its checked, aligned address matches a row below 1 MiB and every requested lane is current. EX rejects a lookup that coincided with a write to the same index.\n\nEX formats the live and replayed words in parallel. EX/MEM registers the replay result and its select bit. A replayed word load can supply the address of the immediately following load in ID. I also moved the forwarding-register comparisons into the preceding cycle to shorten the path from a producer through EX to the next address prediction. The single-retirement interface follows the target's declared `nret: 1`.\n\nReset disables replay immediately and clears one row mask per clock for 512 clocks. During that interval the core uses the external bus. RAM replay assumes the core observes writes to the cached memory region. MMIO always requires a live response.\n\n## Local verification\n\n- Verilator strict lint passed with zero warnings.\n- All 98 cocotb cases passed across six suites. The 30 pipeline cases include sustained denials with unrelated read data, a dependent pointer load, address repair, partial stores, ready-response priority, equal-index aliases, collisions, annulment, misalignment, MMIO and reset.\n- Co-simulation passed on five ELFs. Selftest and three random programs matched the ISS with and without stalls. The stalled CoreMark run produced all five canonical CRCs and the validation banner.\n- CoreMark took 3,088,894 cycles between the MMIO markers for ten iterations, saving 146,102 cycles against the 3,234,996-cycle champion.\n- Gowin placement options 0, 1 and 2 reached 63.704, 66.280 and 66.280 MHz. The median gives 214.58 iterations/s, a 6.03% gain over the champion's 202.37 iterations/s. The timed design uses 4,221 logic cells, 904 registers, eight BSRAMs and two DSPs.\n M cores/bench/rtl/core.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/ex_stage.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/id_stage.sv\n M cores/bench/test/test_pipeline.py\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
