tiply partial products at EX/MEM raised median Fmax from 46.838 to 55.927 MHz, while registering the forwarded operands and doing the full multiply in MEM reached 62.078 MHz with the same 0.81% CoreMark cycle pena\n…[5368 of 8440 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:0d147e11adbb8680]…\nhe radix-32/radix-8 divider.\n- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-001-r9s0 (regression, -1.10%): Annulling dependent instructions in EX preserved the one-bubble M-result interlock, but the path from the registered `fwd_rs1` select to the EX/MEM result register limited median Fmax to 80.185 MHz versus 81.075 MHz with the interlock in ID.\n- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-002-r9s1 (improvement, +5.25%): Registering a distributed-RAM copy of replay `rs1` bypassed BSRAM delay on the path from `rs1` to the address adder, and annulling only validity and operation enables kept the replay PC check off the EX operand muxes, raising median Fmax from 81.075 to 85.328 MHz.\n- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-003-r9s2 (regression, +4.58%): Registering the two-successor RAM output before decode raised median Fmax from 81.075 to 85.931 MHz, but replay on a denied fetch now depended on predicting across two control-flow transitions, costing 43,391 cycles over ten CoreMark iterations and leaving the throughput gain just below the 4.6% acceptance margin.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
