{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261120-001-r10s0.yaml'","aggregated_output":"id: hyp-20261120-001-r10s0\ntitle: Capture integer bypass values at issue and reserve the EX bypass for M results\ncategory: micro_opt\nmotivation: |\n  On the champion's 85.328 MHz placement, the path from MEM/WB result bit 1\n  through the EX branch comparison to ID/EX.pc[16] arrives at 17.014 ns.\n  The path from older_rs1_q through the ALU to EX/MEM.alu_result[27] arrives\n  at 17.006 ns. Both paths begin by reconstructing an operand in EX. A trial\n  that registered only the bypass selects reached 78.497 MHz median. A second\n  prototype captured every result in ID. It preserved all 3,234,996 CoreMark\n  cycles, but its priority-mux version put the MEM multiplier on an 18.270 ns\n  path to ID/EX.rs1_val and reached only 77.070 MHz on placement 1.\nhypothesis: |\n  Move the bypass data mux across the ID/EX boundary. For each incoming\n  source, compute three mutually exclusive enables during decode. The\n  youngest eligible producer supplies the outgoing EX result, including\n  formatted load data on an accepting edge and PC+4 for a legal jump.\n  An older integer writer supplies its registered EX/MEM value. Otherwise\n  capture the write-first register-file value. If the owning older writer\n  is an M instruction, latch one override bit for that source instead.\n  Capture the selected 32-bit value directly in the existing ID/EX source\n  register. Each EX source now has a single two-input mux between this\n  captured operand and registered MEM/WB data, selected directly by its\n  M override flop. This removes the three-way priority path and terminates\n  the multiplier at MEM/WB. An immediately dependent ADD still issues\n  every clock. The timing tradeoff is the return path from the outgoing\n  ALU result to the next operand register. Compute source ownership in\n  parallel with the result so only the data selection follows arithmetic.\n  Qualify the youngest writer with its actual trap-checked write enable.\n  A denied load holds issue until acceptance. An M producer retains its\n  current interlock and then forwards from MEM/WB through the override. A held consumer\n  keeps its captured operands through repair or data-bus backpressure.\n  At 3,234,996 cycles for ten iterations the acceptance target is a\n  three-placement median above 89.25 MHz.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 6\n  confidence: low\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/forward_unit.sv\n    description: Compute combinational one-hot producer ownership for both ID source tags, including x0 exclusion, youngest-writer priority and suppression of a trapping EX writer.\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: Export the exact data and eligibility entering EX/MEM. Use one two-input mux per EX operand to select the captured value or the registered M result for execution, stores and RVFI.\n  - file: rtl/id_stage.sv\n    description: Capture the masked OR of the eligible EX, MEM and register-file values separately for rs1 and rs2 when ID advances. Latch each M override on that same edge and retain both values and overrides on every hold.\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: Connect the outgoing EX value and registered EX/MEM integer value to ID, and route the WB result to the M-only EX muxes. Preserve the address generator's existing base matches and the late-result issue interlock.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: Exercise different producers for both operands, competing writes to one rd with a younger misaligned load, a held store, and an M result consumed after its interlock.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
