{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_14", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261120-002-r1s1.yaml'", "aggregated_output": "id: hyp-20261120-002-r1s1\ntitle: Move DIV and REM off the single-cycle execute path\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  The baseline retires ten CoreMark iterations in 4,491,485 cycles at a median\n  5.442 MHz. Gowin reports a 199.837 ns path through forwarding and the EX ALU.\n  The ALU selects full-width combinational quotient and remainder results onto\n  the same bus that feeds the data address. These operators are the strongest\n  candidate for the reported depth of 156 cells. The RV32IM reference\n  interpreter ran 2,922,015 instructions between the canonical -O3 binary's\n  MMIO markers, including 93,960 MULs and zero DIV, DIVU, REM or REMU\n  instructions. The run produced all five expected CRCs.\nhypothesis: |\n  Give EX one shared, fixed-latency restoring divider. At issue, register the\n  magnitudes of the forwarded operands and the signs needed for the result.\n  Process six quotient bits per clock for six clocks using a 36-bit dividend\n  padded with four leading zeros. Each bit uses the borrow from a trial\n  subtraction to select the next remainder. After the sixth clock, select and\n  sign-correct the quotient or remainder for the ordinary EX/MEM result slot.\n  This bounds the divider feedback path to six subtract/select steps and\n  preserves single-cycle EX for integer ALU operations and all multiplies.\n  Only a valid divide or remainder holds younger instructions. Older operations\n  drain, and the completed result uses the existing forwarding and retirement\n  path. The instruction mix predicts zero divider stalls in the measured\n  loop. At the baseline cycle count, a median Fmax of 8.17 MHz would deliver\n  the projected 50% gain. Check both median Fmax and marker-to-marker cycles\n  because a change in stall-generator phase at entry can shift the cycle count.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 50\n  confidence: high\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/alu.sv\n    description: |\n      Add clocked request, result-valid and consume signals for the four divide\n      and remainder operations. Implement the shared recurrence a\n…[1030 of 4102 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:7457312fe98e33cc]…\nm divider issue through the last\n      iteration. Release them on result transfer. Keep priority for a blocked\n      older data request, which must hold EX/MEM and retain WB bypass data.\n      Let MEM and WB drain normally during divider-only stalls.\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: |\n      Wire the EX divider interlock into hazard control. Use the registered\n      EX/MEM result for immediate consumers and the existing channel-0\n      writeback for retirement.\n  - file: test/test_alu.py\n    description: |\n      Drive the divider handshake for every existing DIV/REM vector. Check\n      mixed-sign, high-bit and randomized inputs against integer arithmetic,\n      bounded completion, and stable results when consumption is delayed.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Exercise consecutive divides, load-to-divide, and divide-to-store\n      dependencies with instruction stalls and an older stalled data request.\n      Check the forwarded values, store handshake, and consecutive RVFI orders.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
