on, +18.45%): Registering four multiply partial products at EX/MEM raised median Fmax from 46.838 to 55.927 MHz, while registering the forwarded operands and doing the full multiply in MEM reached 62.078 MHz with the same 0.81% CoreMark cycle penalty from multiply-use stalls.\n- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-002-r3s1 (improvement, +31.48%): Bubbling invalid fetches at ID/EX let the load/multiply interlock compare raw `rs1` and `rs2` with the producer’s `rd` in parallel with EX redirect resolution, raising prototype median Fmax from 46.286 to 60.355 MHz with the same MEM multiplier.\n- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-003-r3s2 (regression, +9.70%): On the radix-8 core, predicting branches and JAL in fetch with ID/EX recovery saved 302,818 CoreMark cycles, but moving the timing-critical multiply from EX to MEM delivered higher throughput (137.11 vs 114.40 iterations/s).\n- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-001-r4s0 (improvement, +11.97%): Driving combinational imem with the resolved EX target let ID/EX capture a ready target instruction as the branch or jump left EX, avoiding the redirect bubble even for JALR with a forwarded base and cutting CoreMark cycles by 7.88%.\n- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-002-r4s1 (regression, +11.61%): A one-entry IF buffer let decode issue a word saved during an interlock even with `io_imemReady` low, saving 119,419 cycles over unbuffered EX target fetch across ten CoreMark iterations, but lower Fmax reduced throughput from 153.52 to 153.03 iterations/s.\n- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-003-r4s2 (regression, +7.21%): Carrying `alternate_pc` and registering `miss` kept the forwarded EX comparison off the same-cycle ID/EX flush path, but each miss still cost a fetch opportunity and throughput trailed same-cycle EX target fetch (147.00 vs 153.52 CoreMark iterations/s).\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
