{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_13", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'tail -1 cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl'", "aggregated_output": "{\"id\": \"hyp-20261120-001-r11s0\", \"title\": \"Fold the older bypass into the operand read in decode\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The champion's placement 0 critical path starts at older_rs1_q and ends at\\nEX/MEM.alu_result. On placement 1 the WB result also feeds the branch\\ncomparison on the path to ID/EX.pc. Round 10 tried capturing both bypass\\nvalues at issue. That put the producing ALU and the next operand register\\non the same path and reduced median Fmax to 80.207 MHz.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The champion's placement 0 critical path starts at older_rs1_q and ends at\\nEX/MEM.alu_result. On placement 1 the WB result also feeds the branch\\ncomparison on the path to ID/EX.pc. Round 10 tried capturing both bypass\\nvalues at issue. That put the producing ALU and the next operand register\\non the same path and reduced median Fmax to 80.207 MHz.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Move only the older bypass into ID. For each source register, select the\\ncurrent EX/MEM writer's complete MEM result or the write-first register\\nfile value and latch it in the existing ID/EX operand register. Compute\\nthis fallback even when the current EX instruction names the same rd.\\nAt the next edge a legal younger producer has its result in EX/MEM.\\nEach EX operand then needs one two-input mux between that result and the\\ncaptured fallback. A younger writer suppressed by a trap uses the fallback.\\nThis removes WB data and older-source priority from both the ALU and\\nbranch paths while keeping the one-instruction integer dependency loop\\nthrough the existing EX/MEM result register.\\n\\nUse MEM's existing integer/product/divide selection so a dependent\\ninstruction can capture an M result on its producer's MEM-to-WB edge.\\nThe timing risk is the extra data mux after the MEM multiplier. Check that\\npath alongside the ALU and branch endpoints in Gowin. The design should\\nretain 3,234,996 bracketed cycles. At that count a median above 89.26 MHz\\nclears the 4.6% acceptance margin.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 6, \"conf\n…[3229 of 6301 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:9716a6477c0297fe]…\negression\", \"fitness\": 258.94, \"delta_pct\": -1.83, \"vs_baseline\": 2036.47, \"fmax_mhz\": 83.766, \"ipc_coremark\": 3e-06, \"cycles\": 3234996, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 4946, \"ff\": 942, \"seeds\": [80.654, 83.766, 83.766], \"perturbations\": [[0, 80.654, 10], [1, 83.766, 10], [2, 83.766, 10]], \"lutram\": 96, \"bsram\": 6, \"dsp\": 5, \"critical_path\": {\"from\": \"cpu/u_if/successors_successors_0_0_s\", \"to\": \"dmem_2_dmem_2_0_0_s\", \"slack_ns\": -7.399, \"arrival_ns\": 17.693, \"logic_cells\": 11, \"modules\": [\"clock_ibuf\", \"cpu/u_if\", \"cpu/u_id\"]}, \"logic_levels\": 10, \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-11-20T12:55:35.483357Z\", \"slot\": 0, \"round_id\": 11, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-001-r11s0 (regression, -1.83%): Capturing the older MEM bypass in ID preserved 3,234,996 CoreMark cycles, but the path from successor RAM through the ID address adder to data RAM limited median Fmax to 83.766 MHz, with the EX/MEM branch operand path to `ID/EX.pc` within 2 ps.\"}\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
