tiply partial products at EX/MEM raised median Fmax from 46.838 to 55.927 MHz, while registering the forwarded operands and doing the full multiply in MEM reached 62.078 MHz with the same 0.81% CoreMark cycle pena\n…[7569 of 10641 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:e040aaef1e43b87e]…\nimited median Fmax to 83.766 MHz, with the EX/MEM branch operand path to `ID/EX.pc` within 2 ps.\n- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-002-r11s1 (regression, -3.12%): Capturing the older integer bypass in ID left consumers waiting for M results at writeback, adding 85,999 MEM interlock bubbles over ten CoreMark iterations and outweighing the 0.21% gain in median Fmax.\n- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-003-r11s2 (regression, -9.55%): Predicting the carry into bit 16 added only 0.136% to CoreMark cycles, but driving pipeline enables with `carry_miss` put carry verification on the critical path from MEM/WB through divider issue to `quotient_q`, cutting median Fmax from 85.328 to 77.287 MHz.\n- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-001-r12s0 (regression, -5.80%): Exact byte carry lookahead kept CoreMark at 3,234,996 cycles, but the path from `imem_ready` through register-file selection and the ID displacement adder to the data RAM address limited median Fmax to 80.378 MHz, with the MEM multiply bypass into `ID/EX.alu_b_default` only 6 ps shorter.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
