{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_11", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261120-002-r12s1.yaml'", "aggregated_output": "id: hyp-20261120-002-r12s1\ntitle: \"Pipeline the load/store unit through a synchronous RAM lookup in EX\"\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  The champion takes 3,234,996 cycles at 85.328 MHz. Its ID stage calculates\n  five candidate addresses so an EX load can forward to the next instruction.\n  Both round-eleven forwarding experiments reported paths from successor RAM\n  through ID to data memory. A scratch design with a registered external\n  request slot and local load service took 3,074,760 marker cycles with both\n  buses stalling and all five CRCs correct. Its median was only 72.449 MHz.\n  It still launched the local RAM read from the speculative ID address and\n  fed the hit decision back into EX admission.\nhypothesis: |\n  Move the synchronous lookup one stage later. Compute the exact byte address\n  once in EX from forwarded rs1 and the signed displacement. On EX transfer,\n  launch a 512-entry word RAM read with that address and capture the full\n  address, access width, store lanes and instruction in EX/MEM. The RAM\n  output and its requesting instruction arrive in MEM together. This removes\n  the ID address candidates, producer-specific page arithmetic and repair\n  cycle. It also removes the tag check from the issuing load's EX stage.\n\n  Give a resident RAM load the returned word as its provisional forwarding\n  value. In parallel, check the full word tag and valid lanes and format the\n  selected bytes for the consumer. On a hit the next EX instruction can use\n  the value on the normal EX/MEM bypass. Keep the tag comparator off that\n  data bus. On a miss hold the resident load and its younger consumer, refill\n  from an accepted external read, and resume from the registered refill.\n  Gate younger transfers, divide launch and fetch redirection until the\n  older load can complete. Hold a known fetch PC during a miss. A load used\n  immediately as another memory operation's base waits one cycle for its\n  registered WB value, cutting the RAM-output-to-address-adder recurrence.\n\n  MEM also owns a one-entry external co\n…[2049 of 5121 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:7ca42576de36faba]…\n     output in parallel with validation. Retire each instruction once and\n      preserve raw memory fields for the single RVFI channel.\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: |\n      Connect the data port to MEM and its load result to the existing\n      youngest bypass. Remove the ID address predictor wiring. Keep older\n      WB data available throughout a MEM hold and resume from the saved PC.\n  - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n    description: |\n      Coordinate MEM backpressure, the load-to-address interlock and the\n      existing M-result interlock. Allow an older retirement to drain once\n      while retaining stalled operands, forwarding tags and request identity.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Check adjacent load-to-branch forwarding, a missed load feeding JALR,\n      and a load feeding another address. Exercise partial stores, same-row\n      collisions, queue replacement, MMIO and FENCE.I under prolonged bus\n      denials. Compare accepted transactions and channel-zero retirements.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
