{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_13","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261120-002-r13s1.yaml'","aggregated_output":"id: hyp-20261120-002-r13s1\ntitle: Carry both upper sums across EX/MEM for exact integer forwarding\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  The 85.328 MHz champion takes 3,234,996 cycles for ten iterations. Gowin traced a path from older_rs1_q through EX to bit 23 of the EX/MEM result. The same forwarded operands feed a second full-width ADD in ID to form the page of an immediately dependent memory access. Round 12 kept the whole carry-select operation before the register and reached 80.378 MHz. Round 11 put speculative carry verification on pipeline enables and reached 77.287 MHz.\nhypothesis: |\n  Split ADD, ADDI, AUIPC and SUB at bit 16. In EX, compute the low half and its unsigned carry-out alongside two upper halves for carry-in zero and one. For subtraction complement the second operand and seed the low adder with one. Register all three data slices and the carry at EX/MEM. Fold ordinary ALU, link and formatted load results into the same representation using their exact low and high halves and a zero select. Select the correct registered upper half on the MEM side for forwarding and writeback. A dependent instruction can issue on the following clock, including a branch or JALR that reads the full result.\n\n  Apply the same register cut to the ID address path that consumes the outgoing EX ADD. ID already computes the low 13 bits of A+B+displacement independently. For its ADD base page, take bits 15:12 from the low adder and carry both candidate values for bits 31:16 across ID/EX with the producer's carry. Other address sources and a repaired address supply an exact upper half. EX selects the registered upper address bits when it presents the request. The page-cross and alignment checks use the existing low bits. This removes the second full-width ADD without feeding a carry decision into stall or replay control.\n\n  Measure bracketed cycles and the three-placement median. At the champion's cycle count, 90.5 MHz yields about 280 iterations/s and 89.26 MHz clears acceptance. The timing risk is the upper-half mux on a back-to-back forwarded result, especially on the path to a branch successor PC.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 6\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/alu.sv\n    description: Expose the low sum, carry and two upper sums for integer addition and subtraction. Keep multiplier and divider routing and handshakes intact.\n  - file: rtl/core_pkg.sv\n    description: Extend EX/MEM with an alternate upper result and carry select, and ID/EX with the corresponding upper-address fields.\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: Capture split arithmetic results at EX/MEM and select the upper request address from the resident ID/EX fields. Make the split ADD base available to ID on the same cycle as its current operands.\n  - file: rtl/id_stage.sv\n    description: Replace the full-width add_base with the producer's slices, carry the candidate pages across issue, and install an exact address on repair. Hold all address fields together on a denied request.\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: Reconstruct the exact EX/MEM result once for both EX forwarding inputs and ID's MEM-base candidate. Wire the independent EX sum slices to ID.\n  - file: rtl/mem_stage.sv\n    description: Feed the reconstructed integer result to MEM/WB alongside the existing multiply and divide result choices. Preserve the exact value for register writes and RVFI.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
