tiply partial products at EX/MEM raised median Fmax from 46.838 to 55.927 MHz, while registering the forwarded operands and doing the full multiply in MEM reached 62.078 MHz with the same 0.81% CoreMark cycle pena\n…[9527 of 12599 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:39731d74893ba60d]…\nson and redirect to `ID/EX.pc` limited median Fmax to 79.724 MHz versus the champion's 85.328 MHz.\n- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-002-r13s1 (regression, -7.49%): Registering both upper sums kept CoreMark at 3,234,996 cycles, but the next EX shifter had to wait for `EX/MEM.result.carry` to select its forwarded operand, cutting median Fmax from 85.328 to 78.937 MHz.\n- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-003-r13s2 (regression, -26.42%): Replay hits on 97.88% of denied RAM loads saved 133,745 cycles over ten CoreMark iterations, but the falling-edge RAM read left only half a cycle to format subword loads before the candidate register captured them, cutting median Fmax from 85.328 to 60.190 MHz.\n- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-001-r14s0 (regression, -5.96%): Reusing the checked ID sum for ADDI/JALR and moving ADD/SUB/AUIPC to MEM incurred 12,164 ADDI repair holds and 15,229 ADD/SUB dependency bubbles over ten CoreMark iterations, while the path from the forwarded MEM/WB result through EX to `ID/EX.pc` limited median Fmax to 81.446 MHz.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
