{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_8", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261120-003-r5s2.yaml'", "aggregated_output": "id: hyp-20261120-003-r5s2\ntitle: Replay the predicted successor instruction on denied fetches\ncategory: predictor\nmotivation: |\n  The champion already issues a ready branch or JALR target as its\n  predecessor leaves EX. Its ten CoreMark iterations take 4,171,092\n  cycles. The previous direction predictor took 4,223,613 cycles because\n  each miss cost a fetch opportunity. A predictor that remembers the\n  instruction at the successor can recover fetch opportunities during\n  instruction-bus stalls, even in straight-line code.\nhypothesis: |\n  Train a 512-entry table indexed by predecessor PC. Each row predicts the\n  successor PC and its instruction from the last ready external fetch.\n  Read one cycle ahead using the likely next predecessor's word index.\n  Direct JAL and backward branches select the direct target index.\n  Other instructions select fall-through. This guess drives only the\n  internal RAM read port. EX always drives the resolved address on imem.\n\n  On a ready fetch, decode the live response. On a denied fetch, a valid\n  prediction can enter ID/EX provisionally. Compare the saved destination\n  against PC+4 and the full EX redirect target in parallel. Register both\n  equality bits and the actual redirect decision. In the next cycle the\n  selected equality bit admits the instruction to EX. A mismatch annuls\n  that slot and IF retries the resolved address already in retry_pc.\n  This verifies the address for indirect jumps and table aliases as well.\n  Verification costs no extra cycle on a hit or a ready bus response.\n\n  A scratch prototype completed ten iterations in 3,486,909 bracketed\n  cycles with both stall generators, a 16.40% cycle reduction. All five\n  CRCs and the UART validation banner matched. Gowin placement options\n  0, 1 and 2 reached 59.777, 61.398 and 61.398 MHz. The median yields\n  176.08 iterations/s, 14.70% above 153.52. At this cycle count the\n  acceptance threshold requires more than 55.9935 MHz. The predictor\n  added two BSRAMs. The prototype passed all 53 standard single-channel\n  forma\n…[1667 of 4739 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:286696ee24e7a42b]…\nENCE.I by\n      bubbling its successor and refetching it from the bus.\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: |\n      Wire the ID/EX displacement, direct-control hint, stall_id and\n      effective FENCE.I to IF. On a registered prediction mismatch, mask\n      the resident ID/EX valid bit and its entire decoded control bundle\n      before EX, IF and the late-result interlock consume them. This\n      suppresses redirects, divide issue, forwarding and memory strobes\n      from a rejected slot. RVFI retains the actual resolved successor.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      After warm-up, deny the bus throughout eight iterations of a loop\n      whose first instruction is a store. Require one retirement per cycle,\n      an exact store count, and correct recovery on exit. Exercise a\n      changed JALR target behind a stalled store, two predecessor PCs\n      2 KiB apart, and a modified successor across FENCE.I. Drive poison\n      on denied responses and check PC continuity, order and bus writes.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
