{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_11", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261120-002-r6s1.yaml'", "aggregated_output": "id: hyp-20261120-002-r6s1\ntitle: \"Move data access into EX with a separate registered load bypass\"\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  Successor replay brings ten CoreMark iterations down to 3,486,909 cycles,\n  but its load-use interlock still bubbled 197,861 available fetches in\n  that interval. On 38,301 of those cycles the bus denied the fetch and\n  the successor table supplied a candidate. Each bubble also leaves EX\n  without the valid predecessor that enables replay on the retry. Moving\n  data access into EX previously saved 203,487 cycles on the pre-replay\n  core. The champion's option-zero timing path crosses the shared EX ALU\n  on its way to data memory.\nhypothesis: |\n  Generate the effective address while a live or replayed memory instruction\n  enters ID/EX. Drive the data port from that register in EX. At the\n  accepting edge, clock the extended load value and the integer ALU result\n  into separate EX/MEM fields. Select between the registered values at\n  the bypass source. This takes the load mux off the ALU register input\n  and lets the next instruction consume a load, including a branch issued\n  from replay. Only the admitted EX slot owns the data port. A replay\n  address mismatch must squash both bus strobes before the edge.\n\n  Bypass available base producers into address generation. For an immediately\n  preceding ADD, ADDI or AUIPC, fold its two operands and the displacement\n  with a carry-save compressor and one final adder. An accepted aligned LW\n  can supply a pointer directly from the bus. Retry an instruction whose\n  youngest base producer is unavailable. Preserve a single registered\n  byte address on dmem across backpressure.\n\n  A scratch prototype completed ten iterations in 3,235,202 bracketed\n  cycles, saving 251,707 cycles against the champion with both stall\n  generators enabled. The load-use bubbles disappeared. Only 848 extra\n  bubbles were solely due to unavailable address bases. Unheld cycles\n  without a fetch word fell from 81,698 to 26,658. The prototype passed\n  the canonical CRC \n…[2975 of 6047 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:af71ca113b8eff68]…\nwing the older\n      producer to drain. A multiply feeding a memory base must reach WB.\n      Give a resident bus hold priority over a younger dependency bubble.\n  - file: rtl/mem_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Carry the completed access to single-channel WB with aligned RVFI\n      address, lane masks, raw read data and replicated write data. Use\n      the raw word and registered byte offset to form WB read_data in MEM.\n      Preserve held WB operands for consumers stalled in EX.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Check consecutive retirement for a load and dependent branch with a\n      warmed replay entry and denied fetch. Force a mismatching replay of\n      a store and check for zero writes. Exercise LW-to-pointer bypass,\n      unavailable base producers, misalignment, and a multi-cycle denied\n      store followed by exactly one acceptance and retirement. Update\n      cycle checks that pair an outstanding store with its successor's\n      fetch PC to reflect the earlier bus request.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
