tiply partial products at EX/MEM raised median Fmax from 46.838 to 55.927 MHz, while registering the forwarded operands and doing the full multiply in MEM reached 62.078 MHz with the same 0.81% CoreMark cycle pena\n…[6904 of 9976 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:320344160e3812ee]…\ne successor PC in ID/EX limited median Fmax to 83.868 MHz at the same 3,234,996 CoreMark cycles.\n- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-003-r10s2 (regression, -2.10%): Predicting live branches and jumps from the last successor PC removed EX resolution from the fetch path, but misses forced retries even with `imem_ready` high, adding 41,750 cycles over ten CoreMark iterations while the path from `older_rs1_q` to the EX/MEM ALU result limited median Fmax to 84.612 MHz.\n- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-001-r11s0 (regression, -1.83%): Capturing the older MEM bypass in ID preserved 3,234,996 CoreMark cycles, but the path from successor RAM through the ID address adder to data RAM limited median Fmax to 83.766 MHz, with the EX/MEM branch operand path to `ID/EX.pc` within 2 ps.\n- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-002-r11s1 (regression, -3.12%): Capturing the older integer bypass in ID left consumers waiting for M results at writeback, adding 85,999 MEM interlock bubbles over ten CoreMark iterations and outweighing the 0.21% gain in median Fmax.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
