{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261120-003-r1s2.yaml'","aggregated_output":"id: hyp-20261120-003-r1s2\ntitle: Predict direct control flow with a displacement-biased agree predictor\ncategory: predictor\nmotivation: |\n  Fetch advances to PC+4 even at a hot loop backedge or a direct call. Because\n  IF/ID is combinational, a correct next-PC prediction saves one fetch opportunity\n  on a ready cycle. Backward loop branches and forward exit branches can share\n  the same counter constructively if it records agreement with their respective\n  displacement biases.\nhypothesis: |\n  Predict backward conditional branches taken and forward branches fall-through.\n  Use the high bit of a 256-entry table of two-bit saturating counters to invert\n  that prediction at sites that repeatedly disagree with the bias. Predict JAL\n  taken and leave JALR sequential until EX. Decode direct targets from the\n  current instruction, so aliases affect direction alone. On the prototype,\n  stalled CoreMark took 4,188,667 bracketed cycles versus 4,491,485 at baseline\n  and passed all five CRCs. Gowin reported 5.236, 5.528 and 5.528 MHz. The\n  median gives 13.20 iterations/s versus 12.12. The cycle improvement alone\n  gives 7.23% more iterations per cycle. The prototype used 16 additional\n  LUTRAMs and three additional flip-flops. It passed all 53 single-retire\n  fast formal checks.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 9\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Implement a 256 x 2 distributed RAM indexed by PC[9:2], with one\n      asynchronous fetch read and one synchronous EX update. Initialize words\n      to 01 (weak agreement) at FPGA configuration and retain them on warm\n      reset. On update, increment the saved count on disagreement and decrement\n      on agreement, saturating at 3 and 0. For legal B encodings, predict\n      taken as instr[31] XOR counter[1].\n      For JAL, predict taken unconditionally. Share a PC-relative adder for\n      B/J immediates and use a target prediction only when word-aligned and\n      below 0x00100000. Carry the resulting chosen direction and counter\n      snapshot with the instruction. Advance along the prediction only when\n      fetch is accepted. EX recovery has priority even if imemReady is low.\n  - file: rtl/core_pkg.sv\n    description: Add the chosen direction bit and two-bit counter snapshot to if_id_t and id_ex_t.\n  - file: rtl/id_stage.sv\n    description: Capture, hold and flush those three bits with the corresponding instruction under the existing ID/EX enables.\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Resolve the actual successor using forwarded operands and the existing\n      misalignment trap logic. For a valid advancing instruction, flush on a\n      mismatch between chosen and resolved direction. Correct to PC+4 on a\n      fall-through or trap, and to the resolved target on a taken transfer.\n      Drive RVFI pc_next from that same successor. Keep the younger fetch on\n      a match. Emit the resolved conditional branch direction for training.\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: |\n      Wire the correction into the current redirect/flush path. Send the\n      resolving PC index, saved counter and actual-taken XOR imm[31] to IF.\n      Train once per valid advancing conditional branch, inhibiting updates\n      during reset or an older dmem stall. Using the saved count requires\n      only one table read port.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Exercise a trained backward loop through its fall-through exit and two\n      branches 0x400 bytes apart with opposite displacement signs. Assert\n      the RVFI successor sequence and absence of wrong-path stores when\n      a correction overlaps imem backpressure or waits behind a stalled load.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
