{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_12", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261120-003-r3s2.yaml'", "aggregated_output": "id: hyp-20261120-003-r3s2\ntitle: Predict direct branches and jumps on the radix-8 core with recovery at ID/EX\ncategory: predictor\nmotivation: |\n  The champion fetches PC+4 after every instruction. Its combinational IF/ID\n  boundary costs one fetch opportunity on each taken transfer resolved in EX.\n  The two prior predictor runs both saved 302,818 of 4,491,485 bracketed\n  CoreMark cycles. The latest reached 102.72 iterations/s on a parent whose\n  divider limited median Fmax to 43.026 MHz. The radix-8 champion reaches\n  46.838 MHz, but one placement exposes a path from EX recovery through the\n  fetch instruction mux and load-use detection back to ID/EX.\nhypothesis: |\n  Use the tested 256-entry displacement-biased branch predictor and predict\n  aligned direct JAL targets in fetch. Keep correction off the younger\n  instruction's decode and register-read paths. Decode the raw fetch word\n  and annul its effects at the ID/EX control and valid registers when EX\n  corrects the PC. A correct prediction lets the successor enter ID/EX on\n  the next accepted fetch. Keep JALR resolution in EX.\n\n  A prototype on this champion completed ten iterations in 4,188,667\n  marker-to-marker cycles with both stall generators active and all five\n  canonical CRCs correct. Gowin placement options 0, 1 and 2 reached\n  46.283, 47.521 and 47.521 MHz. The median gives 113.45 iterations/s,\n  8.8% above 104.28. At that cycle count the acceptance threshold is\n  45.69 MHz. The prototype used 16 additional LUT-RAMs and three\n  additional flip-flops. The median critical path runs through the\n  multiplier. Check final placement against the frequency threshold.\n\n  The prototype passed all 53 fast single-retire formal checks, the\n  five-ELF cosim suite and the pipeline test for recovery when an older\n  load releases on the same cycle that instruction memory stalls.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 9\n  confidence: high\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Index a 256 x 2 asynchronous-read LUT-RAM with PC[9:2]. Initialize\n     \n…[1867 of 4939 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:74f2c1a282f719dd]…\nlder\n      data stall or div_wait. Otherwise bubble its controls on a load-use\n      hazard, an EX correction or an unready fetch. A load-use bubble\n      retains the fetch PC. A correction installs the recovery PC even\n      with imemReady low. Remove the correction-to-fetch-word mux path.\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: |\n      Connect EX correction and its resolved successor to IF. Route EX's\n      training enable, saturated count and ID/EX.pc[9:2] to the predictor\n      write port. Feed raw instruction fields to ID and hazard detection.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Check hit timing and channel-0 successor PCs through a trained loop\n      and its exit. Exercise colliding counters, direct JAL, a misaligned\n      taken target, and an unpredicted JALR. On a correction behind a\n      stalled data access, drop imemReady on the release edge and place\n      a store on the wrong path. Check the recovery PC, absence of the\n      store on the bus and consecutive retirement orders.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
