 (regression, +18.45%): Registering four multiply partial products at EX/MEM raised median Fmax from 46.838 to 55.927 MHz, while registering the forwarded operands and doing the full multiply in MEM reached 62.078 MHz with the same 0.81% CoreMark cycle pena\n…[4022 of 7094 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:43e3b6569e7fb7b0]…\nign that forwarded DIV/REM from MEM/WB.\n- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-003-r7s2 (regression, +6.03%): Reading a 512-row table in ID let EX replay denied RAM loads when the tag matched and all requested byte lanes were valid, saving 132,870 cycles over ten CoreMark iterations, too few to offset the higher Fmax of the retimed divider and forwarding design (214.58 vs 226.92 iterations/s).\n- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-001-r8s0 (improvement, +10.44%): Splitting at divisor magnitude 2^14 raised Fmax by reducing divider feedback to one digit selection per clock, with the seven radix-8 trial subtractors supplying fit bits and residual prefixes for every fourth nonzero radix-32 digit.\n- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-002-r8s1 (regression, +5.53%): With reciprocal interpolation and one exact correction replacing serial digit selection, the path from successor instruction RAM through decode to the data-memory address limited median Fmax to 77.471 MHz versus 81.075 MHz for the radix-32/radix-8 divider at the same CoreMark cycle count.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
