{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_12", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261120-003-r8s2.yaml'", "aggregated_output": "id: hyp-20261120-003-r8s2\ntitle: Predict small-divisor quotients with a reciprocal table and exact residual correction\ncategory: predictor\nmotivation: |\n  The champion takes 3,234,996 cycles for ten CoreMark iterations. Its\n  reported 74-cell timing path runs through two dependent divider digit\n  decisions, from quotient_q to remainder_q. CoreMark executes no DIV or\n  REM between its markers, so shortening that path can improve every timed\n  instruction at the existing cycle count. Clearing the 4.6% margin at\n  that count requires a median above 76.79 MHz.\nhypothesis: |\n  Predict the entire unsigned quotient for divisor magnitudes 2 through\n  255 using a 256-entry constant ROM of floor(2^32 / D). For dividend\n  magnitude A, the high word of A times the ROM entry gives q0. Since\n  A < 2^32, q0 is exact or one too small. The residual\n  A - q0*D lies in [0, 2D), so its low nine bits suffice. Compare this\n  residual with D. If it is at least D, select q0+1 and residual-D.\n  Use two unsigned 16x16 multipliers across two clocks to form all four\n  partial products. Compute q0+1 in parallel with the residual so the\n  correction decision selects a registered quotient. Handle D=0 and D=1\n  explicitly.\n\n  This predictor covers every magnitude that would require more than 24\n  quotient bits. For D >= 256, A[31:24] is already a legal remainder.\n  Seed with those eight bits and bring down A[23:0] in six radix-16\n  digits. Register the odd multiples from 3D through 15D during setup\n  and wire the even multiples as shifts. In each recurrence, subtract\n  all 15 multiples in parallel. Adjacent borrow decisions select the\n  greatest fitting multiple and its difference. This removes the serial\n  radix-4 to radix-8 dependency from the clock path. The sixth digit lands on the existing\n  EX transfer edge. Both modes deliver the signed result through MEM with\n  the champion's issue, backpressure and forwarding timing.\n\n  A scratch implementation ran ten iterations in 3,234,996 bracketed\n  cycles with both stall generators and all five c\n…[1347 of 4419 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:0875b067be813818]…\n:0] - q0[8:0]*D[7:0]) modulo 512. Hold the estimate and its\n      operands during backpressure and latch the corrected result on consume.\n      Use 36-bit unsigned large-divisor trials with a separate borrow bit.\n      Derive mode from the saved divisor and capture sign and operation\n      on issue. Apply RV32M divide-by-zero and signed overflow rules. Advance\n      the last digit once on consume and keep the MEM result stable. Route\n      the existing ALTOPS result through the same handshake.\n  - file: test/test_alu.py\n    description: |\n      Exercise every predicted divisor at exact multiples and their\n      neighbors, including large dividends and cases needing correction.\n      Cover 255/256/257, the maximum large-path quotient, all four RV32M\n      operations, reset and delayed consume. In the final-group test use\n      signed REM of -0x12345 by 256. After the six wait clocks expect\n      -0x34 throughout the hold, then -0x45 after consume. Repeated\n      consume must leave the completed result stable.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
