{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_11", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nrows=[json.loads(s) for s in open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl')]\nfor r in rows[-8:]:\n print(r['id'],r['hypothesis']['hypothesis'][-2300:])\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261120-002-r9s1 Partition the replay entry by when its fields are needed. Give the five\nrs1 bits a 512-by-5 distributed RAM with an asynchronous read feeding\nfive flip-flops. Read it at the same lookahead index and on the same\nedge as the synchronous successor BSRAM. The flip-flops supply rs1\nearly enough in the next cycle for the register read and address sum.\nThe BSRAM supplies the saved destination PC, valid bit and remaining\ninstruction bits. Both reads have the original one-cycle latency.\n\nLimit replay annulment to the controls that authorize an operation.\nA rejected entry can still select ALU inputs and compute a discarded\nresult while its invalid bit and cleared enables suppress every effect.\nThis takes the full-PC replay check off the arithmetic operand selects.\n\nA scratch implementation ran ten iterations in 3,234,996 bracketed cycles\nwith both stall generators, the five expected CRCs and the validation\nbanner. Gowin placement options 0, 1 and 2 gave 75.641, 85.328 and\n85.328 MHz. The median yields 263.77 iterations/s, 5.25% above 250.62.\nAcceptance at this cycle count requires more than 84.805 MHz. The\nmargin is 0.523 MHz, so a fresh three-placement median matters.\nThe prototype adds 522 logic cells and five registers on placement 0.\nThe register-tag path alone reached 76.264 MHz on placement 1, and\nlimiting annulment alone reached 74.043 MHz on that same placement.\nThe cocotb suite and all 53 standard single-channel formal checks\npassed. Selftest and all three random ELF traces matched the reference\nwith and without stalls. A separate induction proof checked an arbitrary\ntable index. After a write, both banks hold the same rs1 there, and a\nnoncolliding read of that entry supplies matching registered tags\nthrough any subsequent stall.\n\nhyp-20261120-003-r9s2 Train each of the 512 predictor rows on a pair of successive control-flow\ntransitions. For the executed sequence B, C, D, row B learns the full\ndestination word PC and instruction of D from a ready bus fetch. While A\nexecutes, use the existing \n…[7856 of 10928 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:6fefa4047292533c]…\now carry-in of one. If those four bits all propagate, use the\neffective B sign as the carry guess. This costs one short carry chain before\nthe upper sum and handles common additions of small signed displacements.\nUse the same predicted sum for the integer EX result and the ADD-produced\nbase page that ID needs for an immediately dependent load or store.\n\nVerify the carry before the producer transfers. On a mismatch hold the\nproducer, its forwarding sources and its successor for one clock, save the\nexact boundary carry, then use it for the high sum on the retry. Qualify the\ninterlock with a valid, effectful arithmetic operation so annulled slots and\nirrelevant default ALU_ADD decodes cannot hold the pipeline. The check only\ncompares one bit and can run alongside upper-result generation. A key risk is\nmoving the limiting path to carry verification and the pipeline enables.\nMeasure repair cycles and bracketed cycles as well as the three-placement\nmedian. At a 0.5% cycle cost acceptance requires about 89.70 MHz.\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
