{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_12", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfor l in open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl'):\n r=json.loads(l)\n if r['id'].endswith(('r11s0','r9s2','r12s1')):print(r['id'],r['hypothesis']['hypothesis'])\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261120-003-r9s2 Train each of the 512 predictor rows on a pair of successive control-flow\ntransitions. For the executed sequence B, C, D, row B learns the full\ndestination word PC and instruction of D from a ready bus fetch. While A\nexecutes, use the existing short lookahead index for its likely successor B\nto launch that row's synchronous read. While B executes, copy the complete\nreturned row into a replay register. While C executes, that register can\nsupply D on a denied fetch. A ready fetch always supplies the live word.\nThis adds a clock boundary between the RAM read and the ID address logic\nwhile retaining one issue per cycle on a warm stable path.\n\nPair the training key with consecutive admitted instructions. Invalidate\nbroken chains across bubbles, retries or annuls. Freeze the read and replay\npipeline together across EX holds. Carry row validity and read/write\ncollision handling through the extra register. On a denied fetch keep the\nexisting full-PC checks against fall-through and aligned redirect target.\nA mismatch cancels every EX side effect and retries the resolved PC. Flush\npending reads and training on reset or FENCE.I. The tradeoff is reduced\nreplay coverage after unpredictable branches. With a 1% cycle penalty the\nmedian must exceed 85.66 MHz to clear the 4.6% acceptance margin.\n\nhyp-20261120-001-r11s0 Move only the older bypass into ID. For each source register, select the\ncurrent EX/MEM writer's complete MEM result or the write-first register\nfile value and latch it in the existing ID/EX operand register. Compute\nthis fallback even when the current EX instruction names the same rd.\nAt the next edge a legal younger producer has its result in EX/MEM.\nEach EX operand then needs one two-input mux between that result and the\ncaptured fallback. A younger writer suppressed by a trap uses the fallback.\nThis removes WB data and older-source priority from both the ALU and\nbranch paths while keeping the one-instruction integer dependency loop\nthrough the existing EX/MEM result register.\n\nUse MEM's ex\n…[1751 of 4823 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:baa1f4c9b9f33422]…\n buffered in order. Hold its pins until\nacceptance and allow drain and replacement on one edge. Update the local\nrow when ownership transfers. A partial store may invalidate neighboring\nlanes. Clear row validity on reset and hold the read result with its MEM\nslot across stalls. A same-index RAM read/write collision forces a miss,\nand a refill waits behind any older buffered store. Give each command exactly one\ntransfer. Bypass local service for MMIO, drain older writes at fences and\ntermination, and preserve alignment traps before issuing any command.\n\nThe scratch result measures the service mechanism. The EX lookup and\nremoval of ID address generation are the unmeasured timing changes. At\n3.10 million cycles, 85.54 MHz clears the tournament margin and 87 MHz\nyields about 281 iterations/s. At 3.14 million cycles the acceptance\nthreshold rises to 86.64 MHz. Check the delay of RAM-to-branch forwarding\npaths and count pointer interlocks and refill waits between\nthe markers. Either can erase the projected gain.\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
