{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_14", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfor row in map(json.loads,open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl')):\n if row['id'].endswith(('r10s1','r11s0','r11s1','r13s0')):\n  print(row['id'],row['hypothesis']['hypothesis'])\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261120-002-r10s1 Retime operand selection across ID/EX. Form the final youngest,\nolder and local grants for each source while its instruction is in ID.\nFor the ALU, group the two forwarded values on one arm of a mux and\nthe local register value and PC or immediate on the other. Capture the\ngrants alongside the consumer. This keeps the branch-selected PC out\nof the D input of a combined local operand register.\n\nA scratch implementation of that EX routing reached 85.338, 85.574\nand 85.574 MHz across the three placements. It retained 3,234,996\nbracketed cycles with both buses stalling, the five canonical CRCs\nand the validation banner. That yields 264.53 iterations/s. The\nmedian placement's worst path then ran from imem_ready through the rs1\nread and address adder to a data-address register. Apply the same\nlate selection to the ID address operand. Use the live rs1 tag and the\nregistered replay rs1 tag as independent asynchronous RF read addresses.\nForm each RF-based address candidate with its own instruction's displacement.\nSelect the completed candidates with imem_ready. A denied fetch still\nneeds the existing full-PC verification before any memory side effect.\nThe two reads allow both additions to begin before bus ownership is known.\n\nThe additional RF read port costs distributed RAM and the extra\ndisplacement adder costs carry cells. At the existing cycle count,\n89.255 MHz would clear acceptance. The additional live/replay read\nports and parallel address candidates are the unmeasured part of the\nproposal. Check whether they remove the imem_ready-to-address path\nand lift the three-placement median past that threshold.\n\nhyp-20261120-001-r11s0 Move only the older bypass into ID. For each source register, select the\ncurrent EX/MEM writer's complete MEM result or the write-first register\nfile value and latch it in the existing ID/EX operand register. Compute\nthis fallback even when the current EX instruction names the same rd.\nAt the next edge a legal younger producer has its result in EX/MEM.\nEach EX operand th\n…[2295 of 5367 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:665d56f29b40d23d]…\nory instruction. Its upper bits supply the page directly and\na single 13-bit addition supplies the low address and page-cross check.\nThis removes both forwarded EX operands from the arithmetic base bypass.\n\nApply the same rule when the next word is itself ADDI. It can carry a\nverified address through a chain of pointer increments. An unavailable\nbase or a page crossing invalidates the candidate. The youngest writer\nretains ownership even when its candidate is invalid. A dependent memory\naccess then uses the existing one-cycle repair from its exact forwarded\nrs1 before asserting a bus request. Register-register ADD and AUIPC\nproducers take this repair path. Integer ADDI execution, forwarding to\nordinary consumers, and retirement still use the exact ALU result.\n\nMeasure bracketed cycles and the three-placement Gowin median. Around\n3.24 million cycles, acceptance requires approximately 89.4 MHz. The\ntiming risk is that an EX operand-to-branch path still sets the clock\nafter its address-generation fanout falls.\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_15","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c 'cat test/cosim/build.sh && ls -l /opt/hwe-python/bin/python* && ls -l /opt/gowin/IDE/bin/gw_sh && ls -la bench/programs/coremark/baremetal && du -sh cores/bench/rtl'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_15","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c 'cat test/cosim/build.sh && ls -l /opt/hwe-python/bin/python* && ls -l /opt/gowin/IDE/bin/gw_sh && ls -la bench/programs/coremark/baremetal && du -sh cores/bench/rtl'","aggregated_output":"#!/usr/bin/env bash\n# Build the Verilator-based cosim binary that drives `core` against an ELF\n# program. Produces $OBJ_DIR/cosim_sim (default: test/cosim/obj_dir/cosim_sim).\n#\n# RTL_DIR (default: rtl/) is globbed dynamically (with core_pkg.sv forced\n# first because its compilation-unit-scope typedefs and localparams must be\n# visible before any module references them). Hypotheses are allowed to add,\n# rename, or delete files inside rtl/, so a hardcoded file list would\n# silently break restructuring hypotheses.\nset -euo pipefail\n\nREPO_ROOT=\"$(cd \"$(dirname \"${BASH_SOURCE[0]}\")/../..\" && pwd)\"\nCOSIM_DIR=\"$REPO_ROOT/test/cosim\"\nif [ -z \"${RTL_DIR:-}\" ] || [ -z \"${OBJ_DIR:-}\" ]; then\n  echo \"ERROR: test/cosim/build.sh requires RTL_DIR and OBJ_DIR env vars.\" >&2\n  echo \"  Example: RTL_DIR=cores/v1/rtl OBJ_DIR=cores/v1/obj_dir bash test/cosim/build.sh\" >&2\n  exit 2\nfi\n\n# NRET picks which RVFI port set main.cpp expects on `core`.\n# 1 = single-issue (only `_0` ports); 2 = dual-channel (both `_0` and `_1`).\n# Default 2 preserves existing behavior; orchestrator sets to 1 for cores\n# whose core.yaml declares nret: 1.\nNRET=\"${NRET:-2}\"\n\n# Ensure OSS CAD Suite tools are on PATH for non-interactive shells.\nTOOLCHAIN=\"$REPO_ROOT/.toolchain\"\nif [ -d \"$TOOLCHAIN/oss-cad-suite/bin\" ]; then\n  export PATH=\"$TOOLCHAIN/oss-cad-suite/bin:$PATH\"\nfi\n\nmkdir -p \"$OBJ_DIR\"\n\n# Glob $RTL_DIR/*.sv. core_pkg.sv first; the rest in a stable lexicographic\n# order (modulo case-insensitive sort, which Verilator/gcc don't care\n# about). If core_pkg.sv is missing the build will catch that downstream\n# via undefined-typedef errors.\nRTL_FILES=()\n[ -f \"$RTL_DIR/core_pkg.sv\" ] && RTL_FILES+=(\"$RTL_DIR/core_pkg.sv\")\nfor f in \"$RTL_DIR\"/*.sv; do\n  [ \"$(basename \"$f\")\" = \"core_pkg.sv\" ] && continue\n  RTL_FILES+=(\"$f\")\ndone\n\nif [ ${#RTL_FILES[@]} -eq 0 ]; then\n  echo \"ERROR: no rtl/*.sv files found.\" >&2\n  exit 1\nfi\n\nverilator --cc --exe --build \\\n  -Mdir \"$OBJ_DIR\" \\\n  \"+incdir+$RTL_DIR\" \\\n  --top-module core \\\n  -Wall -Wno-fatal -Wno-style \\\n  -CFLAGS \"-DNRET=$NRET\" \\\n  \"${RTL_FILES[@]}\" \\\n  \"$COSIM_DIR/main.cpp\" \\\n  -o cosim_sim\n\necho \"Built: $OBJ_DIR/cosim_sim\"\nls: cannot access '/opt/hwe-python/bin/python*': No such file or directory\n","exit_code":2,"status":"failed"}}
