{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_18","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfor row in map(json.loads,open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl')):\n if row['id'].endswith(('r9s1','r5s2','r4s1','r7s2')):\n  print(row['id'], row['ipc_coremark'],row['motivation'],row['hypothesis']['hypothesis'][:990])\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_18", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfor row in map(json.loads,open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl')):\n if row['id'].endswith(('r9s1','r5s2','r4s1','r7s2')):\n  print(row['id'], row['ipc_coremark'],row['motivation'],row['hypothesis']['hypothesis'][:990])\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261120-002-r4s1 2e-06 The champion spends 4,527,760 cycles on ten CoreMark iterations. A taken\nbranch spends one fetch opportunity on fall-through even though EX knows\nthe target that cycle. A backend hold can also discard a ready instruction\nand force decode to wait for the bus a second time.\n Give IF one fall-through buffer holding an instruction and its PC. When\nempty, drive the instruction address from the registered fetch cursor or\nthe aligned redirect resolved by EX. Decode can accept the target on the\nsame edge that the branch advances. If a redirect stalls, retain the\nexact target for retry. On a sequential fetch, buffer a ready word when\ndecode is blocked and advance the bus cursor. Decode can later issue\nthat word even when imemReady is low.\n\nRecord whether the buffered instruction permits refill. When issuing a\nbuffered branch, JAL, JALR or fence, leave the buffer empty for its\nsuccessor. For an ordinary instruction, dequeue and refill on the same\nedge if the bus is ready. Thus a buffered word and an EX redirect cannot\ncoexist. The registered full bit selects the instruction presented to\ndecode, keeping EX branch comparison off the register-file address path.\n\nThe guarded buffer completed ten iterations in 4,051,673 bracketed\ncycles with both bus stalls e\nhyp-20261120-003-r5s2 3e-06 The champion already issues a ready branch or JALR target as its\npredecessor leaves EX. Its ten CoreMark iterations take 4,171,092\ncycles. The previous direction predictor took 4,223,613 cycles because\neach miss cost a fetch opportunity. A predictor that remembers the\ninstruction at the successor can recover fetch opportunities during\ninstruction-bus stalls, even in straight-line code.\n Train a 512-entry table indexed by predecessor PC. Each row predicts the\nsuccessor PC and its instruction from the last ready external fetch.\nRead one cycle ahead using the likely next predecessor's word index.\nDirect JAL and backward branches select the direct target index.\nOther instructions select fall-through. This guess drives onl\n…[2490 of 5562 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:84c30f7aabdf22ce]…\nissuing a replayed instruction.\n Partition the replay entry by when its fields are needed. Give the five\nrs1 bits a 512-by-5 distributed RAM with an asynchronous read feeding\nfive flip-flops. Read it at the same lookahead index and on the same\nedge as the synchronous successor BSRAM. The flip-flops supply rs1\nearly enough in the next cycle for the register read and address sum.\nThe BSRAM supplies the saved destination PC, valid bit and remaining\ninstruction bits. Both reads have the original one-cycle latency.\n\nLimit replay annulment to the controls that authorize an operation.\nA rejected entry can still select ALU inputs and compute a discarded\nresult while its invalid bit and cleared enables suppress every effect.\nThis takes the full-PC replay check off the arithmetic operand selects.\n\nA scratch implementation ran ten iterations in 3,234,996 bracketed cycles\nwith both stall generators, the five expected CRCs and the validation\nbanner. Gowin placement options 0, 1 and 2 gave 75.641, 85.328 and\n85.328 MHz. T\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
