{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_109", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"cat > cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261120-001-r14s0.yaml <<'YAML'\nid: hyp-20261120-001-r14s0\ntitle: \\\"Use the checked ID address sum for ADDI and JALR\\\"\ncategory: micro_opt\nmotivation: |\n  The champion's two longest paths on its 85.328 MHz placement were within\n  8 ps. One ended at the EX/MEM integer result and the other at the next\n  instruction's PC. ADDI and JALR both need rs1 plus the same signed\n  12-bit immediate that ID can already add for a load. A scratch JALR-only\n  conversion kept the 3,234,996-cycle CoreMark bracket, but reached only\n  80.036 MHz on placement 1. Its longest path still ended at EX/MEM result\n  bit 30. Moving the indirect-jump calculation alone leaves the ordinary\n  integer carry chain in the timing budget.\nhypothesis: |\n  Place RV32I additions according to when their operands are available.\n  For ADDI and JALR, carry ID's existing tentative address into EX. For\n  JALR, extract the I displacement from bits 31:20. A verified ADDI\n  forwards the registered sum through EX/MEM. A verified JALR clears bit\n  zero, checks bit one for a target trap and writes PC+4 for a legal link.\n  For either operation, an unavailable base or page crossing uses the\n  existing one-cycle repair before the result or target is consumed.\n  A verified ADDI can itself supply the next ID base, including after repair.\n  \n  Route register-register ADD and SUB, and PC-relative AUIPC, through the\n  existing MEM late-result slot. Compute their exact sums from EX/MEM\n  operands and interlock immediate dependents using the existing M-result\n  interlock. Keep the DIV launch decode specific to DIV/REM. This removes\n  the general 32-bit add/sub carry chain from the EX integer-result mux\n  and the duplicate A+B page calculation from ID. The EX rs1+imm repair\n  adder ends at the held address register. A ready, in-page ADDI remains\n  available to its immediate consumer on the usual edge.\n\n  Count repair holds on ADDI/JALR and extra dependent-use bubbles on\n  the three MEM operations between the markers. At a 2% cycle increase\n  the three-placement median must exceed 91.04 MHz to clear acceptance.\n  The principal risk is that the forwarded branch comparison or the ID\n  address adder sets the clock after removing the EX arithmetic path.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 6\n  confidence: low\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/core_pkg.sv\n    description: \\\"Carry the immediate-sum classification in ID/EX and enough operation and operand information in EX/MEM to select integer addition, subtraction or the existing M result.\\\"\n  - file: rtl/id_stage.sv\n    description: \\\"Extract S displacements only for stores and I displacements for loads, ADDI and JALR. Capture the checked ADDI base plus displacement directly. Remove the EX operand compressor and full-width ADD-base calculation. Mark legal ADD, SUB and AUIPC as late writers.\\\"\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: \\\"Route the checked resident ADDI result back to the ID base candidates, with youngest-writer ownership and acceptance gating. Preserve LUI, accepted LW, older integer and RF candidates and the existing M interlock.\\\"\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: \\\"Extend repair to ADDI and JALR, hold both forwarding sources, suppress redirect and result transfer until the sum is exact, and use the registered sum on release. Pass PC/immediate or forwarded rs1/rs2 to MEM. Qualify DIV issue with the legal M encoding.\\\"\n  - file: rtl/alu.sv\n    description: \\\"Remove the redundant combinational ADD/SUB outputs from the integrated EX result path while preserving the standalone ALU interface, its unit checks and both M arithmetic implementations.\\\"\n  - file: rtl/mem_stage.sv\n    description: \\\"Select ADD, SUB or AUIPC from the resident operation before M result selection. Register the exact 32-bit result for WB forwarding and retirement, holding it during backpressure.\\\"\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: \\\"Exercise back-to-back ADDI across forward and backward page crossings, an immediately dependent load, ADD/SUB/AUIPC dependencies, and JALR with bit zero or bit one set. Check repaired PCs, link suppression, operand data and single retirement through stalls.\\\"\nYAML\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
