{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_96","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"sed -n '1,50p' cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261120-001-r6s0.yaml\"","aggregated_output":"id: hyp-20261120-001-r6s0\ntitle: \"Forward loads from EX/MEM using a registered decode-stage address\"\ncategory: micro_opt\nmotivation: |\n  The champion takes 3,486,909 cycles for ten CoreMark iterations. Its\n  successor replay can supply the next instruction during a fetch stall,\n  but a load-use interlock still discards that slot. The previous trace\n  counted 135,320 loads immediately followed by a branch with a matching\n  source. On the pre-replay core, early address generation saved 203,487\n  cycles and moved the reported worst path away from the data RAM.\nhypothesis: |\n  Compute the full byte address as a load or store enters ID/EX and drive\n  dmem directly from that register during EX. Capture the formatted load\n  into EX/MEM on acceptance. An immediately dependent branch can then\n  resolve from a registered operand and issue its successor in one cycle.\n\n  Give the decode-stage address adder two candidate bases. The ordinary\n  candidate selects the youngest available EX/MEM result or the write-first\n  register file. If the immediately preceding EX instruction produces the\n  base, bypass only a LUI immediate or the raw word of an aligned LW.\n  Calculate each candidate plus the sign-extended displacement in parallel.\n  Retry consumers of other pending bases when their producer advances. This\n  keeps the arithmetic operands of the preceding EX instruction off the\n  path to the address register. In particular, an ADD followed by a load\n  using its result as the base costs one bubble.\n\n  A replayed instruction may have entered ID/EX provisionally. Qualify\n  every early request and data wait with the resident slot's verified\n  controls and alignment. A rejected prediction must retry the resolved\n  PC without issuing a store, reading MMIO or blocking EX on dmemReady.\n  Keep address, store data and replay verification aligned across waits.\n\n  A scratch prototype took 3,320,238 bracketed cycles with both stall\n  generators, saving 166,671 cycles. All five CRCs and the UART validation\n  banner matched. Gowin placement options 0, 1 and 2 reached 64.787,\n  67.200 and 67.200 MHz. The median gives 202.40 iterations/s, 14.64%\n  above the champion and above the 184.67 iterations/s acceptance threshold.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 15\n  confidence: high\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/core_pkg.sv\n    description: Carry the byte address in ID/EX and the serviced address and raw read word in EX/MEM for RVFI.\n  - file: rtl/id_stage.sv\n    description: Form the ordinary and LUI/LW-bypassed address sums in parallel and latch the selected sum beside the candidate instruction. Mark only products as late. Give loads and jumps an internal ALU selector that produces zero so EX can combine the real result sources in parallel.\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: Present the registered address, alignment-qualified strobes and forwarded, lane-replicated store data. OR the integer ALU result with the individually enabled load, link and divide values into EX/MEM. Record the accepted raw load and address, and suppress the destination write on misalignment.\n  - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n    description: Interlock a memory instruction whose base is pending in EX or on a multiply in MEM. Preserve the multiply-use interlock for other consumers. Freeze ID/EX and EX/MEM on a denied EX data request, clear MEM/WB valid and retain its forwarding data.\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
