cy placements.\n- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-003-r2s2 (regression, +10.77%): Predicting branches and JAL in fetch avoided EX redirects and saved 302,818 CoreMark cycles, but the six-step divider’s `remainder_q` feedback path limited median Fmax to 43.026 MHz, leaving throug\n…[2696 of 5768 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:c1118c22a03c48da]…\nor PC against `fallthrough_pc` and `redirect_target` let EX annul mismatches.\n- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-001-r6s0 (regression, +12.49%): Limiting EX bypasses into the ID address adder to LUI immediates and aligned LW data made addresses using the preceding ALU result wait one cycle, trading 85,036 extra cycles over ten CoreMark iterations for a rise in median Fmax from 63.294 to 67.200 MHz in matched prototypes.\n- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-002-r6s1 (regression, +8.96%): Registering the load and ALU results separately in EX/MEM moved their selection mux off the ALU register input, raising median prototype Fmax from 60.666 to 64.712 MHz at the same 3,235,202 cycles for ten CoreMark iterations.\n- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-003-r6s2 (improvement, +14.62%): Issuing loads in EX let dependent branches use EX/MEM forwarding with either address generator, and the full-width adder took only 22 more cycles over ten CoreMark iterations while reaching 64.020 MHz versus 61.400 MHz for same-page prediction in matched prototypes.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
