{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_54","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"cat > cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261120-001-r7s0.yaml <<'YAML'\nid: hyp-20261120-001-r7s0\ntitle: Finish division on EX transfer and forward the signed result from MEM/WB\ncategory: micro_opt\nmotivation: |\n  In a matched Gowin build of the champion, the median placement's worst\n  divider feedback path arrived at 20.569 ns. A path from the WB destination\n  register through forwarding to EX/MEM.alu_result arrived at 20.547 ns.\n  Both paths carry a cost for division on every clock, although CoreMark\n  executes zero DIV/REM instructions between its markers.\nhypothesis: |\n  Treat DIV/REM as a late MEM/WB producer. The edge that currently copies\n  the completed divide into EX/MEM can clock the last quotient digit group.\n  This supplies seven arithmetic edges for the existing six wait clocks.\n  Seed from the dividend's top two bits and prepare 3D, 5D and 7D on the\n  first edge. On each of the next six edges, take a two-bit radix-4 digit\n  followed by a three-bit radix-8 digit. The final edge is the EX transfer.\n  This covers all 32 bits with ten parallel trial subtractors across the\n  two stages, down from fourteen. Take the signed result directly from\n  the registered quotient or remainder into MEM/WB alongside the product.\n  Ordinary EX results then bypass the divide-result mux.\n  \n  A scratch prototype completed ten iterations in 3,234,996 bracketed cycles\n  with all five CRCs correct. Three Gowin placements reached 65.678, 68.274\n  and 68.274 MHz, giving 211.05 iterations/s. The median rose 4.29% over\n  the matched 65.467 MHz champion. Acceptance requires over 68.478 MHz at\n  that cycle count. The median placement was limited by the replay-table\n  to register-file to data-address path.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 4\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/alu.sv\n    description: |\n      Use three 34-bit trial subtractions for the radix-4 digit and seven\n      35-bit trials for the radix-8 digit, retaining explicit borrow bits.\n      Advance the final group exactly once on a valid transfer. Hold the\n      completed state while MEM waits to advance. The ALTOPS branch uses\n      the same issue and transfer timing. Keep signed truncation, zero\n      divisor and INT_MIN divided by -1 behavior for all four operations.\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Send the divider result on a dedicated wire to MEM. Remove division\n      from the EX/MEM integer-result mux and retain the saved issue operands\n      for RVFI. EX transfers the division metadata on the last group edge.\n  - file: rtl/id_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Mark all eight validated M operations as late results. The existing\n      interlock gives a dependent successor the MEM/WB value and excludes\n      the placeholder EX/MEM result from forwarding and address prediction.\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: Connect the divider result sideband from EX to MEM.\n  - file: rtl/mem_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Select the completed quotient or remainder for a resident divide,\n      the multiplier output for a product and the EX result otherwise.\n      Sample the older divide on the MEM transfer edge even if a younger\n      divide issues on that edge. Preserve held data and tags on backpressure.\n  - file: test/test_alu.py\n    description: |\n      Check the six wait clocks, the final group on the consume edge and\n      the signed result after that edge. Exercise delayed consume, reset,\n      back-to-back requests and all existing arithmetic reference vectors.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Check result timing for independent and dependent divides, a stalled\n      store following a divide, and two successive divides with different\n      signs. Assert exact RVFI values, retirement order and bus handshakes.\nYAML\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
