{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_72", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"cat > cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261120-001-r7s0.yaml <<'YAML'\nid: hyp-20261120-001-r7s0\ntitle: Select forwarding tags in decode and complete all M results through MEM/WB\ncategory: micro_opt\nmotivation: |\n  The champion's median Gowin placement reports a 20.569 ns arrival from\n  triple_q through divider feedback and 20.547 ns from the WB destination\n  tag through forwarding to EX/MEM.alu_result. Division currently needs\n  both a deep recurrence and an extra choice on the ordinary EX result\n  mux. CoreMark executes zero DIV/REM instructions between its markers,\n  but pays the clock cost on every instruction.\nhypothesis: |\n  Retime the M-extension result bypass. Forward DIV/REM from MEM/WB like\n  MUL, and compare consumer source tags with both potential producers one\n  stage earlier. Moving the divide result past EX/MEM frees the EX transfer\n  edge for arithmetic. Seed with two dividend bits on the first wait clock\n  while registering 3D, 5D and 7D. Take five bits on each of the next five\n  wait clocks and on the transfer edge. Within each five-bit group take a\n  radix-4 digit first and a radix-8 digit second. This covers all 32 bits\n  with ten trial subtractors across the two stages, down from fourteen.\n  MEM selects and sign-corrects the registered quotient or remainder.\n\n  In parallel with decoding each consumer, register the younger rd match\n  and the older writer match for each source. In EX, qualify the younger\n  match with the actual EX/MEM write enable and late-result flag, then give\n  it priority. This retains trap suppression and takes both five-bit tag\n  comparisons off the path through the EX operand and result muxes.\n\n  A scratch build reached 74.626, 76.825 and 76.825 MHz across the three\n  Gowin placements. The matched champion reproduced its 65.467 MHz median.\n  Moving division to MEM/WB with the same five-bit groups and the original\n  tag comparisons reached 68.274 MHz. At 3,234,996 cycles for ten iterations,\n  the full prototype's median gives 237.48 iterations/s, a 17.35% gain over\n  202.37. Acceptance requires over 68.478 MHz at that cycle count.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 17\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/alu.sv\n    description: |\n      Implement three 34-bit trial subtractions followed by seven 35-bit\n      trials with explicit borrow outputs. Use the existing six-clock\n      counter. Advance the last group exactly once on div_valid and\n      div_consume, leaving quotient, remainder and sign available to MEM.\n      Preserve divide-by-zero, signed truncation and overflow semantics.\n      ALTOPS supplies its captured substitute to the same MEM/WB path.\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Send the signed divider result to MEM on a dedicated wire. Remove\n      it from the integer EX/MEM result mux. Save operands on divide issue\n      for RVFI and transfer instruction metadata on the last group edge.\n  - file: rtl/id_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Mark all eight legal M operations as late results. Apply the existing\n      late-use interlock to an immediately dependent divide consumer and\n      exclude its placeholder EX/MEM value from address prediction.\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: |\n      On each ID advance capture four match bits. For each incoming rs\n      compare against ID/EX.rd with rd nonzero, and against EX/MEM.rd with\n      rd nonzero and reg_write set. Hold matches when the consumer holds.\n      In EX gate the younger match with EX/MEM.reg_write and \"'!late_result,\n      and use the older match only when the younger source does not forward.\n      Preserve the same-cycle register-file write bypass. Connect the\n      divider result sideband to MEM.\n  - file: rtl/mem_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Select the divider sideband for a resident DIV/REM, the existing\n      product for MUL variants and the EX result otherwise. An older divide\n      transfers its completed value to MEM/WB even if the next independent\n      divide issues on that edge. A held MEM slot keeps the value available.\n  - file: test/test_alu.py\n    description: |\n      Check the six wait clocks, final arithmetic edge on consume, and exact\n      signed result after that edge. Retain every arithmetic vector, reset\n      check and delayed-consume case. Verify that the completed result holds\n      through delayed writeback and that back-to-back operations stay ordered.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Check the new MEM/WB dependency bubble and exact transfer counts in\n      the existing stalled divide chain. Exercise younger-writer priority,\n      an older WB match during a data stall, and a suppressed destination\n      on a trapping instruction with a matching rd. Check RVFI source\n      values, retirement order, and store data on acceptance.\nYAML\npython - <<'\"'PY'\nimport yaml,json,jsonschema\nfrom pathlib import Path\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261120-001-r7s0.yaml')\nd=yaml.safe_load(p.read_text())\njsonschema.validate(d,json.loads(Path('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json').read_text()))\nassert p.stem == d['id'] and d['category']=='micro_opt'\nassert all((Path('cores/bench')/row['file']).is_file() for row in d['changes'])\nprint('Hypothesis schema and paths valid')\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
