{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_15", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfor l in open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl'):\n d=json.loads(l)\n if d['id'].endswith(('r9s0','r6s1','r7s0','r9s2')):print(d['id'], d['critical_path'], d['hypothesis']['hypothesis'], d['implementation_notes'][-6000:])\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261120-002-r6s1 {'from': 'cpu/u_if/successors_successors_0_0_s', 'to': 'dmem_0_dmem_0_0_0_s', 'slack_ns': -11.065, 'arrival_ns': 21.36, 'logic_cells': 14, 'modules': ['clock_ibuf', 'cpu/u_if', 'cpu/u_rf', 'cpu/u_id']} Generate the effective address while a live or replayed memory instruction\nenters ID/EX. Drive the data port from that register in EX. At the\naccepting edge, clock the extended load value and the integer ALU result\ninto separate EX/MEM fields. Select between the registered values at\nthe bypass source. This takes the load mux off the ALU register input\nand lets the next instruction consume a load, including a branch issued\nfrom replay. Only the admitted EX slot owns the data port. A replay\naddress mismatch must squash both bus strobes before the edge.\n\nBypass available base producers into address generation. For an immediately\npreceding ADD, ADDI or AUIPC, fold its two operands and the displacement\nwith a carry-save compressor and one final adder. An accepted aligned LW\ncan supply a pointer directly from the bus. Retry an instruction whose\nyoungest base producer is unavailable. Preserve a single registered\nbyte address on dmem across backpressure.\n\nA scratch prototype completed ten iterations in 3,235,202 bracketed\ncycles, saving 251,707 cycles against the champion with both stall\ngenerators enabled. The load-use bubbles disappeared. Only 848 extra\nbubbles were solely due to unavailable address bases. Unheld cycles\nwithout a fetch word fell from 81,698 to 26,658. The prototype passed\nthe canonical CRC and UART checks.\nThe prototype that selected load data at the ALU register input reached\n60.666 MHz median. Separate EX/MEM result registers reached 61.640,\n64.712 and 64.712 MHz across Gowin placement options 0, 1 and 2 at the\nsame cycle count. The latter median gives 200.02 iterations/s, 13.30\npercent above 176.55. At this cycle count, 59.75 MHz clears acceptance.\nGowin reported 4,134 logic cells and 930 registers for the prototype.\nAll four short ELF traces matched the reference with and withou\n…[4024 of 7096 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:f978f984d0c8481f]…\nly successor B\nto launch that row's synchronous read. While B executes, copy the complete\nreturned row into a replay register. While C executes, that register can\nsupply D on a denied fetch. A ready fetch always supplies the live word.\nThis adds a clock boundary between the RAM read and the ID address logic\nwhile retaining one issue per cycle on a warm stable path.\n\nPair the training key with consecutive admitted instructions. Invalidate\nbroken chains across bubbles, retries or annuls. Freeze the read and replay\npipeline together across EX holds. Carry row validity and read/write\ncollision handling through the extra register. On a denied fetch keep the\nexisting full-PC checks against fall-through and aligned redirect target.\nA mismatch cancels every EX side effect and retries the resolved PC. Flush\npending reads and training on reset or FENCE.I. The tradeoff is reduced\nreplay coverage after unpredictable branches. With a 1% cycle penalty the\nmedian must exceed 85.66 MHz to clear the 4.6% acceptance margin.\n \n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
