{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_40", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfor l in open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl'):\n d=json.loads(l)\n if 'r6' in d['id']: print(d['id'],d['hypothesis']['hypothesis'])\nPY\ntail -3 \\\"\"'$TMPDIR/r10/caplate/p1/gw.log\"\nls -l \"$TMPDIR/r10/caplate/p1/impl/pnr/core_bench.tr\"'", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261120-001-r6s0 Compute the full byte address as a load or store enters ID/EX and drive\ndmem directly from that register during EX. Capture the formatted load\ninto EX/MEM on acceptance. An immediately dependent branch can then\nresolve from a registered operand and issue its successor in one cycle.\n\nGive the decode-stage address adder two candidate bases. The ordinary\ncandidate selects the youngest available EX/MEM result or the write-first\nregister file. If the immediately preceding EX instruction produces the\nbase, bypass only a LUI immediate or the raw word of an aligned LW.\nCalculate each candidate plus the sign-extended displacement in parallel.\nRetry consumers of other pending bases when their producer advances. This\nkeeps the arithmetic operands of the preceding EX instruction off the\npath to the address register. In particular, an ADD followed by a load\nusing its result as the base costs one bubble.\n\nA replayed instruction may have entered ID/EX provisionally. Qualify\nevery early request and data wait with the resident slot's verified\ncontrols and alignment. A rejected prediction must retry the resolved\nPC without issuing a store, reading MMIO or blocking EX on dmemReady.\nKeep address, store data and replay verification aligned across waits.\n\nA scratch prototype took 3,320,238 bracketed cycles with both stall\ngenerators, saving 166,671 cycles. All five CRCs and the UART validation\nbanner matched. Gowin placement options 0, 1 and 2 reached 64.787,\n67.200 and 67.200 MHz. The median gives 202.40 iterations/s, 14.64%\nabove the champion. All three placements cleared 184.67 iterations/s.\nRemoving the three-input arithmetic bypass added 85,036 cycles relative\nto a matched prototype and raised median Fmax from 63.294 to 67.200 MHz.\nThe prototype used 3,898 logic cells in placement option 0. The self-test\nand three random programs matched the reference with and without stalls.\nDirected tests observed consecutive retirements for LW, a dependent LW\nand its taken branch, and zero MMIO reads from a rejected repla\n…[3513 of 6585 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:b34871ba8160c710]…\n 60.066, 61.400 and 61.400 MHz.\nThe median gives 189.80 iterations/s, 7.50% above 176.55. At that cycle\ncount, acceptance requires a median above 59.7411 MHz. All 53 standard\nsingle-channel formal checks passed. The selftest and three random ELF\ntraces matched the reference with and without stalls.\nA directed test observed one accepted store after three denied cycles\nat a repaired address and consecutive retirement of a load and its branch.\n\nA matched control with full-width address adders also issued memory\noperations in EX. It took 3,235,018 cycles at 64.020 MHz. Early load\navailability accounts for the cycle gain over the champion. These\nplacements favor full-width adders for Fmax, so they do not establish\na separate frequency gain from predicting the page.\n\nGenerate file \"/srv/hwebench/tmp/3c491641/a047c98a/r10/caplate/p1/impl/pnr/core_bench.tr\" completed\nFri Nov 20 11:08:38 2026\n\n-rw-rw----+ 1 hwebench hwebench 229329 Nov 20 11:08 /srv/hwebench/tmp/3c491641/a047c98a/r10/caplate/p1/impl/pnr/core_bench.tr\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
