{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_41", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"date -u; python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfor l in open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl'):\n d=json.loads(l)\n if d['id'].endswith('r8s2'): print(d['changes'])\nPY\nhead -130 cores/bench/test/test_alu.py\"", "aggregated_output": "Fri Nov 20 10:10:31 UTC 2026\n[{'file': 'rtl/alu.sv', 'description': \"Replace the two serial digit stages with the verified reciprocal\\nquotient predictor and its six-digit large-divisor path. Latch\\nmagnitudes on issue. On wait clock 1 read the reciprocal ROM. On\\nclock 2 multiply low by low and high by high (p00 and p11). On clock\\n3 cross the reciprocal halves to compute p01 and p10 in the same\\ntwo multipliers. On clock 4 register the 18-bit sum of p00[31:16],\\np01[15:0] and p10[15:0] alongside p11+p01[31:16]+p10[31:16].\\nOn clock 5 add the first sum's top two bits to the second sum to get\\nq0. On clock 6 register q0+1 and the nine-bit residual\\n(A[8:0] - q0[8:0]*D[7:0]) modulo 512. Hold the estimate and its\\noperands during backpressure and latch the corrected result on consume.\\nUse 36-bit unsigned large-divisor trials with a separate borrow bit.\\nDerive mode from the saved divisor and capture sign and operation\\non issue. Apply RV32M divide-by-zero and signed overflow rules. Advance\\nthe last digit once on consume and keep the MEM result stable. Route\\nthe existing ALTOPS result through the same handshake.\\n\"}, {'file': 'test/test_alu.py', 'description': 'Exercise every predicted divisor at exact multiples and their\\nneighbors, including large dividends and cases needing correction.\\nCover 255/256/257, the maximum large-path quotient, all four RV32M\\noperations, reset and delayed consume. In the final-group test use\\nsigned REM of -0x12345 by 256. After the six wait clocks expect\\n-0x34 throughout the hold, then -0x45 after consume. Repeated\\nconsume must leave the completed result stable.\\n'}]\n\"\"\"Check combinational ALU operations and the six-clock RV32M divider.\"\"\"\nfrom __future__ import annotations\n\nimport random\n\nimport cocotb\nfrom cocotb.triggers import Timer\n\nfrom _helpers import (\n    ALU_ADD, ALU_SUB, ALU_AND, ALU_OR, ALU_XOR,\n    ALU_SLT, ALU_SLTU, ALU_SLL, ALU_SRL, ALU_SRA,\n    ALU_LUI,\n    ALU_DIV, ALU_DIVU, ALU_REM, ALU_REMU,\n    run_cocotb,\n)\n\nMASK32 = 0xFFFFFFFF\n\n\nasync def _tick(dut):\n    dut\n…[2078 of 5150 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:656f6b46a17c6918]…\nce(op, a, b):\n    a, b = a & MASK32, b & MASK32\n    if op in (ALU_DIV, ALU_REM):\n        if a >> 31:\n            a -= 1 << 32\n        if b >> 31:\n            b -= 1 << 32\n    if b:\n        q = abs(a) // abs(b)\n        if (a < 0) != (b < 0):\n            q = -q\n        r = a - q * b\n    else:\n        q, r = -1, a\n    return r if op in (ALU_REM, ALU_REMU) else q\n\n\n@cocotb.test()\nasync def add(dut):\n    await _setup(dut)\n    await _check(dut, ALU_ADD, 5, 3, 8)\n    await _check(dut, ALU_ADD, 0xFFFFFFFF, 1, 0)  # wrap\n\n\n@cocotb.test()\nasync def sub(dut):\n    await _setup(dut)\n    await _check(dut, ALU_SUB, 10, 3, 7)\n    await _check(dut, ALU_SUB, 0, 1, 0xFFFFFFFF)  # borrow\n\n\n@cocotb.test()\nasync def bitwise(dut):\n    await _setup(dut)\n    await _check(dut, ALU_AND, 0xFF, 0x0F, 0x0F)\n    await _check(dut, ALU_OR,  0xF0, 0x0F, 0xFF)\n    await _check(dut, ALU_XOR, 0xFF, 0x0F, 0xF0)\n\n\n@cocotb.test()\nasync def slt_signed(dut):\n    await _setup(dut)\n    await _check(dut, ALU_SLT, 0xFFFFFFFF, 0, 1)              # -1 < 0\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_42","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfor l in open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl'):\n d=json.loads(l)\n if d['id'].endswith('r8s1'): print(d['hypothesis'])\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_42", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfor l in open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl'):\n d=json.loads(l)\n if d['id'].endswith('r8s1'): print(d['hypothesis'])\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "{'motivation': \"Gowin traces the champion's 74-cell critical path from quotient_q to remainder_q through two serial digit selections. CoreMark issues zero DIV/REM between the markers, so shortening this path benefits the entire timed loop. The fixed retirement bound matters even for these infrequent instructions. A nine-clock radix-16 prototype failed liveness when a FENCE.I bubble preceded a divide and a younger store needed address repair.\\n\", 'hypothesis': 'Build a fixed-latency divider from a synchronous reciprocal ROM and a registered multiplier used twice. For dividend magnitude A and nonzero divisor magnitude D, normalize V = D << s into [2^31, 2^32). Use i = V >> 20 and x = the low 20 bits of V. A 2048-by-45-bit ROM returns the intercept C = floor(2^42/i) and slope S = ceil(2^34/i²). Round the residual up to t = ceil(x/32768). A six-by-thirteen-bit product gives R = C - ((t*S) << 3). This makes the interpolation term an integer and removes the fractional carry from its subtraction. The shared unsigned multiplier first forms A*R. Extract q0 by shifting this full product right by 62-s, then reuse the multiplier to form P = q0*D.\\n\\nR is a lower bound on 2^62/V because the reciprocal curve is convex and rounding the slope and residual upward makes the subtracted term larger. For 4096 <= D < 131072, s >= 15, so x is already a multiple of 32768. The curvature and coefficient rounding errors total less than 770 reciprocal units and A/2^(62-s) is below 1/2048. For D >= 131072 residual rounding adds at most 32768 units and the scale is below 1/65536. For D < 4096 the residual is zero, C has less than one unit of error, and D=1 is exact. Thus q0 is either the exact quotient or one short. Register A-D with its borrow early. On consume select q0+1 and A-D-P if A >= D and P <= A-D, otherwise q0 and A-P. Sign-correct the registered final magnitude for MEM.\\n\\nAn isolated prototype using a thirteen-bit residual coefficient t = ceil(x/256) passed the real-arithmetic divider tests and completed ten CoreMark iterations in 3,234,996 bracketed cycles with the five canonical CRCs. Gowin placement options 0, 1 and 2 reached 74.365, 76.550 and 76.550 MHz. The median yields 236.63 iterations/s, a 4.28% improvement. Acceptance requires more than 237.36 iterations/s or about 76.79 MHz at this cycle count. The six-bit interpolation is an unmeasured refinement targeting 78 MHz through lower logic and routing demand. The prototype timing limit runs from the successor instruction RAM through decode into the data-memory address.\\n', 'expected_impact': {'fitness_delta_pct': 6, 'confidence': 'low'}, 'changes': [{'file': 'rtl/alu.sv', 'description': 'Capture magnitudes and signs at issue. Across the six wait edges perform byte normalization, fine normalization with synchronous ROM lookup, reciprocal interpolation, the quotient-estimate product, quotient extraction, and the correction product. Pack 32-bit C and 13-bit S into each ROM row, generated with 64-bit constant arithmetic in eight banks of 256 indices. Compute t from bits [19:15] plus a sticky OR of [14:0]. Retain all six bits, including t=32. Zero-extend the product before shifting left three bits and subtracting it from C. Load the shared multiplier operand registers on waits three and five. Let the other pipeline registers capture every clock from the held operands. Commit only on div_valid and div_consume. Preserve reset cancellation, delayed consume, a held MEM result, divide-by-zero, signed overflow and the six-wait ALTOPS protocol.\\n'}, {'file': 'test/test_alu.py', 'description': 'Retain the exact issue-to-valid latency, delayed consume, reset, back-to-back and integer-reference assertions. Check the committed result stays stable until the next consume, including a held valid before consume. Exercise exact and one-short estimates with kD-1, kD and kD+1, the 4095/4096 and 131071/131072 boundaries, normalized bin edges, zero divisors and mixed signs. Compare every quotient and remainder with exact integer arithmetic.\\n'}]}\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
