{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_16", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfor r in map(json.loads,open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl')):\n if r['id'].endswith(('r6s0','r6s1','r10s2','r5s0','r11s0')):\n  print(r['id'],r['hypothesis']['hypothesis'])\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261120-001-r5s0 Compute the full byte address as a memory instruction enters ID/EX. Drive\nthe data bus from this register during EX. On acceptance, latch the raw\nread word for RVFI and the formatted load value in EX/MEM for ordinary EX\nforwarding. Stores use the forwarded rs2 in EX. A stalled request holds\nits address, data, enables and producer operands through the accepting edge.\n\nTo handle a newly produced base, compare the incoming rs1 against the\nyoungest writers. For ADD, ADDI and AUIPC in EX, their operands A and B\nare already available. With displacement D, compute P = A XOR B, then\n(P XOR D) + (((A AND B) OR (P AND D)) << 1) modulo 2^32. This gives\nA+B+D with one carry-propagate adder. Compute it in parallel with the normal\nbase-plus-displacement path. A preceding LUI supplies its immediate and an\naligned, accepted LW can supply the raw bus word. Use the EX/MEM value for\nolder available writers and the write-first register file otherwise.\nInterlock unavailable bases, including pending products. Suppress x0 and\ntrapped writes. Keep the multiply-use interlock for ordinary EX consumers.\n\nA scratch prototype completed ten iterations in 3,967,605 bracketed cycles\nversus 4,171,092 for the champion, with all canonical CRCs. Gowin placement\noptions 0, 1 and 2 reached 61.552, 66.658 and 66.658 MHz respectively.\nThe median gives 168.01 iterations/s, 9.44% above the champion. The\nprototype passed all 53 standard single-channel formal checks. The selftest\nand three random programs matched the reference with and without bus stalls.\n\nhyp-20261120-001-r6s0 Compute the full byte address as a load or store enters ID/EX and drive\ndmem directly from that register during EX. Capture the formatted load\ninto EX/MEM on acceptance. An immediately dependent branch can then\nresolve from a registered operand and issue its successor in one cycle.\n\nGive the decode-stage address adder two candidate bases. The ordinary\ncandidate selects the youngest available EX/MEM result or the write-first\nregister file. If the immediately pre\n…[5061 of 8133 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:423ce2620b02c22f]…\n, select the\ncurrent EX/MEM writer's complete MEM result or the write-first register\nfile value and latch it in the existing ID/EX operand register. Compute\nthis fallback even when the current EX instruction names the same rd.\nAt the next edge a legal younger producer has its result in EX/MEM.\nEach EX operand then needs one two-input mux between that result and the\ncaptured fallback. A younger writer suppressed by a trap uses the fallback.\nThis removes WB data and older-source priority from both the ALU and\nbranch paths while keeping the one-instruction integer dependency loop\nthrough the existing EX/MEM result register.\n\nUse MEM's existing integer/product/divide selection so a dependent\ninstruction can capture an M result on its producer's MEM-to-WB edge.\nThe timing risk is the extra data mux after the MEM multiplier. Check that\npath alongside the ALU and branch endpoints in Gowin. The design should\nretain 3,234,996 bracketed cycles. At that count a median above 89.26 MHz\nclears the 4.6% acceptance margin.\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
