{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_60","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"cat >cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261120-002-r12s1.yaml <<'YAML'\nid: hyp-20261120-002-r12s1\ntitle: \\\"Decouple EX memory completion from the external data port\\\"\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  A denied data request currently holds IF, ID/EX and both forwarding\n  sources. The round-seven load replay experiment saved 132,870 of\n  3,234,996 cycles, which gives 4.28% more throughput at equal Fmax.\n  Stores still waited in EX for external acceptance. A separate registered\n  transaction slot can let EX advance while the port finishes an older store.\nhypothesis: |\n  Split the load/store unit into an in-order CPU port and an external\n  port with a one-entry RAM store buffer. On a denied aligned RAM store,\n  capture its address, replicated data and byte enables. EX can advance\n  as soon as the buffer owns the store. Drive that transaction until\n  accepted. An accepting edge can replace it with a younger RAM store.\n  An independent ALU instruction or a locally satisfied load can proceed\n  while the external port is occupied.\n\n  Give the CPU port a 512-row synchronous RAM indexed by word address.\n  Start the lookup from the ID address candidate and repeat it on address\n  repair. A row holds the upper address tag, data and a valid bit per byte.\n  An external read fills the raw word. Taking ownership of a store updates\n  the row with its byte enables and lane data on that same edge. A partial\n  store can invalidate the neighboring lanes. Suppress collisions, clear\n  validity after reset, and confirm the exact RAM word and all requested\n  lanes before accepting local data. A miss waits for any older buffered\n  write and then uses the external bus. MMIO bypasses local service and\n  waits for older stores to drain. Serialize fences with buffered stores.\n\n  Keep the external address in a dedicated register. While a store waits,\n  hold it independently of the address of the current EX instruction. On\n  drain, load the replacement store address, the resident waiting address,\n  or the next ID address according to which instruction owns the port.\n  This register is important for Gowin to infer the synchronous address\n  input of the board wrapper's data RAM.\n\n  In a scratch simulation this organization completed ten iterations in\n  3,051,410 bracketed cycles with both stall generators, all five CRCs and\n  the validation banner. That saves 183,586 cycles and requires a median\n  above 84.19 MHz for acceptance. The main timing risk is the local hit\n  decision feeding the pipeline enables.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 6\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/local_mem_port.sv\n    description: |\n      Implement the buffered external transaction, registered bus address,\n      arbitration, and tagged byte-valid RAM. Hold denied requests stable\n      and permit simultaneous store drain and replacement. Report CPU-side\n      completion independently of external ready.\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: |\n      Connect checked EX requests to the CPU port. Feed the selected read\n      word to both load formatting and dependent LW address generation.\n      Use CPU-side completion for the EX interlock. Drain for fences and\n      order MMIO and termination after pending stores.\n  - file: rtl/id_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Export the incoming address candidate and repaired address for the\n      synchronous lookup. Advance the row with its ID/EX instruction and\n      hold it with that instruction throughout a denied request.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Check store drain and replacement, partial stores followed by loads\n      of overlapping widths, alias collisions, repair, and back-to-back\n      MMIO markers under extended denials. Scoreboard accepted external\n      writes and RVFI effects, including the absence of wrong-path writes.\nYAML\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
