{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_3", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"rg '\\\"category\\\"\\\\s*:\\\\s*\\\"structural\\\"' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl | cut -c1-460\"", "aggregated_output": "{\"id\": \"hyp-20261120-002-r1s1\", \"title\": \"Move DIV and REM off the single-cycle execute path\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The baseline retires ten CoreMark iterations in 4,491,485 cycles at a median\\n5.442 MHz. Gowin reports a 199.837 ns path through forwarding and the EX ALU.\\nThe ALU selects full-width combinational quotient and remainder results onto\\nthe same bus that feeds the data address. These operators are the strongest\\ncandidate fo\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20261120-002-r2s1\", \"title\": \"Resolve six quotient bits with two radix-8 divider stages\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The champion's worst Gowin path runs from divisor_q to remainder_q through\\nsix dependent trial subtractions. Eight trials per clock reached 31.094 MHz\\nand six reached 41.649 MHz. CoreMark executed zero DIV or REM instructions\\nin its 4,491,485 bracketed cycles, so the divider limits the clock even\\nwhen it contrib\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20261120-002-r3s1\", \"title\": \"Execute multiplies in MEM from registered, forwarded operands\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The champion's two 46.838 MHz Gowin placements were multiplier-limited.\\nEX currently puts register forwarding, multiplication and result selection\\nin one cycle. EX/MEM already captures both forwarded source values, and\\na multiply leaves the data bus idle in MEM. In the reference trace, only\\n35,640 of 93,960 \n{\"id\": \"hyp-20261120-002-r4s1\", \"title\": \"Issue resolved branch targets through a one-entry fetch buffer\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The champion spends 4,527,760 cycles on ten CoreMark iterations. A taken\\nbranch spends one fetch opportunity on fall-through even though EX knows\\nthe target that cycle. A backend hold can also discard a ready instruction\\nand force decode to wait for the bus a second time.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"Th\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20261120-002-r5s1\", \"title\": \"Issue fall-through instructions from a replay window during fetch stalls\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The champion resolves a branch in time to issu\n…[2921 of 5993 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:4ed14c2b36331be7]…\ne fetch PC.\\nThe round-ten experiment that captured both bypass ages during issue\\ncreated a path fro\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20261120-002-r12s1\", \"title\": \"Pipeline the load/store unit through a synchronous RAM lookup in EX\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The champion takes 3,234,996 cycles at 85.328 MHz. Its ID stage calculates\\nfive candidate addresses so an EX load can forward to the next instruction.\\nBoth round-eleven forwarding experiments reported paths from successor RAM\\nthrough ID to data memory. A scratch design with a registered external\\nreque\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20261120-002-r13s1\", \"title\": \"Carry both upper sums across EX/MEM for exact integer forwarding\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The 85.328 MHz champion takes 3,234,996 cycles for ten iterations. Gowin traced a path from older_rs1_q through EX to bit 23 of the EX/MEM result. The same forwarded operands feed a second full-width ADD in ID to form the page of an immediately dependent memory access. Round 12 kept the whole carry-select op\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
