{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_15", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfor l in open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl'):\n d=json.loads(l)\n if d['id'].endswith(('r10s0','r10s1','r12s0','r11s0','r11s1')):\n  print(d['id'],d['hypothesis']['hypothesis'][:2500])\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261120-001-r10s0 Move the bypass data mux across the ID/EX boundary. For each incoming\nsource, compute three mutually exclusive enables during decode. The\nyoungest eligible producer supplies the outgoing EX result, including\nformatted load data on an accepting edge and PC+4 for a legal jump.\nAn older integer writer supplies its registered EX/MEM value. Otherwise\ncapture the write-first register-file value. If the owning older writer\nis an M instruction, latch one override bit for that source instead.\nCapture the selected 32-bit value directly in the existing ID/EX source\nregister. Each EX source now has a single two-input mux between this\ncaptured operand and registered MEM/WB data, selected directly by its\nM override flop. This removes the three-way priority path and terminates\nthe multiplier at MEM/WB. An immediately dependent ADD still issues\nevery clock. The timing tradeoff is the return path from the outgoing\nALU result to the next operand register. Compute source ownership in\nparallel with the result so only the data selection follows arithmetic.\nQualify the youngest writer with its actual trap-checked write enable.\nA denied load holds issue until acceptance. An M producer retains its\ncurrent interlock and then forwards from MEM/WB through the override. A held consumer\nkeeps its captured operands through repair or data-bus backpressure.\nAt 3,234,996 cycles for ten iterations the acceptance target is a\nthree-placement median above 89.25 MHz.\n\nhyp-20261120-002-r10s1 Retime operand selection across ID/EX. Form the final youngest,\nolder and local grants for each source while its instruction is in ID.\nFor the ALU, group the two forwarded values on one arm of a mux and\nthe local register value and PC or immediate on the other. Capture the\ngrants alongside the consumer. This keeps the branch-selected PC out\nof the D input of a combined local operand register.\n\nA scratch implementation of that EX routing reached 85.338, 85.574\nand 85.574 MHz across the three placements. It retained 3,234,996\nbracketed cycles \n…[3579 of 6651 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:e6840a04a6279e7c]…\n carry. Form the three boundary carries with a\nbalanced group lookahead. Select each upper byte's sum for carry-in\nzero or one with its exact boundary carry. Apply the same construction\nto subtraction with complemented B and initial carry one. Supply ID's\nADD-produced base page from the same exact EX sum. This keeps a second\nfull-width carry chain off the dependent load/store address path.\n\nUse the equivalent borrow lookahead for comparisons. In parallel each\nbyte computes unsigned less-than and equality. The most significant\nunequal byte determines the result. Signed compares override that result\nwhen operand signs differ. Use these predicates for branch resolution\non forwarded rs1/rs2 and for SLT/SLTU on their actual ALU inputs.\nThe network computes the exact result in the original EX cycle, so every\nready successor can still issue and the expected bracket is 3,234,996\ncycles. At that count acceptance requires a median above 89.25 MHz.\nThe principal timing risk is extra routing for the upper-byte selects.\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
