{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_70", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport yaml\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261120-002-r14s1.yaml')\ns=\\\"\\\"\\\"id: hyp-20261120-002-r14s1\ntitle: Steer integer execution to EX or MEM according to operand availability\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  The champion runs ten iterations in 3,234,996 cycles at 85.328 MHz.\n  Its older-source bypass feeds both the integer ALU and the branch that\n  selects the next fetch address. Timing experiments that shorten just one\n  path expose the other. In a scratch design that moved dependent shifts\n  to MEM, the older rs2 select through the branch to ID/EX.pc limited the\n  median to 75.986 MHz. Duplicating fetch and decode for the two branch\n  outcomes also put a late selection on the data-address path.\nhypothesis: |\n  Give a single issued instruction an early or late execution time. In ID,\n  prepare operands from the write-first register file, PC, immediate and\n  forwardable integer EX/MEM result. For each actual source, the youngest\n  in-flight writer owns the register. If both inputs are available, latch\n  the final operands. An early scalar ALU instruction or conditional branch\n  then executes in EX directly from two registers. If an input is still\n  owned by the current EX instruction or a late MEM result, capture its\n  exact forwarded value in EX and execute the operation in MEM. Carry a\n  fast/late bit with the instruction. Apply the current late-use interlock\n  to consumers of late integer writers. A dependent memory base uses the\n  existing exact address repair when an early ADD base is unavailable.\n  The ALU producing an early ADD and the ID add-base candidate both use\n  the registered early operands.\n\n  Apply the same boundary to a conditional branch or JALR awaiting an\n  operand. While it transfers from EX to MEM, launch one predicted successor\n  using the existing tagged successor PC, with fall-through as the default.\n  Admit at most one provisional instruction. In MEM compute the predicate\n  or indirect target from the registered, fully forwarded operands. Compare\n  the predicted PC with the architectural successor, including the linear\n  successor on a misalignment trap. Validate the younger EX slot before any\n  request, divide issue, write, redirect or retirement. On a mismatch,\n  annul it, save the exact corrected PC and retry. A correctly predicted\n  late branch incurs no mandatory bubble. Keep the local-operand branch\n  path exact in EX and direct jumps on their existing path. Only aligned,\n  in-range PCs may drive a speculative fetch. Retain full-PC validation\n  and FENCE.I invalidation for instruction replay.\n\n  A scratch scalar-only split completed the canonical CoreMark run in\n  3,323,883 bracketed cycles with both stalls, all five CRCs and the\n  validation banner. This measures the cost of late integer results.\n  Deferring operand-dependent control transfers is the proposed timing\n  change. It cuts the forwarded-data-to-compare-to-successor path while\n  keeping the common ALU recurrence off those same buses. At 3.35 million\n  cycles the three-placement median must exceed 92.43 MHz for acceptance.\n  A median of 95 MHz at that count would yield 284 iterations/s. Measure\n  deferred control transfers, wrong successors and address repairs between\n  markers. The principal risk is a new critical path from MEM validation\n  to a younger bus strobe or from the register file to ID address formation.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 7\n  confidence: low\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/core_pkg.sv\n    description: \\\"Carry prepared scalar operands and their readiness, late-operation class, forwarded inputs and the assumed successor across the appropriate registers.\\\"\n  - file: rtl/forward_unit.sv\n    description: \\\"Expose both source ownership decisions to ID. Exclude x0 and distinguish an available older integer result from an outstanding M or late scalar result.\\\"\n  - file: rtl/id_stage.sv\n    description: \\\"Bind each used operand to a value or mark it late. Capture ready operands, ignoring unused instruction fields for scheduling, and retain the true raw source tags for RVFI.\\\"\n  - file: rtl/alu.sv\n    description: \\\"Factor the existing combinational scalar operations into an integer unit shared as RTL by the early and late instances. Keep standalone ALU behavior and both M arithmetic modes.\\\"\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: \\\"Execute ready scalar and control operations from registered operands. For late instructions capture fully forwarded sources, the decoded operation, link PC and proposed successor in EX/MEM.\\\"\n  - file: rtl/mem_stage.sv\n    description: \\\"Execute late scalar results and resolve late branches and JALR. Supply the precise successor, trap and link-write qualification, and compare the provisional successor independently of the scalar result bus.\\\"\n  - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n    description: \\\"Interlock a dependent late integer result. Give an older correction priority over younger data holds and division, and retain precise forwarding through a denied request.\\\"\n  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n    description: \\\"Use the saved successor PC for a deferred control transfer, qualify the fetched word and recover from the registered correction. Train only verified successor words and retain the predecessor across recovery.\\\"\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: \\\"Wire the early and late execution times into one in-order stream. Qualify every younger effect with older validation. Preserve the single retirement channel, exact RVFI operand data and consecutive order.\\\"\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: \\\"Exercise a load feeding a branch that changes direction, back-to-back late branches, and a forwarded JALR with a misaligned target. Check every retirement and accepted store while both buses stall and a wrong-path store or divider occupies EX.\\\"\n\\\"\\\"\\\"\nassert yaml.safe_load(s)['id']==p.stem\np.write_text(s)\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
