{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_76", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261120-002-r15s1.yaml'", "aggregated_output": "id: hyp-20261120-002-r15s1\ntitle: \"Write integer results into the register file on EX transfer\"\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  An integer result spends two clocks in the forwarding network before WB\n  writes the champion's register file. Gowin has traced limiting paths from\n  the older-source select through the ALU and from WB through the branch\n  comparison to the fetch PC. The round-11 split bypass moved the older\n  value selection into ID, where the RF-to-address path became critical.\n  Writing the file earlier can remove the older bypass at both consumers.\nhypothesis: |\n  Move the integer register-file write port to the edge where a valid\n  instruction leaves EX. Write the ALU result, aligned jump link, or\n  formatted load data on its accepting edge. Decode reads the distributed\n  RAM directly. By the time the next-but-one instruction is in ID, the\n  result is already in the array. For the instruction entering EX on the\n  write edge, register the write data and the two nonzero destination\n  matches alongside its captured operands. Each EX source then needs one\n  two-input mux. The registered data supplies deterministic same-edge RAW\n  forwarding independently of the array's read-during-write behavior.\n\n  Give M results the same physical write port and bypass register. An M\n  instruction transfers its operands to MEM as today. Reserve the following\n  EX slot as a bubble so MEM can write its multiply or divide result on\n  the MEM-to-WB edge. Admit its successor on that edge, including an\n  immediately dependent instruction. Apply the reservation once per M\n  transfer, including for an independent successor. The existing interlock\n  already charges many dependent pairs one bubble. This arrangement avoids\n  simultaneous RAM writes and lets a string of WAW dependencies use the\n  same last-write rule as an integer chain.\n\n  ID address generation reads older integer bases directly from the array.\n  Keep the existing EX ADD/LUI/LW base candidates for the simultaneous\n  younger writer. If the single write is an \n…[1451 of 4523 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:122387e1886de63f]…\n the same result into MEM/WB for retirement, preserving real and ALTOPS behavior.\"\n  - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n    description: \"Reserve one empty EX slot immediately after each successful M transfer. Retry the successor and admit it on the M write edge. Preserve divider progress and priority of existing holds.\"\n  - file: rtl/id_stage.sv\n    description: \"Remove the older MEM address candidate. Use the array for older bases, keep supported EX base candidates and mark a matching M write for exact repair.\"\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: \"Route the mutually exclusive integer and M writes to the file and bypass register. Keep WB data for RVFI. Qualify annulled, trapping, stalled and x0 destinations at the early write point.\"\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: \"Check back-to-back RAW and WAW pairs across both write times, a denied load feeding a branch, an M result used as a store base, and a trapping JALR that names a live rd. Compare retirement operands and each accepted bus transaction.\"\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
