{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_4", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'cat formal/checks-deep.cfg && cat cores/bench/test/test_alu.py'", "aggregated_output": "[options]\nisa rv32im\nnret 1\nsolver bitwuzla\n\n[depth]\n# BMC depth per check. 2nd column (where present) is the k-induction prove\n# depth. Keep depths matched to the current pipeline so bugs within ~20\n# cycles of any symbolic instruction stream are exposed.\ninsn          20\nreg       10  20\npc_fwd    10  20\npc_bwd    10  20\ncausal    10  20\n# unique   — no two retirements share rvfi_order (catches double-retire)\n# cover    — sanity that the CPU retires (prevents vacuous-pass suites)\n# ill      — illegal instructions must trap; rvfi_trap from decoder.is_illegal\n# liveness — CPU makes forward progress under any symbolic stream\nunique     1 10 20\ncover      1    20\nill             20\nliveness   1 10 20\n\n[defines]\n`define RISCV_FORMAL_ALIGNED_MEM\n# RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS — fast mode. Substitutes M-ext mul/div/rem with\n# simpler XOR-of-add/sub formulas so bitwuzla solves them inside the\n# 20-step BMC budget (rvfi.rst §7.6 \"Alternative arithmetic operations\").\n#\n# IMPORTANT — what this DOES and DOES NOT prove:\n#   ✓ proves: M-ext bypassing, operand routing (rs1/rs2 -> unit -> rd),\n#             stall/forwarding behavior on mul/div, all I-base arithmetic\n#             and trap discipline.\n#   ✗ does NOT prove: the actual MUL/DIV/REM arithmetic is correct.\n#     The DUT and the spec both implement the same XOR substitute under\n#     this define, so a wrong real multiplier can still pass formal here.\n#\n# M-extension arithmetic correctness is covered separately by:\n#   1. test/test_alu.py — 30+ vectors per M-ext op, exact bit results.\n#   2. cosim against test/cosim/reference.py — every retired MUL/DIV/REM\n#      diff'd field-by-field.\n#   3. `make formal-deep` — same checks without ALTOPS. SLOW (~hours per\n#      M-ext check) but proves real arithmetic. Run periodically, not in\n#      the per-iteration orchestrator loop.\n\n[cover]\n# Sanity cover: the CPU must retire at least one non-trapping instruction.\n# A vacuous suite (nothing ever retires) would fail this — no symbolic\n# trace can make rvfi_valid && !rvfi_trap go true.\nal\n…[6408 of 9480 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:c415473d8c0ee810]…\n\n    await _setup(dut)\n    await _check_div(dut, ALU_REMU, 7, 3, 1)\n\n\n@cocotb.test()\nasync def remu_by_zero(dut):\n    await _setup(dut)\n    await _check_div(dut, ALU_REMU, 0xCAFEBABE, 0, 0xCAFEBABE)\n\n\n@cocotb.test()\nasync def back_to_back_divides(dut):\n    \"\"\"Verify the FSM resets cleanly between consecutive iterative\n    divides — no carry-over of operands or state from the previous op.\n    \"\"\"\n    await _setup(dut)\n    # Real divides only (avoid edge-case short-circuit) so each pass\n    # exercises the full 33-cycle FSM.\n    await _check_div(dut, ALU_DIVU, 100, 7, 14)\n    await _check_div(dut, ALU_DIVU, 0xFFFFFFFF, 2, 0x7FFFFFFF)\n    await _check_div(dut, ALU_DIV, 0xFFFFFFFC, 2, 0xFFFFFFFE)  # -4/2 = -2\n    await _check_div(dut, ALU_REM, 10, 3, 1)\n    await _check_div(dut, ALU_REMU, 7, 3, 1)\n\n\ndef test_alu_runner():\n    \"\"\"pytest entry — runs every @cocotb.test() above under Verilator.\"\"\"\n    run_cocotb(toplevel=\"alu\",\n               sources=[\"core_pkg.sv\", \"alu.sv\"],\n               test_module=\"test_alu\")\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
