{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_2", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"rg -n -i 'structural|divider|division' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl | cut -c1-2300\"", "aggregated_output": "2:{\"id\": \"hyp-20261120-001-r1s0\", \"title\": \"Compute eight quotient bits per clock in a shared DIV/REM unit\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The baseline's 199.837 ns path crosses operand forwarding and the EX ALU.\\nThe ALU computes signed and unsigned division and remainder combinationally.\\nA full-width divide is a plausible source of the 156-cell path. Its delay\\nlimits the clock even when executing other instructions. A reference-ISS run\\nof the canonical ten-iteration binary retired 2,922,015 instructions between\\nthe markers, including 93,960 multiplies and zero divides or remainders.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The baseline's 199.837 ns path crosses operand forwarding and the EX ALU.\\nThe ALU computes signed and unsigned division and remainder combinationally.\\nA full-width divide is a plausible source of the 156-cell path. Its delay\\nlimits the clock even when executing other instructions. A reference-ISS run\\nof the canonical ten-iteration binary retired 2,922,015 instructions between\\nthe markers, including 93,960 multiplies and zero divides or remainders.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Replace the four DIV/REM operators with one unsigned restoring divider.\\nCapture the forwarded operand magnitudes on launch, then reuse eight\\nshift-and-trial-subtract steps over four clocks to produce the 32 quotient\\nbits and the remainder. Register the partial result after each pass. Apply\\nthe RV32M result sign at completion. Only DIV/REM occupy EX for extra clocks.\\nThe instruction count predicts zero divider stalls in the timed region.\\nAt the baseline's 4,491,485 bracketed cycles, 5.70 MHz would clear the 4.6%\\nacceptance threshold and 8.17 MHz would deliver the projected 50% gain.\\nCompare both the cycle count and median Gowin Fmax to test this prediction.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 50, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Add launch, completion and consume signals for DIV, DIVU, REM and REMU.\\nUse the trial subtraction's borrow to choose the next partial\n…[1530 of 4602 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:d9b17d8cbd21c8d5]…\ninstructions. The run produced all five expected CRCs.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Give EX one shared, fixed-latency restoring divider. At issue, register the\\nmagnitudes of the forwarded operands and the signs needed for the result.\\nProcess six quotient bits per clock for six clocks using a 36-bit dividend\\npadded with four leading zeros. Each bit uses the borrow from a trial\\nsubtraction to select the next remainder. After the sixth clock, select and\\nsign-correct the quotient or remainder for the ordinary EX/MEM result slot.\\nThis bounds the divider feedback path to six subtract/select steps and\\npreserves single-cycle EX for integer ALU operations and all multiplies.\\nOnly a valid divide or remainder holds younger instructions. Older operations\\ndrain, and the completed result uses the existing forwarding and retirement\\npath. The instruction mix predicts zero divider stalls in the measured\\nloop. At the baseline cycle count, a median Fmax of 8.17 MHz would deliver\\nthe projected 50% gain. Check both median Fmax\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
