{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_1", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"cat cores/bench/LESSONS.md && rg -n 'structural' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl | cut -c1-1800\"", "aggregated_output": "- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-001-r1s0 (regression, +471.20%): Eight serial trial subtractions on the `partial_q` feedback path limited median Fmax to 31.094 MHz versus 41.649 MHz for six steps per clock, while CoreMark executed no DIV/REM between markers to benefit from the shorter divide latency.\n- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-002-r1s1 (improvement, +665.10%): Moving combinational `/` and `%` off the shared EX result path into a six-cycle restoring divider raised median Fmax from 5.442 to 41.649 MHz without adding divider stalls because CoreMark issued no DIV/REM between its markers.\n- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-003-r1s2 (regression, +6.19%): Predicting branches by displacement bias and taking JAL in fetch saved 302,818 CoreMark cycles by avoiding EX redirects, while the combinational DIV/REM path still limited median Fmax to 5.392 MHz.\n- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-001-r2s0 (regression, +10.91%): Replacing six serial trials with three radix-4 stages that test divisor multiples in parallel raised median Fmax from 41.649 to 46.194 MHz, exposing the path from the register file through hazard logic to ID/EX as the new timing limit.\n- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-002-r2s1 (improvement, +12.46%): Registering 3D, 5D and 7D let two radix-8 stages replace six serial trial subtractions, raising median Fmax from 41.649 to 46.838 MHz at the same 4,491,485 CoreMark cycles, with the single-cycle multiplier limiting both median-frequency placements.\n- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-003-r2s2 (regression, +10.77%): Predicting branches and JAL in fetch avoided EX redirects and saved 302,818 CoreMark cycles, but the six-step divider’s `remainder_q` feedback path limited median Fmax to 43.026 MHz, leaving throughput below the radix-8 core (102.72 vs 104.28 iterations/s).\n3:{\"id\": \"hyp-20261120-002-r1s1\", \"title\": \"Move DIV and REM off the single-cycle execute path\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The baseline retires ten CoreMark iterations in 4,491,485 cycles at a median\\n5.442 MHz. Gowin reports a 199.837 ns path through forwarding and the EX AL\n…[2287 of 5359 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:213d1376c960a8cd]…\no the divider limits the clock even\\nwhen it contributes no stalls.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Keep the six iteration clocks and change the quotient-digit datapath.\\nOn the first clock after issue, divide the dividend's top two bits by the\\nregistered divisor magnitude D. Only D values 1, 2 and 3 can yield a\\nnonzero digit for a nonzero divisor. In parallel, register 3D, 5D and 7D\\nusing shifts and adds or subtracts. The other multiples are wires or shifts.\\nOn each of the next five clocks, compute two radix-8 digits in series.\\nEach digit appends three dividend bits to the remainder and tries the seven\\nnonzero divisor multiples in parallel. Borrow bits select the largest\\nmultiple that fits and its already-computed difference. This produces all\\n32 quotient bits with two dependent subtract/select levels per feedback\\ncycle. The existing EX issue, result transfer and retirement schedule apply.\\n\\nPredict the same bracketed cycle count and a median Fmax near 50 MHz,\\ngiving about 111 CoreMark iterations/s. At that c\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
