{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_43","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c 'cat formal/wrapper_si.sv | head -170'","aggregated_output":"// riscv-formal wrapper for single-issue (`nret 1`) cores. Same shape and\n// memory model as wrapper.sv, but instantiates `core` with only the\n// channel-0 RVFI port set — no `_1` ports. The single-issue path produces\n// only ch0 checks; checks_si.cfg sets `nret 1` so genchecks does not\n// emit any `_ch1` tasks (no PREUNSAT-as-pass tally needed). Selected by\n// the orchestrator when cores/<target>/core.yaml declares nret: 1.\n`include \"rvfi_macros.vh\"\n\nmodule rvfi_wrapper (\n    input clock,\n    input reset,\n    `RVFI_OUTPUTS\n);\n    // Solver picks any 32-bit instruction each cycle.\n    (* keep *) `rvformal_rand_reg [31:0] imem_data;\n\n    // 8 KiB dmem (2048 words).\n    reg [31:0] dmem [0:2047];\n\n    wire [31:0] imem_addr;\n    wire [31:0] dmem_addr;\n    wire [31:0] dmem_wdata;\n    wire [3:0]  dmem_wen;\n    wire        dmem_ren;\n\n    always @(posedge clock) begin\n        if (dmem_wen[0]) dmem[dmem_addr[12:2]][7:0]   <= dmem_wdata[7:0];\n        if (dmem_wen[1]) dmem[dmem_addr[12:2]][15:8]  <= dmem_wdata[15:8];\n        if (dmem_wen[2]) dmem[dmem_addr[12:2]][23:16] <= dmem_wdata[23:16];\n        if (dmem_wen[3]) dmem[dmem_addr[12:2]][31:24] <= dmem_wdata[31:24];\n    end\n\n    core uut (\n        .clock              (clock),\n        .reset              (reset),\n        .io_imemAddr        (imem_addr),\n        .io_imemData        (imem_data),\n        .io_imemReady       (1'b1),\n        .io_dmemAddr        (dmem_addr),\n        .io_dmemWData       (dmem_wdata),\n        .io_dmemRData       (dmem[dmem_addr[12:2]]),\n        .io_dmemWEn         (dmem_wen),\n        .io_dmemREn         (dmem_ren),\n        .io_dmemReady       (1'b1),\n        // Channel 0 — the only retirement channel. With nret=1 in\n        // checks_si.cfg, every rvfi_* signal here is sized for a single\n        // channel, so the core's `_0` ports connect directly without\n        // slicing. No `_1` ports exist on the core's IO under nret=1.\n        .io_rvfi_valid_0    (rvfi_valid),\n        .io_rvfi_order_0    (rvfi_order),\n        .io_rvfi_insn_0     (rvfi_insn),\n        .io_rvfi_trap_0     (rvfi_trap),\n        .io_rvfi_halt_0     (rvfi_halt),\n        .io_rvfi_intr_0     (rvfi_intr),\n        .io_rvfi_mode_0     (rvfi_mode),\n        .io_rvfi_ixl_0      (rvfi_ixl),\n        .io_rvfi_rs1_addr_0 (rvfi_rs1_addr),\n        .io_rvfi_rs1_rdata_0(rvfi_rs1_rdata),\n        .io_rvfi_rs2_addr_0 (rvfi_rs2_addr),\n        .io_rvfi_rs2_rdata_0(rvfi_rs2_rdata),\n        .io_rvfi_rd_addr_0  (rvfi_rd_addr),\n        .io_rvfi_rd_wdata_0 (rvfi_rd_wdata),\n        .io_rvfi_pc_rdata_0 (rvfi_pc_rdata),\n        .io_rvfi_pc_wdata_0 (rvfi_pc_wdata),\n        .io_rvfi_mem_addr_0 (rvfi_mem_addr),\n        .io_rvfi_mem_rmask_0(rvfi_mem_rmask),\n        .io_rvfi_mem_wmask_0(rvfi_mem_wmask),\n        .io_rvfi_mem_rdata_0(rvfi_mem_rdata),\n        .io_rvfi_mem_wdata_0(rvfi_mem_wdata)\n    );\n\nendmodule\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_44","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c 'cat .gitignore | tail -70'","aggregated_output":"cores/*/experiments/hypotheses/*.yml\ncores/*/experiments/hypotheses/*.yaml\n\n# Editor / OS\n.DS_Store\n*.swp\n.idea/\n.vscode/\n\n# Claude Code workspace\n.claude/\ndocs/assets/\n\n# Local git worktrees (subagent-driven dev; not the orchestrator's experiments/worktrees)\n.worktrees/\n\n# Node deps for bench-fence pi extension (installed locally for typecheck;\n# pi handles installs at run time so the deps don't need to be committed).\nnode_modules/\n\n# Pi-coding-agent session storage inside per-rep clones.\n.pi-sessions/\n\n# Per-rep agent telemetry inside bench/ is now tracked: log.jsonl carries\n# the full per-iteration data (LUT4, Fmax, IPC, error class) that the\n# rolled-up summary.json doesn't surface, and agent.log is the model's\n# verbatim transcript — both are essential for reproducibility and\n# postmortems. Combined they're ~3-4 MB per rep × 9 reps = ~35 MB; cheap.\n# (Previously gitignored as \"regenerable\", but they're only regenerable\n# by re-running the bench at considerable wall time and cost.)\n\n# repo.bundle (each rep's full git history, task-7 disk hygiene) is NOT\n# tracked, unlike orchestrator.log: a bundle runs about 45 MB binary per\n# rep, and at full compute-matrix scale that would bloat the git history\n# by gigabytes. It stays on disk as a local forensics artifact instead,\n# and tools/bench/gc.py archives (or recreates) it before deleting the\n# clone it was bundled from, so the content is not lost.\nbench/**/repo.bundle\n\n# agent.full.log.gz: the verbatim agent transcript. The tracked agent.log\n# next to it has tool-output fields capped (tools/bench/transcript.py),\n# which cut the published transcripts ~60%; the full stream stays on\n# disk for forensics, same policy as repo.bundle.\nbench/**/agent.full.log.gz\n\n# env.json (per-rep environment snapshot: PATH + resolved EDA tool paths)\n# IS tracked, decided at the first v2 rep publish (e1a static, 2026-07-16):\n# it is tiny, contains no secrets, and pins which toolchain binaries\n# produced each rep's numbers.\n\n# Throwaway harness-verification runs (one-model YAMLs + their output\n# trees). These are reliability checks, not benchmark data — output is\n# regenerable and the YAMLs only encode \"rerun this single model to\n# confirm a fix landed\". Naming convention: `*-verify` for output dirs,\n# `models-*-verify.yaml` for the throwaway model YAML.\nbench/*-verify/\nbench/smoke-*/\ntools/bench/models-*-verify.yaml\n\n# Aborted/partial bench output preserved locally with a debug-tag suffix\n# (e.g. `gpt-5_5.failed-pwd-bug/`, `gpt-5_5.validate-v3/`, sibling\n# `results.jsonl.<tag>`). They document a known-broken state for the\n# author's own postmortem; the canonical results live in the un-suffixed\n# per-model dirs.\nbench/*.failed-*\nbench/*.validate-*\nbench/*.prev-*\nbench/*.stale-*\nbench/*.partial-*\nbench/results.jsonl.*\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_45","type":"agent_message","text":"A scratch build gave the load/store unit its own address adder. That removed the reported memory path, but median Fmax rose only to 64.642 MHz, below the 66.98 MHz needed at the champion’s cycle count. I’ll test whether changing how operands enter EX produces a larger gain."}}
