{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_97", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"cat > cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261120-002-r8s1.yaml <<'YAML'\nid: hyp-20261120-002-r8s1\ntitle: \\\"Replace divider feedback with reciprocal interpolation and one exact correction\\\"\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  Gowin traces the champion's 74-cell critical path from quotient_q to remainder_q through two serial digit selections. CoreMark issues zero DIV/REM between the markers, so shortening this path benefits the entire timed loop. The fixed retirement bound matters even for these infrequent instructions. A nine-clock radix-16 prototype failed liveness when a FENCE.I bubble preceded a divide and a younger store needed address repair.\nhypothesis: |\n  Build a fixed-latency divider from a synchronous reciprocal ROM and a registered multiplier used twice. For dividend magnitude A and nonzero divisor magnitude D, normalize V = D << s into [2\"'^31, 2''^32). Use i = V >> 20 and x = the low 20 bits of V. A 2048-by-45-bit ROM returns the intercept C = floor(2''^42/i) and slope S = ceil(2''^34/i²). Round the residual up to t = ceil(x/256). A 13-by-13-bit interpolation product gives R = C - ceil(t*S/16). The shared unsigned multiplier first forms A*R. Extract q0 by shifting this full product right by 62-s, then reuse the multiplier to form P = q0*D.\n  \n  R is a lower bound on 2''^62/V because the reciprocal curve is convex and rounding the slope and residual upward makes the subtracted term larger. For D >= 4096 the curvature and rounding errors together are below 1030 reciprocal units, and A/2''^(62-s) is below 1/2048. For D < 4096 the residual is zero, C has less than one unit of error, and D=1 is exact. Thus q0 is either the exact quotient or one short. Register A-D with its borrow early. On consume select q0+1 and A-D-P if A >= D and P <= A-D, otherwise q0 and A-P. Sign-correct the registered final magnitude for MEM.\n  \n  An isolated prototype passed the real-arithmetic divider tests and completed ten CoreMark iterations in 3,234,996 bracketed cycles with the five canonical CRCs. Gowin placement options 0, 1 and 2 reached 74.365, 76.550 and 76.550 MHz. The median yields 236.63 iterations/s, a 4.28% improvement. Acceptance requires more than 237.36 iterations/s or about 76.79 MHz at this cycle count. The remaining timing limit runs from the successor instruction RAM through decode into the data-memory address.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 4\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/alu.sv\n    description: |\n      Capture magnitudes and signs at issue. Across the six wait edges perform byte normalization, fine normalization with synchronous ROM lookup, reciprocal interpolation, the quotient-estimate product, quotient extraction, and the correction product. Pack 32-bit C and 13-bit S into each ROM row, generated with 64-bit constant arithmetic in eight banks of 256 indices. Compute t from bits [19:8] and a sticky OR of [7:0]. Use ((C << 4) - t*S) >> 4 at sufficient width. Load the shared multiplier operand registers on waits three and five. Let the other pipeline registers capture every clock from the held operands. Commit only on div_valid and div_consume. Preserve reset cancellation, delayed consume, a held MEM result, divide-by-zero, signed overflow and the six-wait ALTOPS protocol.\n  - file: test/test_alu.py\n    description: |\n      Retain the exact issue-to-valid latency, delayed consume, reset, back-to-back and integer-reference assertions. Check the committed result stays stable until the next consume, including a held valid before consume. Exercise exact and one-short estimates with kD-1, kD and kD+1, the 4095/4096 boundary, normalized bin edges, zero divisors and mixed signs. Compare every quotient and remainder with exact integer arithmetic.\nYAML\npython - <<'\"'PY'\nimport json,yaml,jsonschema\nfrom pathlib import Path\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261120-002-r8s1.yaml')\nd=yaml.safe_load(p.read_text());jsonschema.validate(d,json.loads(Path('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json').read_text()))\nprint(d['id'], d['category'], 'valid', 76.550e6*10/3234996, 226.92*1.046)\nPY\ndate -u\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
