0261120-002-r3s1', 'title': 'Execute multiplies in MEM from registered, forwarded operands', 'category': 'structural', 'motivation': \"The champion's two 46.838 MHz Gowin placements were multiplier-limited.\\nEX currently puts register forwarding, multiplication and result selection\\nin one cycle. EX/MEM already captures both forwarded source values, and\\na multiply leaves the data bus idle in MEM. In the reference trace, only\\n35,640 \n…[2132 of 5204 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:6d4a461b564334be]…\nk of the execute stage. The champion's reported\\n75-cell path crosses two full-width radix-8 digit selections, although\\nCoreMark executes zero DIV/REM instructions between its markers. In an\\nisolated prototype, shortening that recurrence exposed a path from the\\nWB destination tag through forwarding and the ALU into EX/MEM. Median\\nFmax reached 67.435 MHz. Moving operand matches into ID by itself gave\\n64.393 MHz wit\n{'id': 'hyp-20261120-002-r8s1', 'title': 'Replace divider feedback with reciprocal interpolation and one exact correction', 'category': 'structural', 'motivation': \"Gowin traces the champion's 74-cell critical path from quotient_q to remainder_q through two serial digit selections. CoreMark issues zero DIV/REM between the markers, so shortening this path benefits the entire timed loop. The fixed retirement bound matters even for these infrequent instructions. A nine-clock radix-16 prototype failed liveness when a FENCE.I bubble preceded a divide and a younger store needed address repair.\\n\", '\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
