{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_6", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nr=json.loads(open(\\\"cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\\\").readlines()[-3])\nprint({k: str(v)[:12000] for k,v in r.items()})\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "{'id': 'hyp-20261120-001-r8s0', 'title': 'Share seven trial subtractors between radix-32 and radix-8 division', 'category': 'micro_opt', 'motivation': \"The champion's 74-cell path goes from quotient_q[31] to remainder_q[1]\\nthrough two dependent divider digit selections. Even the earlier split\\nat 2^17 kept two radix-8 steps on the narrow feedback path. CoreMark\\nexecutes zero DIV/REM between markers, so this path costs clock speed on\\nevery measured instruction. A single digit decision on each feedback\\nedge can improve Fmax within the existing retirement-latency bound.\\n\", 'hypothesis': '{\\'motivation\\': \"The champion\\'s 74-cell path goes from quotient_q[31] to remainder_q[1]\\\\nthrough two dependent divider digit selections. Even the earlier split\\\\nat 2^17 kept two radix-8 steps on the narrow feedback path. CoreMark\\\\nexecutes zero DIV/REM between markers, so this path costs clock speed on\\\\nevery measured instruction. A single digit decision on each feedback\\\\nedge can improve Fmax within the existing retirement-latency bound.\\\\n\", \\'hypothesis\\': \"Use one 32-bit remainder R and one 32-bit dividend/quotient shift\\\\nregister Q. For a nonzero divisor magnitude D below 2^14, R fits in 14\\\\nbits. Resolve the top two dividend bits during setup, then consume 30\\\\nbits in six radix-32 digits. For D >= 2^14, the high 14 dividend bits\\\\nalready form a legal remainder. Seed R with them and Q with the low\\\\n18 dividend bits followed by 14 zeros. Six radix-8 digits then give\\\\nthe complete quotient.\\\\n\\\\nShare the trials for every fourth radix-32 candidate. Write T8 as\\\\n8*R + Q[31:29] and T32 as 4*T8 + Q[28:27]. For j=1..7, T32 >= 4*j*D\\\\nexactly when T8 >= j*D. Seven 35-bit unsigned subtractors supply these\\\\nfit bits to both modes. When digit 4*j wins, its narrow residual is\\\\nthe wide difference with Q[28:27] appended, truncated to 14 bits.\\\\nThe other 24 narrow candidates need only 19-bit trials. Adjacent fit\\\\nflags select the largest fitting digit and its residual in each mode.\\\\nBoth banks read the shared registe\n…[4412 of 7484 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:0079fe86e906afb7]…\n10.44', 'vs_baseline': '1967.82', 'fmax_mhz': '81.075', 'ipc_coremark': '3e-06', 'cycles': '3234996', 'iterations': '10', 'lut4': '4443', 'ff': '939', 'seeds': '[77.777, 81.075, 81.075]', 'perturbations': '[[0, 77.777, 10], [1, 81.075, 11], [2, 81.075, 11]]', 'lutram': '32', 'bsram': '6', 'dsp': '5', 'critical_path': \"{'from': 'cpu/u_if/successors_successors_0_0_s', 'to': 'dmem_2_dmem_2_0_0_s', 'slack_ns': -7.857, 'arrival_ns': 18.152, 'logic_cells': 12, 'modules': ['clock_ibuf', 'cpu/u_if', 'cpu/u_hazard', 'cpu/u_id']}\", 'logic_levels': '10', 'formal_passed': 'True', 'cosim_passed': 'True', 'error': 'None', 'implementation_notes': '', 'timestamp': '2026-11-20T08:17:14.601115Z', 'slot': '0', 'round_id': '8', 'lesson': '- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-001-r8s0 (improvement, +10.44%): Splitting at divisor magnitude 2^14 raised Fmax by reducing divider feedback to one digit selection per clock, with the seven radix-8 trial subtractors supplying fit bits and residual prefixes for every fourth nonzero radix-32 digit.'}\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
