{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_67","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"cat > cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261120-002-r9s1.yaml <<'YAML'\nid: hyp-20261120-002-r9s1\ntitle: Give replayed instructions a registered rs1 read path\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  On the champion's 81.075 MHz placement, Gowin traces the worst path\n  from successor BSRAM output bit 15 through rs1 selection, register-file\n  write bypass and the address adder to the data-address register. The\n  RAM's clock-to-output delay alone is 2.260 ns. Another path with almost\n  identical slack runs from the replay target-match register through the\n  annul mask and the EX operand mux to the ALU result. Both paths arise\n  when issuing a replayed instruction.\nhypothesis: |\n  Partition the replay entry by when its fields are needed. Give the five\n  rs1 bits a 512-by-5 distributed RAM with an asynchronous read feeding\n  five flip-flops. Read it at the same lookahead index and on the same\n  edge as the synchronous successor BSRAM. The flip-flops supply rs1\n  early enough in the next cycle for the register read and address sum.\n  The BSRAM supplies the verified destination, valid bit and remaining\n  instruction bits. Both reads have the original one-cycle latency.\n\n  Limit replay annulment to the controls that authorize an operation.\n  A rejected entry can still select ALU inputs and compute a discarded\n  result while its invalid bit and cleared enables suppress every effect.\n  This takes the full-PC replay check off the arithmetic operand selects.\n\n  A scratch implementation ran ten iterations in 3,234,996 bracketed cycles\n  with both stall generators, the five expected CRCs and the validation\n  banner. Gowin placement options 0, 1 and 2 gave 75.641, 85.328 and\n  85.328 MHz. The median yields 263.77 iterations/s, 5.25% above 250.62.\n  Acceptance at this cycle count requires more than 84.805 MHz. The\n  margin is 0.523 MHz, so a fresh three-placement median matters.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 5\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Add the distributed rs1 array alongside successors. On write_enable\n      write train_insn_q[19:15] at write_index. On \"'!stall register its\n      read at read_index. Keep the original live/replay instruction mux\n      and source bits [19:15] from the live word or the registered rs1\n      field using the same imem_ready select. The unused five payload\n      bits in the block RAM can be removed by synthesis. Share the row\n      validity, read/write collision guard, reset and FENCE.I sweep.\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: |\n      Keep the default id_ex_w copy of id_ex_q. On annul clear valid\n      and the is_branch, is_jump, is_jalr, mem_read, mem_write, reg_write\n      and late_result bits individually. These suppress redirects, memory\n      strobes and repair, register writes, M issue and interlocks. Leave\n      alu_op, alu_src, is_auipc and the other payload fields available\n      to EX. Reuse the existing denied-fetch store and FENCE.I tests to\n      check that rejected payloads have no architectural effects.\nYAML\npython - <<'\"'PY'\nimport yaml,json,jsonschema\nfrom pathlib import Path\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261120-002-r9s1.yaml')\njsonschema.validate(yaml.safe_load(p.read_text()),json.loads(Path('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json').read_text()))\nprint('Schema valid')\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_67","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"cat > cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261120-002-r9s1.yaml <<'YAML'\nid: hyp-20261120-002-r9s1\ntitle: Give replayed instructions a registered rs1 read path\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  On the champion's 81.075 MHz placement, Gowin traces the worst path\n  from successor BSRAM output bit 15 through rs1 selection, register-file\n  write bypass and the address adder to the data-address register. The\n  RAM's clock-to-output delay alone is 2.260 ns. Another path with almost\n  identical slack runs from the replay target-match register through the\n  annul mask and the EX operand mux to the ALU result. Both paths arise\n  when issuing a replayed instruction.\nhypothesis: |\n  Partition the replay entry by when its fields are needed. Give the five\n  rs1 bits a 512-by-5 distributed RAM with an asynchronous read feeding\n  five flip-flops. Read it at the same lookahead index and on the same\n  edge as the synchronous successor BSRAM. The flip-flops supply rs1\n  early enough in the next cycle for the register read and address sum.\n  The BSRAM supplies the verified destination, valid bit and remaining\n  instruction bits. Both reads have the original one-cycle latency.\n\n  Limit replay annulment to the controls that authorize an operation.\n  A rejected entry can still select ALU inputs and compute a discarded\n  result while its invalid bit and cleared enables suppress every effect.\n  This takes the full-PC replay check off the arithmetic operand selects.\n\n  A scratch implementation ran ten iterations in 3,234,996 bracketed cycles\n  with both stall generators, the five expected CRCs and the validation\n  banner. Gowin placement options 0, 1 and 2 gave 75.641, 85.328 and\n  85.328 MHz. The median yields 263.77 iterations/s, 5.25% above 250.62.\n  Acceptance at this cycle count requires more than 84.805 MHz. The\n  margin is 0.523 MHz, so a fresh three-placement median matters.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 5\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Add the distributed rs1 array alongside successors. On write_enable\n      write train_insn_q[19:15] at write_index. On \"'!stall register its\n      read at read_index. Keep the original live/replay instruction mux\n      and source bits [19:15] from the live word or the registered rs1\n      field using the same imem_ready select. The unused five payload\n      bits in the block RAM can be removed by synthesis. Share the row\n      validity, read/write collision guard, reset and FENCE.I sweep.\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: |\n      Keep the default id_ex_w copy of id_ex_q. On annul clear valid\n      and the is_branch, is_jump, is_jalr, mem_read, mem_write, reg_write\n      and late_result bits individually. These suppress redirects, memory\n      strobes and repair, register writes, M issue and interlocks. Leave\n      alu_op, alu_src, is_auipc and the other payload fields available\n      to EX. Reuse the existing denied-fetch store and FENCE.I tests to\n      check that rejected payloads have no architectural effects.\nYAML\npython - <<'\"'PY'\nimport yaml,json,jsonschema\nfrom pathlib import Path\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261120-002-r9s1.yaml')\njsonschema.validate(yaml.safe_load(p.read_text()),json.loads(Path('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json').read_text()))\nprint('Schema valid')\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"Schema valid\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
