{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_9", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfor l in open(\\\"cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\\\"):\n d=json.loads(l)\n if d[\\\"id\\\"].endswith((\\\"r9s1\\\",\\\"r11s0\\\",\\\"r7s2\\\",\\\"r6s2\\\",\\\"r10s1\\\",\\\"r9s2\\\")):\n  print(d[\\\"id\\\"],d['seeds'],d['critical_path'],d['hypothesis']['hypothesis'], d['implementation_notes'][:2000])\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261120-003-r6s2 [62.112, 65.467, 65.467] {'from': 'cpu/u_ex/u_alu/triple_q_0_s0', 'to': 'cpu/u_ex/u_alu/remainder_q_1_s0', 'slack_ns': -11.1, 'arrival_ns': 21.394, 'logic_cells': 75, 'modules': ['clock_ibuf', 'cpu/u_ex/u_alu']} Predict that adding a load or store's signed 12-bit displacement preserves\nits base register's 4 KiB page. In decode, take bits [31:12] from the\nselected base and compute only (base + displacement) modulo 8192.\nConcatenate the page with the low 12 sum bits. Comparing sum bit 12 with\nbase bit 12 detects every crossing, including borrow and 32-bit wrap,\nbecause one displacement can cross at most one page. Register the\ncandidate address, the crossing bit and whether the base source was\navailable. This puts the check beside the memory instruction before it\ncan assert a bus strobe.\n\nAn in-page hit issues in EX from a single address register. A crossing\nor unavailable base costs one repair cycle. Suppress the request, hold\nthe instruction, and overwrite the same register with forwarded rs1\nplus the full signed displacement. The repaired instruction can issue\non the next cycle and holds its request through backpressure. An accepted\nload forwards from EX/MEM to the next instruction.\n\nA scratch implementation completed the ten timed iterations in 3,234,996\ncycles with both stall generators and all five canonical CRCs. Gowin\nplacement options 0, 1 and 2 reached 60.066, 61.400 and 61.400 MHz.\nThe median gives 189.80 iterations/s, 7.50% above 176.55. At that cycle\ncount, acceptance requires a median above 59.7411 MHz. All 53 standard\nsingle-channel formal checks passed. The selftest and three random ELF\ntraces matched the reference with and without stalls.\nA directed test observed one accepted store after three denied cycles\nat a repaired address and consecutive retirement of a load and its branch.\n\nA matched control with full-width address adders also issued memory\noperations in EX. It took 3,235,018 cycles at 64.020 MHz. Early load\navailability accounts for the cycle gain over the champion. Th\n…[7116 of 10188 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:114ad8e10ecda061]…\n select the\ncurrent EX/MEM writer's complete MEM result or the write-first register\nfile value and latch it in the existing ID/EX operand register. Compute\nthis fallback even when the current EX instruction names the same rd.\nAt the next edge a legal younger producer has its result in EX/MEM.\nEach EX operand then needs one two-input mux between that result and the\ncaptured fallback. A younger writer suppressed by a trap uses the fallback.\nThis removes WB data and older-source priority from both the ALU and\nbranch paths while keeping the one-instruction integer dependency loop\nthrough the existing EX/MEM result register.\n\nUse MEM's existing integer/product/divide selection so a dependent\ninstruction can capture an M result on its producer's MEM-to-WB edge.\nThe timing risk is the extra data mux after the MEM multiplier. Check that\npath alongside the ALU and branch endpoints in Gowin. The design should\nretain 3,234,996 bracketed cycles. At that count a median above 89.26 MHz\nclears the 4.6% acceptance margin.\n \n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
