{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_137","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"cat > cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261120-003-r12s2.yaml <<'YAML'\nid: hyp-20261120-003-r12s2\ntitle: Predict load replay rows independently for register-file and forwarded bases\ncategory: predictor\nmotivation: |\n  A scratch 512-word load replay table saved 145,252 of the champion's\n  3,234,996 bracketed cycles. It passed the five CoreMark CRCs, but its\n  80.119 MHz Gowin median yielded only 259.31 iterations/s. The worst path\n  on the median placement ran from successor instruction RAM through ID to\n  a data-memory address input. The single replay read port also consumes\n  that late address, after ID selects among five base sources.\nhypothesis: |\n  Give the verified load-word predictor two synchronous read ports by\n  replicating its 512 rows in block RAM. In ID, read one copy directly at\n  rf_sum[10:2] and the other at the word index formed from the MEM, ADD,\n  LUI and LW candidates. Exclude the register-file candidate from this\n  second index. Capture use_rf and both collision results with the reads.\n  This lets the register-file lookup launch as soon as its own sum is ready\n  and removes the replay RAM load from the final five-source address mux.\n  \n  In EX, verify each row's upper tag and recorded read index against the\n  checked effective address in parallel. Use the registered source choice\n  to qualify the appropriate match. Require ordinary RAM, natural alignment\n  and valid bytes for every requested lane. A hit supplies the raw load word\n  and suppresses the external read. A miss uses the normal bus handshake.\n  A verified LW hit can supply the youngest base for the next address. A\n  dependent memory operation after an LW miss uses the existing local repair.\n  \n  Broadcast identical updates to both copies. An accepted RAM read fills all\n  four lanes. An accepted store replaces the tag, data and byte-valid mask\n  with the bytes actually written. Clear validity after reset, hold reads\n  across stalls, and reject a same-index read/write collision independently\n  on each port. Use the selected raw word for both forwarding and RVFI.\n  \n  Both ports compute the same selected index as the single-port prototype,\n  so the target is its measured 3,089,744 cycles. Acceptance at that count\n  requires a median above 85.2471 MHz. The second port is the unmeasured\n  change. Its timing benefit depends on shortening the ID address path enough\n  to cover the extra selection between registered RAM outputs in EX.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 5\n  confidence: low\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/id_stage.sv\n    description: Expose independent RF and forwarded word indices alongside the existing architectural address candidate.\n  - file: rtl/load_replay.sv\n    description: Replicate the coherent table, register source and collision metadata, and verify the selected row against the resolved load address and lane mask.\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: Connect verified replay to the load result and dependent LW address bypass. Send misses and stores through the existing bus interface.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: Exercise both read ports under denied reads, partial stores, index aliases and repair. Check every retired load value and accepted store.\nYAML\npython3 - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport yaml,json,jsonschema\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261120-003-r12s2.yaml')\nd=yaml.safe_load(p.read_text());jsonschema.validate(d,json.loads(Path('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json').read_text()));assert p.stem==d['id'];print('Schema valid')\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
