{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_1", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"rg -n 'predictor|successor|replay' cores/bench/LESSONS.md\"", "aggregated_output": "13:- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-001-r5s0 (regression, +9.73%): Registering effective addresses in ID/EX let loads issue in EX and forward from EX/MEM to the next dependent branch without a bubble, raising CoreMark throughput by 9.73% versus 15.0% for instruction replay on denied fetches.\n14:- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-002-r5s1 (regression, +8.45%): The 128-word replay window saved 444,281 cycles over ten CoreMark iterations by supplying fall-through instructions on denied fetches, but its `!redirect` check left taken loop backedges dependent on `io_imemReady`.\n15:- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-003-r5s2 (improvement, +15.00%): Replaying the successor instruction from a table indexed by predecessor PC kept CoreMark issuing across taken loop backedges with `io_imemReady` low, while parallel checks of the saved successor PC against `fallthrough_pc` and `redirect_target` let EX annul mismatches.\n21:- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-003-r7s2 (regression, +6.03%): Reading a 512-row table in ID let EX replay denied RAM loads when the tag matched and all requested byte lanes were valid, saving 132,870 cycles over ten CoreMark iterations, too few to offset the higher Fmax of the retimed divider and forwarding design (214.58 vs 226.92 iterations/s).\n23:- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-002-r8s1 (regression, +5.53%): With reciprocal interpolation and one exact correction replacing serial digit selection, the path from successor instruction RAM through decode to the data-memory address limited median Fmax to 77.471 MHz versus 81.075 MHz for the radix-32/radix-8 divider at the same CoreMark cycle count.\n26:- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-002-r9s1 (improvement, +5.25%): Registering a distributed-RAM copy of replay `rs1` bypassed BSRAM delay on the path from `rs1` to the address adder, and annulling only validity and operation enables kept the replay PC check off the EX operand muxes, raising median Fmax from 81.075 to 85.328 MHz.\n27:- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-003-r9s2 (regression, +4.58%): Registering the two-successor RAM output before decode raised median Fmax from 81.075 to 85.931 MHz, but replay on a denied fetch now depended on predicting across two control-flow transitions, costing 43,391 cycles over ten CoreMark iterations and leaving the throughput gain just below the 4.6% acceptance margin.\n28:- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-001-r10s0 (regression, -6.00%): Capturing integer bypasses at issue preserved all 3,234,996 CoreMark cycles but put the path from `rs1_from_m_wb` through the EX ALU to the next `ID/EX.rs1_val` within 5 ps of the critical fetch replay path as median Fmax fell from 85.328 to 80.207 MHz.\n29:- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-002-r10s1 (regression, -1.71%): Computing live and replay addresses in parallel took `imem_ready` off the critical path, but the path from `rs1_grants.older` through EX to the successor PC in ID/EX limited median Fmax to 83.868 MHz at the same 3,234,996 CoreMark cycles.\n30:- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-003-r10s2 (regression, -2.10%): Predicting live branches and jumps from the last successor PC removed EX resolution from the fetch path, but misses forced retries even with `imem_ready` high, adding 41,750 cycles over ten CoreMark iterations while the path from `older_rs1_q` to the EX/MEM ALU result limited median Fmax to 84.612 MHz.\n31:- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-001-r11s0 (regression, -1.83%): Capturing the older MEM bypass in ID preserved 3,234,996 CoreMark cycles, but the path from successor RAM through the ID address adder to data RAM limited median Fmax to 83.766 MHz, with the EX/MEM branch operand path to `ID/EX.pc` within 2 ps.\n36:- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-003-r12s2 (regression, -11.15%): Independent replay reads for register-file and forwarded bases matched the single-port table's 3,089,744 CoreMark cycles, but a 16-level path from `MEM/WB.alu_result` through EX and ID to `EX/MEM.alu_result` limited median Fmax to 72.409 MHz versus 80.119 MHz with one port.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
