eedback path limited median Fmax to 31.094 MHz versus 41.649 MHz for six steps per clock, while CoreMark executed no DIV/REM between markers to benefit from the shorter divide latency.\n- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-002-r1s1 (improvement, +665.10%): Moving combinational `/` and `%` off the shared EX result path into a six-cycle restoring divider raised median Fmax from 5.442 to 41.649 MHz without adding divider stalls because CoreMark issued no DIV/REM between its markers.\n- 2026-11-2\n…[9687 of 12759 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:a4fa31bd03bf69dd]…\n77.287 MHz.\n- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-001-r12s0 (regression, -5.80%): Exact byte carry lookahead kept CoreMark at 3,234,996 cycles, but the path from `imem_ready` through register-file selection and the ID displacement adder to the data RAM address limited median Fmax to 80.378 MHz, with the MEM multiply bypass into `ID/EX.alu_b_default` only 6 ps shorter.\n- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-002-r12s1 (regression, -7.93%): With the synchronous RAM lookup in EX, 59,268 load-to-address interlocks and 52,157 refill-wait cycles limited CoreMark cycle savings to 1.92%, while the path from `fwd_rs1` through the EX ALU to EX/MEM limited median Fmax to 77.051 MHz versus 85.328 MHz for the champion.\n- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-003-r12s2 (regression, -11.15%): Independent replay reads for register-file and forwarded bases matched the single-port table's 3,089,744 CoreMark cycles, but a 16-level path from `MEM/WB.alu_result` through EX and ID to `EX/MEM.alu_result` limited median Fmax to 72.409 MHz versus 80.119 MHz with one port.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
