{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_10", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfor l in open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl'):\n d=json.loads(l)\n if d['id'].endswith(('r6s2','r7s2','r9s1','r9s2','r10s1','r10s2','r12s2','r11s1','r11s2','r12s0')):\n  print(d['id'],d['title'],d['motivation']+d['hypothesis']['hypothesis'])\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261120-003-r6s2 Predict same-page data addresses to forward loads from EX/MEM The champion already replays instructions across taken backedges. Ten\niterations took 3,486,909 cycles with both stalls and 3,353,265 with\ninstruction stalls disabled, a 4.0% throughput difference at equal Fmax.\nThe acceptance margin is 4.6%. A prior trace counted 135,320 loads\nimmediately followed by a branch with a matching source field. An\naddress predictor can make those load values available a cycle earlier.\nPredict that adding a load or store's signed 12-bit displacement preserves\nits base register's 4 KiB page. In decode, take bits [31:12] from the\nselected base and compute only (base + displacement) modulo 8192.\nConcatenate the page with the low 12 sum bits. Comparing sum bit 12 with\nbase bit 12 detects every crossing, including borrow and 32-bit wrap,\nbecause one displacement can cross at most one page. Register the\ncandidate address, the crossing bit and whether the base source was\navailable. This puts the check beside the memory instruction before it\ncan assert a bus strobe.\n\nAn in-page hit issues in EX from a single address register. A crossing\nor unavailable base costs one repair cycle. Suppress the request, hold\nthe instruction, and overwrite the same register with forwarded rs1\nplus the full signed displacement. The repaired instruction can issue\non the next cycle and holds its request through backpressure. An accepted\nload forwards from EX/MEM to the next instruction.\n\nA scratch implementation completed the ten timed iterations in 3,234,996\ncycles with both stall generators and all five canonical CRCs. Gowin\nplacement options 0, 1 and 2 reached 60.066, 61.400 and 61.400 MHz.\nThe median gives 189.80 iterations/s, 7.50% above 176.55. At that cycle\ncount, acceptance requires a median above 59.7411 MHz. All 53 standard\nsingle-channel formal checks passed. The selftest and three random ELF\ntraces matched the reference with and without stalls.\nA directed test observed one accepted store after three denied cycles\nat a rep\n…[16068 of 19140 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:b586901019d74bfb]…\nnt\nand valid bytes for every requested lane. A hit supplies the raw load word\nand suppresses the external read. A miss uses the normal bus handshake.\nA verified LW hit can supply the youngest base for the next address. A\ndependent memory operation after an LW miss uses the existing local repair.\n\nBroadcast identical updates to both copies. An accepted RAM read fills all\nfour lanes. An accepted store replaces the tag, data and byte-valid mask\nwith the bytes actually written. Clear validity after reset, hold reads\nacross stalls, and reject a same-index read/write collision independently\non each port. Use the selected raw word for both forwarding and RVFI.\n\nBoth ports compute the same selected index as the single-port prototype,\nso the target is its measured 3,089,744 cycles. Acceptance at that count\nrequires a median above 85.2471 MHz. The second port is the unmeasured\nchange. Its timing benefit depends on shortening the ID address path enough\nto cover the extra selection between registered RAM outputs in EX.\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
