{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_14", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfor l in open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl'):\n d=json.loads(l)\n if d['id'].endswith(('r12s1','r10s0','r5s2','r8s1')):\n  print(d['id'],d['hypothesis']['hypothesis'][:3600])\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261120-003-r5s2 Train a 512-entry table indexed by predecessor PC. Each row predicts the\nsuccessor PC and its instruction from the last ready external fetch.\nRead one cycle ahead using the likely next predecessor's word index.\nDirect JAL and backward branches select the direct target index.\nOther instructions select fall-through. This guess drives only the\ninternal RAM read port. EX always drives the resolved address on imem.\n\nOn a ready fetch, decode the live response. On a denied fetch, a valid\nprediction can enter ID/EX provisionally. Compare the saved destination\nagainst PC+4 and the full EX redirect target in parallel. Register both\nequality bits and the actual redirect decision. In the next cycle the\nselected equality bit admits the instruction to EX. A mismatch annuls\nthat slot and IF retries the resolved address already in retry_pc.\nThis verifies the address for indirect jumps and table aliases as well.\nVerification costs no extra cycle on a hit or a ready bus response.\n\nA scratch prototype completed ten iterations in 3,486,909 bracketed\ncycles with both stall generators, a 16.40% cycle reduction. All five\nCRCs and the UART validation banner matched. Gowin placement options\n0, 1 and 2 reached 59.777, 61.398 and 61.398 MHz. The median yields\n176.08 iterations/s, 14.70% above 153.52. At this cycle count the\nacceptance threshold requires more than 55.9935 MHz. The predictor\nadded two BSRAMs. The prototype passed all 53 standard single-channel\nformal checks. With both buses stalled, the four small ELF traces\nmatched the reference. A directed test retired 24 consecutive loop\ninstructions while the instruction bus denied every fetch, including\nthe taken backedges.\n\nhyp-20261120-002-r8s1 Build a fixed-latency divider from a synchronous reciprocal ROM and a registered multiplier used twice. For dividend magnitude A and nonzero divisor magnitude D, normalize V = D << s into [2^31, 2^32). Use i = V >> 20 and x = the low 20 bits of V. A 2048-by-45-bit ROM returns the intercept C = floor(2^42/i) and slope S = c\n…[4608 of 7680 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:621f330437edd04a]…\n buffered in order. Hold its pins until\nacceptance and allow drain and replacement on one edge. Update the local\nrow when ownership transfers. A partial store may invalidate neighboring\nlanes. Clear row validity on reset and hold the read result with its MEM\nslot across stalls. A same-index RAM read/write collision forces a miss,\nand a refill waits behind any older buffered store. Give each command exactly one\ntransfer. Bypass local service for MMIO, drain older writes at fences and\ntermination, and preserve alignment traps before issuing any command.\n\nThe scratch result measures the service mechanism. The EX lookup and\nremoval of ID address generation are the unmeasured timing changes. At\n3.10 million cycles, 85.54 MHz clears the tournament margin and 87 MHz\nyields about 281 iterations/s. At 3.14 million cycles the acceptance\nthreshold rises to 86.64 MHz. Check the delay of RAM-to-branch forwarding\npaths and count pointer interlocks and refill waits between\nthe markers. Either can erase the projected gain.\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
