{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_59", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261120-003-r13s2.yaml'", "aggregated_output": "id: hyp-20261120-003-r13s2\ntitle: Predict the load replay index from its predecessor PC\ncategory: predictor\nmotivation: |\n  A 512-word load replay prototype saved 145,252 of the champion's\n  3,234,996 cycles, but its 80.119 MHz median erased the gain. Both\n  the single-port and replicated versions needed a current base value\n  and displacement to address the replay RAM during decode. The fetch\n  unit already looks up a predicted predecessor PC one clock earlier.\nhypothesis: |\n  Use that lookahead to predict which ordinary RAM word a successor\n  load will access. For a sequence A, B, load C, read a predictor\n  with the likely word PC of B while A executes. While B executes,\n  the registered prediction launches one synchronous data-table read.\n  When C reaches EX the candidate word and its full physical word tag\n  are available beside C's existing checked address. A bad fetch or\n  stride prediction can only lose a replay opportunity. The live fetch\n  still uses the architectural successor resolved in EX.\n\n  Train the predictor under B's index when C completes. Keep the last\n  nine-bit data word index and a next-index prediction in each entry.\n  Two observations provide the modular word stride. Predict the last\n  index for a stationary address and extrapolate a repeating stride\n  for a stream. Compute the prediction on the training side. The\n  read path from the registered prediction to data RAM is wiring.\n\n  Maintain one coherent, physically indexed 512-word replay table.\n  An accepted RAM load fills the full word. An accepted store replaces\n  its row with the bytes actually written and their mask. Replay only\n  aligned, checked RAM loads whose full word address and requested\n  lanes match. On a miss use the normal load handshake. A hit supplies\n  the raw word for load formatting, forwarding and RVFI. A verified\n  word load may also supply the base for a dependent memory operation.\n  Measure the share of denied loads served, bracketed cycles and median\n  Gowin Fmax. At 3.10 million cycles, exceeding 85.54 MHz clears the\n  tournament margin. Irregular pointer loads are the main coverage risk.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 6\n  confidence: low\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n    description: Expose the existing nine-bit likely predecessor index and its lookup advance enable for the data index predictor.\n  - file: rtl/load_replay.sv\n    description: |\n      Add the predecessor-indexed stride predictor and single-read-port\n      coherent word table. Key writes to the data table by the actual\n      accepted byte address. Store the full RAM word address or compare\n      both the returned tag and captured read index. Invalidate replay\n      through reset and same-row read/write collisions. Hold the candidate\n      with its EX slot through stalls and invalidate it on address repair.\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: |\n      Carry the genuine predecessor key and matching prediction metadata\n      with an admitted successor. Train once per completed ordinary RAM\n      load, excluding invalid and annulled slots. Route accepted stores\n      and load fills to the table and a verified LW word to ID.\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Validate the replay against the checked address, natural alignment,\n      RAM range and byte mask. A hit completes locally and suppresses the\n      external read. A miss holds the normal request to acceptance. Use\n      the same selected raw word for the load result and RVFI.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Cover a repeated load and a fixed-stride loop under data stalls.\n      Force a wrong predicted index, a same-row store collision, partial\n      store overlap and an MMIO address with matching low bits. Check the\n      accepted bus transfers and retired values against the memory model.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
