{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_14", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfor r in map(json.loads,open(\\\"cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\\\")):\n if r['id'].endswith(('r9s2','r10s2','r12s2','r13s2')):\n  print(r['id']); print(r['hypothesis']['hypothesis'])\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261120-003-r9s2\nTrain each of the 512 predictor rows on a pair of successive control-flow\ntransitions. For the executed sequence B, C, D, row B learns the full\ndestination word PC and instruction of D from a ready bus fetch. While A\nexecutes, use the existing short lookahead index for its likely successor B\nto launch that row's synchronous read. While B executes, copy the complete\nreturned row into a replay register. While C executes, that register can\nsupply D on a denied fetch. A ready fetch always supplies the live word.\nThis adds a clock boundary between the RAM read and the ID address logic\nwhile retaining one issue per cycle on a warm stable path.\n\nPair the training key with consecutive admitted instructions. Invalidate\nbroken chains across bubbles, retries or annuls. Freeze the read and replay\npipeline together across EX holds. Carry row validity and read/write\ncollision handling through the extra register. On a denied fetch keep the\nexisting full-PC checks against fall-through and aligned redirect target.\nA mismatch cancels every EX side effect and retries the resolved PC. Flush\npending reads and training on reset or FENCE.I. The tradeoff is reduced\nreplay coverage after unpredictable branches. With a 1% cycle penalty the\nmedian must exceed 85.66 MHz to clear the 4.6% acceptance margin.\n\nhyp-20261120-003-r10s2\nUse the saved destination as the fetch PC for a valid branch or jump with a\nusable successor row, including when instruction memory is ready. Predict\nPC+4 for a cold row and for ordinary instructions. Only select an aligned\nin-range speculative destination. Fetch and decode that address while EX\nindependently resolves the architectural successor with forwarded operands\nand the existing trap rules. Register the comparison and the recovery\naddress at the transfer edge. A wrong prediction costs one retry on a ready\nbus. A correct prediction removes the EX operand-to-fetch dependency on\nboth the direction and the target without adding a stage to replay.\n\nTrain the predecessor with the instruction \n…[2877 of 5949 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:b45ed833f9f9ee1d]…\nr can execute on\nthat cycle. Only a dependent address that missed the EX LW base\nbypass needs the existing address-repair cycle. On a tag or lane miss,\nfreeze the younger slot and both forwarding sources. Give the older\nload the external bus until its refill is accepted, capture the word\nin its resident slot, then release it through the normal bypass.\nAuthorize younger bus strobes, redirects, divider launch and pipeline\ntransfer only after the older result is verified. A refused candidate\nmust never drive a speculative JALR fetch address onto imem.\n\nThe experiment succeeds if the saved denial cycles offset MEM\nverification and refill costs. At 3.10 million bracketed cycles,\nacceptance requires more than 85.53 MHz across the median placement.\nAt 86.5 MHz that cycle count would yield 279.0 iterations/s. Measure\ndenied-load probes, verified hits, refill holds and immediate address\nrepairs alongside the three-placement median. The main timing risk\nis the path from replay RAM through a dependent branch to fetch.\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
