{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_104", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261120-003-r15s2.yaml'", "aggregated_output": "id: hyp-20261120-003-r15s2\ntitle: \"Use next-PC prediction to put a register between fetch and decode\"\ncategory: predictor\nmotivation: |\n  Several placements trace a long path from instruction RAM or imemReady through\n  register selection and the ID address adder. Round 10 predicted the live fetch\n  PC using the predecessor's last successor. It paid 41,750 extra cycles while\n  leaving instruction selection and register read in the same cycle. The\n  champion takes 3,234,996 cycles at 85.328 MHz. A predictor is worth the\n  recovery bubbles if it buys a clock boundary on that second path as well.\nhypothesis: |\n  Run fetch ahead of decode using a 256-entry tagged branch target buffer\n  indexed by the registered fetch PC. Each row holds the last aligned target,\n  a conditional/unconditional bit and a two-bit direction counter. Predict\n  PC+4 on a miss. Update once per advancing, validated control transfer in EX.\n  Divert speculative fetches only to aligned PCs in ordinary instruction RAM.\n\n  Use a 512-word direct-mapped instruction replay RAM tagged by the instruction's\n  own address. Launch its synchronous read with the chosen next fetch index.\n  In the following cycle the row and the registered external request PC refer\n  to the same word. Capture the ready bus word or a fully tagged replay hit,\n  its PC and its predicted successor into an elastic IF/ID register. Decode,\n  register reads and ID address formation start from that register. Count a\n  fetch only when its word enters the register. If both sources miss, retry\n  the same PC. A full register holds the complete entry during backpressure\n  and can be consumed and replaced on the same edge.\n\n  Carry the predicted successor to EX and compare it to the full architectural\n  next PC, including linear continuation on a target-alignment trap. On a\n  mismatch, discard both younger slots, save the exact correction at the edge\n  and restart fetch from it. Qualify recovery by the branch actually advancing.\n  Keep forwarded branch operands off the same-cycle external fetc\n…[1053 of 4125 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:0c566fa5611dd4df]…\nrtl/core_pkg.sv\n    description: \"Carry the predicted next PC with the instruction through IF/ID and ID/EX.\"\n  - file: rtl/id_stage.sv\n    description: \"Decode the registered fetch word, latch its prediction on admission, and bubble on an older correction.\"\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: \"Compare the saved prediction with the exact next PC. Supply the correction, branch class, direction and target for training.\"\n  - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n    description: \"Distinguish consuming IF/ID from holding EX. Retain the buffered consumer on an M-result interlock while bubbling ID/EX.\"\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: \"Wire fetch acceptance, resolved transfers, recovery and serialization. Ensure killed slots cannot issue a bus request, launch division or retire.\"\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: \"Cover changing branch outcomes, two JALR targets at one PC, BTB aliases, denied correction fetches and FENCE.I with younger buffered words. Check exact RVFI PCs, order and accepted stores.\"\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
