{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_54","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261120-003-r1s2.yaml'","aggregated_output":"id: hyp-20261120-003-r1s2\ntitle: Predict direct branches and jumps at fetch with a bimodal direction table\ncategory: predictor\nmotivation: |\n  Fetch currently advances to PC+4 for every accepted instruction. With a\n  combinational IF/ID boundary, each taken branch or jump resolved in EX\n  discards one younger fetch opportunity. CoreMark repeatedly executes the\n  same loop branches and direct calls. Predicting their successor can reduce\n  the 4,491,485 marker-bracketed baseline cycles. A temporary prototype took\n  4,202,272 cycles under both bus stalls and matched all five expected CRCs.\n  Gowin reported 5.271, 5.359 and 5.359 MHz across placements. The median\n  gives 12.75 iterations/s, 5.2% above the champion. The small margin over\n  the 4.6% threshold makes placement sensitivity material.\nhypothesis: |\n  Use a 128-entry PC-indexed table of two-bit saturating counters for legal\n  conditional branches. Read the direction in parallel with fetch decode.\n  Predict JAL taken and JALR sequential. For a taken direct prediction use\n  the signed B or J immediate from the accepted instruction to compute the\n  target, permitting speculative fetch only at aligned addresses in the\n  instruction memory range. Carry one predicted-taken bit into EX. Since\n  both stages use the same PC and immediate for direct targets, EX can check\n  direction without a target tag or a 32-bit target comparison. On a mismatch\n  recover to the resolved next PC, including PC+4 after a predicted-taken\n  branch falls through. A correct prediction lets the younger fetch enter ID/EX.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 5\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/core_pkg.sv\n    description: Add a predicted-taken bit to the IF/ID and ID/EX bundles.\n  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Store the two counter bits as two 128-bit vectors indexed by PC[8:2].\n      Reset to weakly not-taken (01). Train by resolving PC once per advancing legal conditional\n      branch using the actual direction. Decode the six legal B conditions\n      and JAL from the raw instruction. Share a direct-target adder between\n      B and J. Advance the PC along the prediction only on ordinary fetch\n      acceptance. A correction from EX overrides an instruction-bus wait.\n  - file: rtl/id_stage.sv\n    description: Latch the chosen direction with its instruction under the existing ID/EX capture, bubble, and hold enables.\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Derive actual taken from the forwarded branch comparison or legal jump,\n      suppressing taken on a misalignment trap. When a valid EX instruction\n      advances, redirect only on a direction mismatch. Drive the correction\n      target and RVFI pc_next from the same resolved successor. Emit the branch\n      training enable, PC and outcome once, deferring both correction and\n      training while an older memory operation holds EX.\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: Wire EX training feedback into IF and pass the misprediction pulse through the existing redirect and flush connections.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
