{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_40", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261120-003-r2s2.yaml && git status --short'", "aggregated_output": "id: hyp-20261120-003-r2s2\ntitle: Predict direct branches and JAL in fetch on the six-step divider core\ncategory: predictor\nmotivation: |\n  The champion still fetches PC+4 after every branch. Its combinational IF/ID\n  boundary makes each correctly predicted taken transfer worth one fetch\n  opportunity. On the previous parent, the displacement-biased agree predictor\n  cut the ten-iteration CoreMark interval from 4,491,485 to 4,188,667 cycles.\n  The champion takes the same 4,491,485 cycles, and its reported critical path\n  runs from divisor_q to remainder_q inside the divider.\nhypothesis: |\n  Apply the measured 256-entry agree predictor to the champion. Predict a legal\n  backward branch taken and a forward branch sequential unless the site's\n  two-bit counter predicts disagreement with that bias. Predict direct JAL\n  taken. Use the fetched immediate for the target, so a table collision can\n  affect direction only. Resolve JALR in EX. On an accepted prediction, let\n  the successor enter ID/EX on the next ready cycle. Recover in EX on a\n  direction mismatch, using the architectural successor including trap handling.\n  A prototype on the champion reproduced 4,188,667 marker-to-marker cycles\n  with both stall generators active and all five canonical CRCs correct.\n  Gowin's three placement options gave 41.642, 42.217 and 42.217 MHz.\n  The table added 16 LUT-RAMs and the metadata added three flip-flops.\n  The median gives 100.79 iterations/s, 8.69% above the champion. At this\n  cycle count the 4.6% acceptance margin requires 40.63 MHz. The median\n  placement's worst path remains inside the divider. Placement option 0's\n  worst path passes through EX recovery and the younger instruction's hold\n  logic, making redirect fanout a timing risk in the final implementation.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 9\n  confidence: high\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Read a 256 x 2 distributed table asynchronously with PC[9:2]. Initialize\n      to weak agreement (01) at configuration. Use instr[31] XOR cou\n…[1132 of 4204 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:2cfe5707af032522]…\nor fall-through and misalignment traps.\n      Use the same actual successor for RVFI. Train once per advancing legal\n      conditional branch using its resolved condition and displacement sign.\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: |\n      Route EX correction through the existing redirect and flush inputs.\n      Send the resolving branch's PC index and saturated next count to IF,\n      incrementing its saved count on disagreement and decrementing on\n      agreement. Suppress correction and training during reset and while an\n      older memory operation holds EX. Preserve the divider interlock.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Exercise a trained loop through exit, opposite-bias branches sharing\n      an index, direct JAL and a misaligned taken branch. Check cycle-by-cycle\n      fetch on correct predictions, channel-0 retirement PCs and order, and\n      absence of wrong-path stores. Cover recovery with imemReady low and a\n      resolving branch held behind an older stalled data access.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
