{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"rg -n 'imem|oob|FENCE' test/cosim/main.cpp | head -120 && ls -al cores/bench/experiments && ls /opt\"","aggregated_output":"25:static uint8_t imem[MEM_SIZE] = {};\n27:static bool oob_access = false;  // sticky: once set, final marker reports it\n68:    // --istall: random ~22% imem-bus backpressure (VexRiscv-style).\n92:            memcpy(imem+vaddr, elf.data.data()+foff, filesz);\n100:    top->io_imemReady = 1;\n116:        // both imem and dmem when the corresponding flag is on. Drives 1\n119:        top->io_imemReady = istall ? bus_accepts_main() : 1;\n124:        //   - imem: PC should always be in range; flag every fetch outside.\n127:        //     silently alias to dmem[addr & 0xFFFFC] and not flag oob_access,\n129:        //     UART reads flag oob and return 0.\n135:        if (!in_mem_range(top->io_imemAddr)) oob_access = true;\n136:        top->io_imemData  = rw(imem, top->io_imemAddr);\n141:            if (top->io_dmemREn) oob_access = true;\n173:                oob_access = true;\n267:        printf(\"{\\\"ebreak\\\":%s,\\\"maxcycles_hit\\\":%s,\\\"oob\\\":%s,\"\n272:               oob_access ? \"true\" : \"false\",\n283:    if (oob_access)  return 3;\ntotal 184\ndrwxrwx---+ 3 nobody nogroup  4096 Nov 20 05:34 .\ndrwxrwx---+ 5 nobody nogroup  4096 Nov 20 05:34 ..\n-rw-rw----+ 1 nobody nogroup     0 Nov 20 05:34 .gitkeep\ndrwxrwx---+ 2 nobody nogroup  4096 Nov 20 05:34 hypotheses\n-rw-rw----+ 1 nobody nogroup 91599 Nov 20 05:34 log.jsonl\n-rw-rw----+ 1 nobody nogroup 65941 Nov 20 05:34 progress.png\ngowin\nhwe-python\nhwe-toolchain\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfor r in map(json.loads,open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl')):\n if r['round_id']==4:\n  print(r['id'],r['fitness'],r['cycles'],r['motivation'],r['hypothesis'].get('hypothesis',''))\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_9", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfor r in map(json.loads,open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl')):\n if r['round_id']==4:\n  print(r['id'],r['fitness'],r['cycles'],r['motivation'],r['hypothesis'].get('hypothesis',''))\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261120-001-r4s0 153.52 4171092 The champion spends 4,527,760 cycles on ten CoreMark iterations. On a\ntaken branch, JAL or JALR, IF presents the fall-through address while EX\nresolves the target, then discards that fetch. The combinational imem\ninterface can serve the resolved target during the same cycle. This also\nworks for indirect jumps because EX already has the forwarded JALR base.\n Let the resident EX instruction select the fetch address. Give its aligned\nredirect target first priority. Otherwise issue ID/EX.pc + 4 when ID/EX\nis valid. When ID/EX is empty, issue a retry address held in a 32-bit IF\nregister. Reset this register to zero and capture the actual bus address\non every other rising edge. Send the same address to ID as the fetched PC.\nA held EX instruction keeps selecting its successor. A bubble from an\nunready fetch or a late-result dependency retries the address on the next\ncycle. A ready target can enter ID/EX on the edge its branch leaves EX.\nThis uses the existing branch decision and requires no prediction state.\n\nA scratch RTL prototype completed ten iterations in 4,171,092 bracketed\ncycles with both stall generators enabled, saving 356,668 cycles (7.88%).\nAll five CRCs and the UART validation banner matched. The self-test and\nthree randomized programs matched the reference with and without stalls.\nThe directed pipeline tests and all 53 single-channel formal checks passed.\nGowin placement options 0, 1 and 2 reached 62.465, 64.034 and 64.034 MHz.\nThe median gives 153.52 iterations/s, 11.97% above the 137.11 champion.\n\nhyp-20261120-002-r4s1 153.03 4051673 The champion spends 4,527,760 cycles on ten CoreMark iterations. A taken\nbranch spends one fetch opportunity on fall-through even though EX knows\nthe target that cycle. A backend hold can also discard a ready instruction\nand force decode to wait for the bus a second time.\n Give IF one fall-through buffer holding an instruction and its PC. When\nempty, drive the instruction address from the registered fetch cursor or\nthe aligned redirect re\n…[1809 of 4881 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:f822f35978d74070]…\nchosen address alongside\nthe instruction. At the end of EX, register that recovery address and\nthe miss bit in parallel. After the edge, the bit selects the recovery\naddress on imemAddr and annuls the one younger instruction in ID/EX.\nThe two fetch addresses are ready before the forwarded branch comparison\nfinishes. A miss costs one fetch opportunity.\n\nOn a scratch copy of the champion the registered version completed ten\niterations in 4,223,613 marker-to-marker cycles with both bus stalls and\nall five canonical CRCs. Gowin placement options 0, 1 and 2 reached\n60.312, 62.834 and 62.834 MHz. That median gives 148.77 iterations/s,\n8.50% above 137.11. The median placement's worst path runs from the\nregister file through EX to data RAM. The median frequency margin over\nthe acceptance threshold is 2.26 MHz. The prototype passed all 53 fast\nsingle-retire formal checks, the five-ELF cosim suite and directed tests\nfor predictor collisions, misaligned targets and recovery under both\ninstruction- and data-bus stalls.\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
