{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_162", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261120-003-r6s2.yaml; python - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport os,json\np=Path(os.environ['TMPDIR'])/'probe/v7-time.json';r=json.loads(p.read_text())[0]; print({k:r[k] for k in ('logic','regs','lutram','bsram','dsp')})\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "id: hyp-20261120-003-r6s2\ntitle: Predict same-page data addresses to forward loads from EX/MEM\ncategory: predictor\nmotivation: |\n  The champion already replays instructions across taken backedges. Ten\n  iterations took 3,486,909 cycles with both stalls and 3,353,265 with\n  instruction stalls disabled, a 4.0% throughput difference at equal Fmax.\n  The acceptance margin is 4.6%. A prior trace counted 135,320 loads\n  immediately followed by a branch with a matching source field. An\n  address predictor can make those load values available a cycle earlier.\nhypothesis: |\n  Predict that adding a load or store's signed 12-bit displacement preserves\n  its base register's 4 KiB page. In decode, take bits [31:12] from the\n  selected base and compute only (base + displacement) modulo 8192.\n  Concatenate the page with the low 12 sum bits. Comparing sum bit 12 with\n  base bit 12 detects every crossing, including borrow and 32-bit wrap,\n  because one displacement can cross at most one page. Register the\n  candidate address, the crossing bit and whether the base source was\n  available. This puts the check beside the memory instruction before it\n  can assert a bus strobe.\n\n  An in-page hit issues in EX from a single address register. A crossing\n  or unavailable base costs one repair cycle. Suppress the request, hold\n  the instruction, and overwrite the same register with forwarded rs1\n  plus the full signed displacement. The repaired instruction can issue\n  on the next cycle and holds its request through backpressure. An accepted\n  load forwards from E