{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_86","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"cat > cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261120-003-r7s2.yaml <<'YAML'\nid: hyp-20261120-003-r7s2\ntitle: Predict the data word in decode and replay verified loads during bus stalls\ncategory: predictor\nmotivation: |\n  A ready instruction already issues across a taken branch, and the successor\n  predictor covers many denied instruction reads. The champion takes 3,234,996\n  cycles with both stall generators. With data memory always ready, the same\n  pipeline takes 3,043,747 cycles. A load's predicted effective address is\n  already available in ID, one edge before EX needs the data.\nhypothesis: |\n  Use the low word index of the ID address prediction to read a synchronous\n  on-chip table before EX has verified the address. Each row holds the most\n  recent RAM word observed at that index, its upper address tag, and the byte\n  lanes known to be current. In EX, a live ready response still supplies the\n  load. On a denied read, a matching tag and complete coverage of the requested\n  lanes let that load advance and forward the predicted data from EX/MEM.\n  Stores train the table when the data bus accepts them. An SB or SH replaces\n  the row's mask with just the lanes written. This prevents stale neighboring\n  bytes from satisfying a wider load.\n  \n  A 512-row prototype took 3,102,126 bracketed cycles, with all five CRCs and\n  the validation banner correct. That saves 132,870 cycles over ten iterations.\n  At that cycle count a median above 65.67 MHz would clear the tournament's\n  4.6 percent margin. Keeping address validation off the ID-to-RAM read path\n  is important to that frequency target.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 4\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/id_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Expose the predicted address's low word index for the synchronous lookup.\n      Associate the lookup with the same instruction that captures the predicted\n      address in ID/EX.\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Infer a simple dual-port block RAM for tag, byte mask and word. Train\n      only on accepted accesses to ordinary RAM below 1 MiB. Store the whole\n      bus word on a read and the replicated write data and accepted strobes on\n      a store. Clear validity on reset and reject read/write collisions.\n      Match the verified word address and all requested lanes before releasing\n      a denied aligned load. Keep the row aligned with EX across holds and\n      either re-read at a repaired address or suppress replay for that slot.\n      Use the selected raw word for both load extension and RVFI.\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: |\n      Connect the speculative read index and distinguish a verified local read\n      from a pending external request. Keep annulled, misaligned and MMIO loads\n      out of replay. An EX word load used as the next address base must supply\n      the selected word or take the existing unavailable-base repair.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Warm a replay row. Deny a dependent load while driving unrelated bus\n      data and check its retirement, forwarding and RVFI byte mask. Exercise\n      partial stores followed by narrow and wide reads, equal-index aliases,\n      reset, collisions and a repaired address under sustained backpressure.\nYAML\npython - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport json, yaml,jsonschema\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261120-003-r7s2.yaml')\njsonschema.validate(yaml.safe_load(p.read_text()), json.loads(Path('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json').read_text()))\nprint('Valid',202.37*1.046*3102126/1.e7)\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
