{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_53", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'date -u; tail -4 \"$TMPDIR/quotient_shared/tests.log\"; tail -4 \"$TMPDIR/quotient/pipeline.log\"; tail -3 \"$TMPDIR/quotient_shared/pnr/gowin_p1/gw.log\"; cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261120-003-r8s2.yaml'", "aggregated_output": "Fri Nov 20 07:25:09 UTC 2026\n..                                                                       [100%]\n2 passed in 22.43s\n.                                                                        [100%]\n1 passed in 10.46s\n[10%] Placement Phase 0 completed\n[20%] Placement Phase 1 completed\n[30%] Placement Phase 2 completed\nid: hyp-20261120-003-r8s2\ntitle: Predict small-divisor quotients with a reciprocal table and exact residual correction\ncategory: predictor\nmotivation: |\n  The champion takes 3,234,996 cycles for ten CoreMark iterations. Its\n  reported 74-cell timing path runs through two dependent divider digit\n  decisions, from quotient_q to remainder_q. CoreMark executes no DIV or\n  REM between its markers, so shortening that path can improve every timed\n  instruction at the existing cycle count. Clearing the 4.6% margin at\n  that count requires a median above 76.79 MHz.\nhypothesis: |\n  Predict the entire unsigned quotient for divisor magnitudes 2 through\n  255 using a 256-entry constant ROM of floor(2^32 / D). For dividend\n  magnitude A, the high word of A times the ROM entry gives q0. Since\n  A < 2^32, q0 is exact or one too small. The true tentative remainder\n  A - q0*D lies in [0, 2D), so its low nine bits suffice. Compare this\n  residual with D, increment q0 and subtract D exactly when it fits.\n  Pipeline the ROM read, four 16x16 partial products, their carry-preserving\n  reduction, and the small residual calculation across the existing wait\n  clocks. Handle D=0 and D=1 explicitly.\n\n  This predictor covers every magnitude that would require more than 24\n  quotient bits. For D >= 256, A[31:24] is already a legal remainder.\n  Seed with those eight bits and bring down A[23:0] in six radix-16\n  digits. Register multiples 1D through 15D during setup and use one\n  parallel trial-subtract bank per recurrence. Select the greatest fitting\n  multiple and its difference. This removes the serial radix-4 to radix-8\n  dependency from the clock path. The sixth digit lands on the existing\n  EX transfer edge. Both modes deliver the signed result through MEM with\n  the champion's issue, backpressure and forwarding timing.\n\n  A scratch implementation ran ten iterations in 3,234,996 bracketed\n  cycles with both stall generators and all five canonical CRCs. Gowin\n  placement options 0, 1 and 2 reached 76.460, 77.259 and 77.259 MHz.\n  The median gives 238.82 iterations/s, 5.25% above the champion. The\n  median placement's worst path now starts at the instruction replay RAM.\n  Directed tests passed for every divisor from 2 through 257 at exact\n  multiples and adjacent dividends. Selftest and all three random ELF\n  traces matched the reference with and without bus stalls.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 5\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/alu.sv\n    description: |\n      Replace the two serial digit stages with the verified reciprocal\n      quotient predictor and its six-digit large-divisor path. Use explicit\n      product widths and retain carries from the low partial products.\n      Compute (A[8:0] - q0[8:0]*D[7:0]) modulo 512 for the exact residual.\n      Use 36-bit unsigned large-divisor trials with a separate borrow bit.\n      Capture mode, sign and quotient/remainder selection on issue. Apply\n      RV32M sign and divide-by-zero rules including INT_MIN / -1. Advance\n      the last digit once on consume and keep the MEM result stable. Route\n      the existing ALTOPS result through the same handshake.\n  - file: test/test_alu.py\n    description: |\n      Exercise every predicted divisor at exact multiples and their\n      neighbors, including large dividends and cases needing correction.\n      Cover 255/256/257, the maximum large-path quotient, all four RV32M\n      operations, reset and delayed consume. Adapt the internal partial-\n      remainder expectation to the new digit schedule while retaining the\n      six-clock handshake and final-result stability checks.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
