{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_6", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\nrows=list(map(json.loads,Path('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl').read_text().splitlines()))\nr=rows[-3]\nfor k,v in r.items(): print(k,str(v)[:10000])\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "id hyp-20261120-001-r8s0\ntitle Share seven trial subtractors between radix-32 and radix-8 division\ncategory micro_opt\nmotivation The champion's 74-cell path goes from quotient_q[31] to remainder_q[1]\nthrough two dependent divider digit selections. Even the earlier split\nat 2^17 kept two radix-8 steps on the narrow feedback path. CoreMark\nexecutes zero DIV/REM between markers, so this path costs clock speed on\nevery measured instruction. A single digit decision on each feedback\nedge can improve Fmax within the existing retirement-latency bound.\n\nhypothesis {'motivation': \"The champion's 74-cell path goes from quotient_q[31] to remainder_q[1]\\nthrough two dependent divider digit selections. Even the earlier split\\nat 2^17 kept two radix-8 steps on the narrow feedback path. CoreMark\\nexecutes zero DIV/REM between markers, so this path costs clock speed on\\nevery measured instruction. A single digit decision on each feedback\\nedge can improve Fmax within the existing retirement-latency bound.\\n\", 'hypothesis': \"Use one 32-bit remainder R and one 32-bit dividend/quotient shift\\nregister Q. For a nonzero divisor magnitude D below 2^14, R fits in 14\\nbits. Resolve the top two dividend bits during setup, then consume 30\\nbits in six radix-32 digits. For D >= 2^14, the high 14 dividend bits\\nalready form a legal remainder. Seed R with them and Q with the low\\n18 dividend bits followed by 14 zeros. Six radix-8 digits then give\\nthe complete quotient.\\n\\nShare the trials for every fourth radix-32 candidate. Write T8 as\\n8*R + Q[31:29] and T32 as 4*T8 + Q[28:27]. For j=1..7, T32 >= 4*j*D\\nexactly when T8 >= j*D. Seven 35-bit unsigned subtractors supply these\\nfit bits to both modes. When digit 4*j wins, its narrow residual is\\nthe wide difference with Q[28:27] appended, truncated to 14 bits.\\nThe other 24 narrow candidates need only 19-bit trials. Adjacent fit\\nflags select the largest fitting digit and its residual in each mode.\\nBoth banks read the shared registers directly. The registered mode\\nselects the next Q and R on\n…[4095 of 7167 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:b6606d7946ac10b2]…\nnd keep the exact six-wait, delayed-consume,\\nreset and post-consume result checks.\\n'}]\noutcome improvement\nfitness 250.62\ndelta_pct 10.44\nvs_baseline 1967.82\nfmax_mhz 81.075\nipc_coremark 3e-06\ncycles 3234996\niterations 10\nlut4 4443\nff 939\nseeds [77.777, 81.075, 81.075]\nperturbations [[0, 77.777, 10], [1, 81.075, 11], [2, 81.075, 11]]\nlutram 32\nbsram 6\ndsp 5\ncritical_path {'from': 'cpu/u_if/successors_successors_0_0_s', 'to': 'dmem_2_dmem_2_0_0_s', 'slack_ns': -7.857, 'arrival_ns': 18.152, 'logic_cells': 12, 'modules': ['clock_ibuf', 'cpu/u_if', 'cpu/u_hazard', 'cpu/u_id']}\nlogic_levels 10\nformal_passed True\ncosim_passed True\nerror None\nimplementation_notes \ntimestamp 2026-11-20T08:17:14.601115Z\nslot 0\nround_id 8\nlesson - 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-001-r8s0 (improvement, +10.44%): Splitting at divisor magnitude 2^14 raised Fmax by reducing divider feedback to one digit selection per clock, with the seven radix-8 trial subtractors supplying fit bits and residual prefixes for every fourth nonzero radix-32 digit.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
